DB.addExt(`
caffeine-citrate|Your baby is getting caffeine to help with pauses in breathing that are common in premature babies. It is given once a day by vein or by mouth and can make your baby a little fussy or have a faster heartbeat. If your baby goes home on caffeine, give the exact dose with the oral syringe provided and never double a missed dose. Tell the team about spit-ups, a swollen belly, or more breathing pauses.|Confirm the order is written as caffeine citrate (2 mg citrate equals 1 mg base) and double check the dose; give the 20 mg/kg load over 30 min and maintenance over 10 min by syringe pump; assess heart rate before dosing and hold and notify for sustained tachycardia (often above 180 per protocol); monitor apnea, bradycardia, and desaturation events, feeding tolerance, abdominal distension, and glucose; report a new rise in events because it may signal sepsis, NEC, or anemia.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Caffeine citrate;;NCBI StatPearls: Caffeine
poractant-alfa|Your baby's lungs are too young to make enough surfactant, a slippery coating that keeps the air sacs open. The team will put this medicine into your baby's breathing tube, and breathing often gets easier within minutes. Your baby's oxygen level and heart rate may dip briefly during the dose, and the team will watch closely.|Confirm ETT position and dose by birth weight (2.5 mL/kg initial, 1.25 mL/kg repeats); warm vial to room temperature and gently invert without shaking; suction before dosing and avoid suctioning afterward per protocol; pause for bradycardia, desaturation, or ETT reflux; wean FiO2 and ventilator pressures promptly as compliance improves to prevent hyperoxia and pneumothorax.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Poractant alfa;;American Academy of Pediatrics. Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP), 8th edition;;NCBI StatPearls: Surfactant
alprostadil|Your baby has a heart problem that depends on a small blood vessel called the ductus staying open until surgery. This medicine runs continuously through an IV to keep it open. It can cause pauses in breathing, fever, or flushing, so your baby may need a breathing tube and will be watched closely. Never touch or pause the IV pump.|High-alert continuous infusion: standard concentration, smart pump, and independent double check; never bolus or interrupt the line; keep bag-mask, suction, and intubation equipment at the bedside because apnea (boxed warning) is most common in the first hour and in infants under 2 kg; monitor respirations, pre- and postductal SpO2, BP, temperature, and perfusion; report fever, apnea, hypotension, or worsening cyanosis or perfusion.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Alprostadil;;American Academy of Pediatrics. Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP), 8th edition;;NCBI StatPearls: Alprostadil
indomethacin|Your baby is getting this medicine through an IV to help close the ductus, a blood vessel that should close after birth. It is usually given as 3 doses, and the team will check urine, blood tests, and the belly between doses. Adults taking indomethacin pills should take them with food, avoid alcohol, and report black stools, stomach pain, or swelling.|Before each neonatal dose check urine output, creatinine, platelets, and signs of bleeding; hold and notify if urine output is under 0.6 mL/kg/h, platelets are low, or NEC or bleeding is suspected; reconstitute with preservative-free diluent and infuse over 20-30 min; monitor electrolytes, abdomen, and stools, and watch aminoglycoside and digoxin levels; avoid concurrent corticosteroids because of intestinal perforation risk.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Indomethacin;;NCBI StatPearls: Indomethacin;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
meclizine|This medicine helps with spinning dizziness (vertigo) and motion sickness. For travel, take it about 1 hour before you leave. It can make you sleepy and dry your mouth, so do not drive or drink alcohol until you know how it affects you. Get help right away for sudden dizziness with weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, a severe headache, or trouble walking, because these can be signs of a stroke.|Assess the dizziness pattern and screen for central causes (new neuro deficits, ataxia, headache) before treating as peripheral vertigo; give 25-100 mg/day in divided doses for vertigo per the Rx label (OTC motion sickness 25-50 mg once daily 1 h before travel); implement fall precautions and monitor sedation, confusion, urinary retention, and blurred vision, especially in older adults (Beers list); avoid stacking with other anticholinergics or CNS depressants; use caution with asthma, glaucoma, BPH, and hepatic or renal impairment.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Meclizine hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Meclizine;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
dimenhydrinate|This medicine prevents motion sickness and nausea. Take the first dose 30 to 60 minutes before travel. It causes drowsiness, so do not drive, and avoid alcohol and other sleep or allergy medicines, especially ones that contain diphenhydramine (Benadryl). Keep it away from children and teens, because large amounts are dangerous.|Check for other diphenhydramine-containing products to prevent double dosing; dilute each 50 mg in 10 mL NS and give IV over 2 min, or give 50 mg IM; monitor sedation, BP, heart rate, confusion, and urinary retention; use fall precautions in older adults; watch children for paradoxical excitation; in overdose expect an anticholinergic toxidrome with possible seizures and wide QRS.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dimenhydrinate injection;;FDA OTC Drug Facts label (DailyMed): Dimenhydrinate tablets;;NCBI StatPearls: Dimenhydrinate
chlorpheniramine|This allergy medicine relieves sneezing, runny nose, and itchy eyes. It can make you sleepy, so avoid alcohol and driving until you know how it affects you. It is found in many cold and cough products, so read every label to avoid taking it twice, and do not give it to children under 6 years.|Review all OTC combination products to avoid duplicate antihistamines, acetaminophen, or dextromethorphan; assess sedation, dry mouth, urinary retention, and confusion in older adults (Beers list); use caution with glaucoma, BPH, and asthma; in adolescent overdoses consider Coricidin (dextromethorphan plus chlorpheniramine) and monitor for anticholinergic and serotonergic toxicity.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Chlorpheniramine maleate tablets (OTC Drug Facts);;NCBI StatPearls: Chlorpheniramine
brompheniramine|This allergy medicine is mostly found in children's cold and allergy liquids. Measure each dose only with the cup that comes with the product, not a kitchen spoon. Do not give it to children under 6 years, and do not use two cold medicines at the same time. It can cause sleepiness or, in some children, excitement.|Verify the exact product and all active ingredients (decongestants, dextromethorphan) before dosing; confirm age is 6 yr or older and use the provided dosing cup; monitor sedation, paradoxical excitation, heart rate, and BP if a decongestant is present; teach families about accidental ingestion and poison control; use caution in glaucoma, BPH, and asthma.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Brompheniramine maleate oral liquid (OTC Drug Facts);;NCBI StatPearls: Antihistamines
cyproheptadine|This medicine treats allergies and itching and may also increase your appetite. It can make you sleepy and dry your mouth, so avoid alcohol and driving until you know how it affects you. If it is being used for serotonin syndrome, tell the nurse right away about new shaking, muscle stiffness, fever, or confusion.|For serotonin syndrome, give orally or crushed via NG tube along with cooling, benzodiazepines, and stopping serotonergic drugs; monitor temperature, clonus, rigidity, HR, BP, and mental status; observe for sedation, urinary retention, and anticholinergic effects; do not give with MAOIs, to newborns or nursing mothers, or in angle-closure glaucoma or symptomatic BPH; use caution in older adults.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Cyproheptadine hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Serotonin Syndrome;;NCBI StatPearls: Cyproheptadine
doxylamine|This medicine helps you fall asleep. Take one tablet 30 minutes before bed only when you can sleep a full night, and do not take it with nighttime cold medicines that also contain doxylamine. Do not drink alcohol with it, and do not give it to children under 12. If you are pregnant and taking it with vitamin B6 for nausea, swallow the tablets whole.|Confirm no duplicate doxylamine in nighttime combination products; implement fall precautions and monitor next-day sedation and confusion in older adults (Beers list); use caution with asthma, COPD, glaucoma, and BPH; for the delayed-release pyridoxine combination in pregnancy, give whole on an empty stomach and titrate to symptom control; in overdose monitor CK, urine color, renal function, QRS, and seizures because rhabdomyolysis is characteristic.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Doxylamine succinate (Unisom SleepTabs Drug Facts);;FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Diclegis (doxylamine and pyridoxine);;NCBI StatPearls: Doxylamine
levocetirizine|Take this allergy pill once a day in the evening. It causes less sleepiness than older allergy medicines, but some people still feel tired, so avoid alcohol and see how it affects you before driving. Tell your provider if you have kidney disease, because the dose may need to be lower. Report trouble urinating.|Check renal function and adjust: CrCl 50-80 mL/min 2.5 mg daily, 30-50 mL/min every other day, 10-30 mL/min twice weekly, contraindicated under 10 or on dialysis; do not exceed 2.5 mg in children 6-11 yr; monitor somnolence, fatigue, and urinary retention; remind that it is not a treatment for anaphylaxis.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Levocetirizine dihydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Levocetirizine
desloratadine|Take this allergy pill once a day. It usually does not cause sleepiness. If you have liver or kidney disease, your provider may have you take it every other day. Products with a D in the name also contain a decongestant that can raise your blood pressure and heart rate.|Assess allergy symptoms and hepatic and renal function (start 5 mg every other day if impaired); verify pediatric dosing by age with the oral solution; if Clarinex-D is used, check BP and avoid with MAOIs or uncontrolled hypertension; remind that it does not treat anaphylaxis.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Clarinex (desloratadine);;NCBI StatPearls: Desloratadine
clemastine|This allergy medicine can make you sleepy and dry your mouth. Do not drive or drink alcohol until you know how it affects you. Do not use it while breastfeeding. Older adults may become confused or have trouble urinating, so report these problems.|Clarify salt versus base: 1.34 mg clemastine fumarate equals 1 mg clemastine; do not exceed the labeled daily maximum; monitor sedation, fall risk, urinary retention, and confusion in older adults (Beers list); contraindicated in nursing mothers, with MAOIs, and for lower respiratory symptoms including asthma.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Clemastine fumarate tablets;;FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Clemastine fumarate syrup;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
carbinoxamine|This prescription allergy medicine can make you or your child sleepy. Never give it to a child younger than 2 years. Measure liquid doses with a proper milliliter syringe or cup from the pharmacy, not a kitchen spoon, and shake the extended-release liquid well before each dose.|Confirm the patient is 2 yr or older and verify the formulation (IR 3-4 times daily vs ER every 12 h) and mg/mL concentration before dosing; use an oral syringe; monitor sedation, respiratory status in young children, and paradoxical excitation; contraindicated in nursing mothers and with MAOIs; use caution in older adults.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Karbinal ER (carbinoxamine maleate);;FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Carbinoxamine maleate tablets;;NCBI StatPearls: Antihistamines
`);

