DB.addExt(`
azelastine|Use the nose spray twice a day as directed, tilting your head forward and aiming away from the middle of your nose; prime it before the first use. It works within minutes but can leave a bitter taste and make you sleepy, so avoid alcohol and do not drive until you know how it affects you. For the eye drops, take out contact lenses first and wait 10 minutes before putting them back in.|Confirm formulation (nasal spray vs 0.05% eye drops) and teach priming (4 sprays first use, 2 sprays if unused 3 or more days); assess for sedation, especially with opioids, benzodiazepines, or alcohol; teach not to spray into the eyes; report epistaxis or persistent nasal sores; do not confuse with nasal steroids, which take days to work.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Azelastine hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Azelastine
olopatadine|Put 1 drop in each itchy eye as the label says: once a day for the 0.2% or 0.7% strength, or twice a day for the 0.1% strength. Take out contact lenses first and wait at least 10 minutes before putting them back in, and do not let the dropper touch your eye. Stop and call your provider for eye pain, vision changes, or redness that does not go away. The nose spray can cause a bitter taste, nosebleeds, or drowsiness.|Verify strength and frequency (0.1% BID, 0.2% or 0.7% daily, nasal 0.6% 2 sprays per nostril BID); assess eyes for pain, discharge, or vision change that suggests infection rather than allergy; teach contact lens removal and a 5 min gap between eye products; with the nasal spray assess for epistaxis and sedation with CNS depressants.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Olopatadine hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Olopatadine
ketotifen|Put 1 drop in the itchy eye twice a day, 8 to 12 hours apart, and no more than twice a day. Take out contact lenses before using and wait at least 10 minutes before putting them back. Do not use it for eye irritation from contact lenses, and stop and call your provider if you have eye pain, vision changes, or redness or itching lasting more than 3 days.|Confirm the complaint is allergic itching, not infection, injury, or contact lens problems; teach dropper hygiene, contact lens removal, and a 5 min gap between eye products; limit to twice daily; refer for eye pain, photophobia, vision change, or symptoms lasting over 72 h; keep out of reach of children.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ketotifen fumarate;;NCBI StatPearls: Ketotifen
cromolyn|This medicine prevents allergy symptoms but does not stop an attack that has already started. Use the nose spray every day, starting up to a week before allergy season, and give it 1 to 2 weeks to reach full effect. For the oral form, empty the ampule into a glass of water, stir, and drink it 30 minutes before meals and at bedtime. Do not use it to treat asthma attacks; use your rescue inhaler and call for help if you are wheezing or short of breath.|Teach that cromolyn is preventive only and must be used regularly before allergen exposure; confirm the patient has a separate rescue inhaler if asthmatic; oral concentrate is diluted in water and never inhaled or injected; stop and notify for wheezing, chest tightness, hives, or throat swelling; reassess benefit after 2 weeks (nasal) or up to 6 weeks (eye).|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Cromolyn sodium;;NCBI StatPearls: Cromolyn Sodium
mometasone|Shake the spray and use it once a day while sniffing gently, aiming toward the outside of the nostril. It takes a few days to work fully, so use it every day rather than only when you feel stuffy. Call your provider for frequent nosebleeds, sores in your nose, white patches in your nose or throat, or vision changes, and avoid people with chickenpox or measles if you have not had them.|Teach technique (shake, prime, aim away from the septum) and once-daily regular use; assess for epistaxis, septal irritation, and nasal candidiasis; monitor growth in children ; lowest effective dose advised per label; ask about ritonavir or other strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (systemic steroid effects); refer for vision changes (glaucoma, cataracts).|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Mometasone furoate nasal spray;;NCBI StatPearls: Mometasone
triamcinolone|Use 2 sprays in each nostril once a day while sniffing gently, then cut back to 1 spray in each nostril once your symptoms improve. Some relief may start the first day, but it can take up to a week to work fully. Stop and call your provider for severe or frequent nosebleeds, vision changes, signs of infection, or if you are exposed to chickenpox, measles, or tuberculosis.|Teach priming (re-prime if unused over 2 weeks), shaking, and gentle sniffing; reinforce step-down to 1 spray per nostril once improved and no doubling of missed doses; assess for epistaxis and septal ulceration; monitor growth in children and supervise use under 12 yr; do not confuse the OTC nasal spray with triamcinolone injection (Kenalog).|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Triamcinolone acetonide nasal spray;;NCBI StatPearls: Triamcinolone
pseudoephedrine|Take only as directed and never more than the daily limit; check other cold and allergy medicines for a second decongestant (look for a D on the label). It can raise your blood pressure and heart rate and keep you awake, so take the last dose well before bedtime. Do not take it with an MAO inhibitor, and ask your provider first if you have high blood pressure, heart disease, thyroid disease, diabetes, or trouble urinating.|Assess BP and HR before use and hold and notify for hypertension, tachycardia, or palpitations; screen for MAOI use within 14 days, linezolid, and stimulants; ask about BPH and urinary retention; review all OTC combination products for duplicate decongestants; ER tablets are swallowed whole.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pseudoephedrine hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Pseudoephedrine
oxymetazoline|Use 2 or 3 sprays in each nostril no more often than every 10 to 12 hours and no more than twice a day. Do not use it for more than 3 days, because your nose can become more stuffy when you stop (rebound congestion). Keep the bottle away from children, because swallowing even a small amount can make a young child very sleepy with a slow heartbeat; call Poison Control right away if this happens.|Assess duration of use and ask about rebound congestion; check BP and HR, especially with heart disease or hypertension; limit to 2 doses per 24 h and 3 days; for epistaxis or before nasal airway or intubation, use per protocol and hold firm pressure; treat any pediatric ingestion as potentially serious (bradycardia, hypotension, CNS and respiratory depression).|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oxymetazoline hydrochloride nasal spray;;NCBI StatPearls: Oxymetazoline
zafirlukast|Take 1 tablet twice a day on an empty stomach, at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after a meal. It helps prevent asthma attacks but will not stop one that has already started, so keep your rescue inhaler with you. Call your provider right away for yellow skin or eyes, dark urine, pain in the right upper belly, or mood or behavior changes, and tell them if you take warfarin.|Administer 1 h before or 2 h after meals; assess for hepatic disease before starting and teach signs of hepatotoxicity, stopping the drug and notifying for jaundice or RUQ pain; monitor INR closely with warfarin; ensure a rescue inhaler is available; monitor for neuropsychiatric changes and for eosinophilia, rash, or neuropathy when oral steroids are tapered.|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Zafirlukast;;Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) Strategy Report (2024);;NCBI StatPearls: Zafirlukast
`);

