DB.addExt(`
glecaprevir-pibrentasvir|This medicine is three tablets taken together once a day with food, usually for 8 weeks, to cure hepatitis C. Missing doses can lower the chance of cure. Yellow skin or eyes, dark urine, belly swelling or confusion are reported to the care team right away, and all other medicines and supplements are shared with the pharmacist.|HBsAg and anti-HBc are reviewed before therapy because of the boxed warning for HBV reactivation; hepatic status is reviewed, since the drug is contraindicated in Child-Pugh B or C; interacting medicines (rifampin, atazanavir, statins, ethinyl estradiol products, carbamazepine) are screened per label; blood glucose in diabetes and INR on warfarin can change as HCV clears; HCV RNA is typically checked 12 weeks after completion|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Glecaprevir and pibrentasvir;;AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance: Recommendations for Testing, Managing, and Treating Hepatitis C;;NCBI StatPearls: Glecaprevir/Pibrentasvir
sofosbuvir-velpatasvir|This medicine is one tablet taken once a day, with or without food, usually for 12 weeks, to cure hepatitis C. People taking amiodarone for heart rhythm let the care team know, since a dangerously slow heartbeat can occur. Dizziness, fainting, chest pain, yellow skin or dark urine are reported right away, and antacids or acid reducers are timed as the pharmacist explains.|Amiodarone use is reviewed before starting because of serious symptomatic bradycardia per label; HBsAg and anti-HBc are reviewed because of the boxed warning for HBV reactivation; acid-reducing agents and inducers (rifampin, carbamazepine, St. John's wort) lower levels per label; glucose and INR can change as HCV clears; ribavirin, when added for decompensated cirrhosis, brings anemia and pregnancy precautions|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Sofosbuvir and velpatasvir;;AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance: Recommendations for Testing, Managing, and Treating Hepatitis C;;NCBI StatPearls: Sofosbuvir/Velpatasvir
entecavir|This pill is taken once a day on an empty stomach, at least 2 hours after a meal and 2 hours before the next one, to control hepatitis B. Stopping it on your own can cause a serious liver flare, so refills are planned ahead. Yellow skin, dark urine, belly pain, unusual tiredness or fast breathing are reported to the care team.|HIV testing is typically completed before starting because entecavir alone can select HIV resistance (boxed warning); renal function is reviewed, since the dose is reduced for CrCl below 50 mL/min; the drug is given on an empty stomach per label; hepatic function is followed closely for several months after discontinuation because of severe HBV exacerbations; symptoms of lactic acidosis are reported to the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Entecavir;;AASLD Guidance: Prevention, Diagnosis, and Treatment of Chronic Hepatitis B (2018);;NCBI StatPearls: Entecavir
palivizumab|Your child receives this antibody shot in the thigh muscle once a month during RSV season to help prevent serious RSV illness. It does not treat RSV once a child is sick. Keeping every monthly appointment matters for protection, and hives, swelling or trouble breathing after a shot are reported right away.|Prior hypersensitivity is reviewed before each dose; dose is 15 mg/kg IM monthly and is recalculated with current weight per label; injection volumes over 1 mL are typically split; the child is observed for hypersensitivity after injection; an extra dose is given after cardiopulmonary bypass per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Palivizumab;;American Academy of Pediatrics. Red Book: Report of the Committee on Infectious Diseases;;NCBI StatPearls: Palivizumab
nirsevimab|Your baby gets this one-time antibody shot in the thigh, usually around birth or before RSV season, and it protects for about 5 months. It is not a vaccine and does not treat RSV once a baby is sick. Hives, swelling or trouble breathing after the shot are reported right away.|Maternal RSV vaccination status and prior hypersensitivity are reviewed before dosing; weight determines the dose (50 mg under 5 kg, 100 mg at 5 kg or more) per label; the infant is observed after injection for hypersensitivity; it can be given at the same visit as routine childhood vaccines|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Nirsevimab;;CDC/ACIP Recommendations for Nirsevimab for Prevention of RSV Disease Among Infants and Young Children (2023);;NCBI StatPearls: Nirsevimab
rabies-immune-globulin|This medicine is injected into and around the bite or scratch once, on the first day of rabies shots, to give protection right away. Rabies vaccine shots continue on the scheduled days, and every follow-up dose matters, since rabies is fatal in nearly all cases once symptoms start. Soreness at the site is common.|Prior rabies vaccination is reviewed, since previously vaccinated persons receive vaccine only per label; dose is 20 IU/kg once, infiltrated around the wound as much as feasible with any remainder IM distant from the vaccine site; RIG and vaccine are in separate syringes and sites, and it is not given IV; RIG is not given after day 7 of the vaccine series per label; hypersensitivity is monitored|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Rabies immune globulin (human);;CDC/ACIP: Use of a Reduced (4-Dose) Vaccine Schedule for Postexposure Prophylaxis to Prevent Human Rabies (2010);;NCBI StatPearls: Rabies
tetanus-immune-globulin|This shot gives ready-made protection against tetanus after a wound when your tetanus shots are not up to date. A tetanus vaccine is usually given at the same visit in the other arm. Soreness at the site is common, and jaw stiffness or muscle spasms in the weeks after a wound are reported right away.|Tetanus vaccination history and wound type are reviewed, since TIG is indicated for tetanus-prone wounds with fewer than 3 prior toxoid doses or unknown history per CDC guidance; dose is 250 units deep IM for adults; TIG and toxoid are given at separate sites with separate syringes; it is not given IV; hypersensitivity is monitored|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tetanus immune globulin (human);;CDC Pink Book: Tetanus;;NCBI StatPearls: Tetanus
hepatitis-b-immune-globulin|This shot gives ready-made protection against hepatitis B after a needlestick, blood or sexual exposure, or for a newborn whose mother has hepatitis B. The hepatitis B vaccine series is usually started at the same visit in a different spot. Follow-up blood tests show whether protection developed.|Source HBsAg status and the exposed person's vaccine and anti-HBs response history are reviewed; adult postexposure dose is 0.06 mL/kg IM per label, and newborns of HBsAg-positive mothers receive 0.5 mL IM ideally within 12 hours of birth with vaccine; HBIG and vaccine are given at separate sites; hypersensitivity is monitored|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Hepatitis B immune globulin (human);;CDC Guidelines for Management of Health Care Personnel Exposures to Hepatitis B (2018);;NCBI StatPearls: Hepatitis B Immune Globulin
varicella-zoster-immune-globulin|This shot gives ready-made antibodies after being near someone with chickenpox or shingles, for people whose bodies cannot fight it well. It works best given as soon as possible. A chickenpox rash or fever in the next 4 weeks is reported to the care team, since the illness can still appear later than usual.|Immune status, exposure date and weight are reviewed; dose follows label weight bands up to 625 international units IM, ideally within 96 hours of exposure; the patient is observed for varicella for 28 days because incubation can be prolonged per label; live vaccines are typically deferred after administration; hypersensitivity is monitored|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Varicella zoster immune globulin (human);;CDC: Updated Recommendations for Use of VariZIG (2013);;NCBI StatPearls: Varicella Zoster Immune Globulin
botulism-antitoxin-heptavalent|This medicine is given through an IV in the hospital to stop botulism toxin from causing more weakness. It comes from horse antibodies, so allergies to horses or horse products are shared with the team. Itching, trouble breathing or fever during the infusion, and rash or joint pain 1 to 3 weeks later, are reported.|Equine allergy history is reviewed and anaphylaxis medications and airway equipment are prepared before infusion; one vial is diluted 1:10 in 0.9% sodium chloride and started at 0.5 mL/min for 30 minutes per label; continuous cardiac monitoring is typical because bradycardia is reported; vital capacity and NIF trends are followed for respiratory failure; delayed serum sickness is assessed for 1 to 3 weeks|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Botulism antitoxin heptavalent (equine);;CDC Clinical Guidelines for Diagnosis and Treatment of Botulism (2021);;NCBI StatPearls: Botulism
`);
