DB.addExt(`
baricitinib|This pill is taken once a day, with or without food, to calm an overactive immune system. It raises the chance of infections such as shingles and tuberculosis, so fever, cough or a painful blistering rash are reported promptly. Sudden leg swelling, chest pain or shortness of breath can mean a blood clot or heart problem and is an emergency.|Tuberculosis screening, CBC with lymphocyte count, liver enzymes and lipids are typically reviewed at baseline and during therapy per label; ANC below 1,000, lymphocyte count below 500 or hemoglobin below 8 g/dL are labeled reasons to interrupt dosing; the dose is lower when eGFR is 30 to below 60, and use is not recommended below 30 per label; signs of DVT, PE, MI or serious infection commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; strong OAT3 inhibitors such as probenecid and live vaccines are known interaction concerns|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Baricitinib;;NCBI StatPearls: Janus Kinase Inhibitors;;American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Treatment of Rheumatoid Arthritis (2021)
secukinumab|This medicine is a shot under the skin, given weekly at first and then once a month. It can raise the risk of infections, including thrush in the mouth, so fever, cough or white patches are reported. New belly pain, ongoing diarrhea or blood in the stool can signal bowel inflammation and is reported to the prescriber.|Tuberculosis screening is typically completed before the first dose; active infection commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; new or worsening Crohn disease or ulcerative colitis symptoms are reported per label; prefilled devices are allowed to reach room temperature and are injected away from psoriatic plaques or bruised skin; live vaccines are avoided during treatment|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Secukinumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Secukinumab;;American College of Rheumatology/National Psoriasis Foundation Guideline for the Treatment of Psoriatic Arthritis (2018)
apremilast|This pill is started slowly over 5 days with a starter pack and then taken twice a day, with or without food. Diarrhea, nausea and headache are common in the first weeks; severe vomiting or diarrhea, unplanned weight loss, or low mood and thoughts of self-harm are reported right away.|Body weight and mood are typically reviewed during therapy per label; severe diarrhea, nausea or vomiting with volume depletion, especially in older adults, commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; the maintenance dose is 30 mg once daily when CrCl is below 30 per label; strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin, carbamazepine and phenytoin lower exposure and are not recommended per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Apremilast;;NCBI StatPearls: Apremilast
deucravacitinib|This psoriasis pill is taken once a day, with or without food, and is swallowed whole. It can raise the chance of infections such as cold sores and shingles. Muscle pain or weakness, dark urine, fever or a rash are reported to the prescriber.|Tuberculosis screening is typically completed before starting per label; active serious infection commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; CPK is reviewed when muscle symptoms appear because of rhabdomyolysis reports; lipids and liver enzymes are followed per label; use is not recommended in severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh C) or with other potent immunosuppressants per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Deucravacitinib;;NCBI StatPearls: Deucravacitinib
belimumab|This lupus medicine is given as an IV infusion over about 1 hour or as a weekly shot under the skin. Allergic reactions can happen during the infusion or hours to days later, so swelling, rash, wheezing or trouble breathing are reported right away. Fever, new infections and low mood or thoughts of self-harm are also reported.|Vital signs are typically reviewed before and during the IV infusion, which runs over 1 hour and is not given as a push or bolus per label; the lyophilized product is diluted in normal saline, half-normal saline or Lactated Ringer's, and dextrose solutions are incompatible; hypersensitivity can be delayed, so patients are observed after infusion; serious infection, new neurologic deficits concerning for PML, or depression and suicidality commonly prompt notifying the provider; live vaccines are not given within 30 days before or during treatment|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Belimumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Belimumab;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Lupus Nephritis (2024)
avacopan|These capsules are taken twice a day with food, swallowed whole, alongside the other vasculitis medicines. Grapefruit and grapefruit juice are avoided. Yellow skin or eyes, dark urine, belly pain, swelling of the face or lips, or fever are reported promptly.|Liver tests (ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin) are typically reviewed before starting, every 4 weeks for the first 6 months and as clinically indicated per label; transaminase elevation with raised bilirubin commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; hepatitis B screening is done before starting; the dose is 30 mg once daily with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors per label; strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin are avoided|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Avacopan;;NCBI StatPearls: Avacopan;;American College of Rheumatology/Vasculitis Foundation Guideline for the Management of ANCA-Associated Vasculitis (2021)
pegloticase|This gout medicine is given as an IV infusion every two weeks in a clinic, together with weekly methotrexate pills. Medicines are given before each infusion to lower the chance of an allergic reaction, and staff watch closely afterward. Rash, swelling, chest discomfort, trouble breathing, dark urine or blue lips are reported right away, and other uric acid pills such as allopurinol are not taken during treatment.|G6PD deficiency is screened before starting in higher-risk patients, and G6PD deficiency is a contraindication per label; serum uric acid is reviewed before each infusion, and two consecutive levels above 6 mg/dL are a labeled reason to consider discontinuation; premedication with antihistamines and corticosteroids is given per label, and the infusion runs over 120 minutes, not as a push or bolus; patients are observed after infusion in a setting prepared for anaphylaxis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pegloticase;;NCBI StatPearls: Pegloticase;;American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Management of Gout (2020)
probenecid-colchicine|This gout tablet is taken once a day for the first week and then twice a day to lower uric acid and help prevent flares. Plenty of fluids help lower the chance of kidney stones. Severe diarrhea, vomiting, muscle weakness, numbness or flank pain are reported, and new antibiotics or antifungals are reviewed with a pharmacist because some raise colchicine levels.|Kidney function, uric acid and CBC are typically reviewed during therapy; reduced effectiveness at GFR 30 mL/min or less is noted per label; the combination is not started during an acute gout attack per label; severe GI symptoms, muscle weakness or numbness commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; strong CYP3A4 or P-gp inhibitors such as clarithromycin, salicylates and penicillin or methotrexate levels are known interaction concerns|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Probenecid and Colchicine;;NCBI StatPearls: Probenecid;;American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Management of Gout (2020)
mycophenolic-acid|This transplant medicine is taken twice a day on an empty stomach and swallowed whole. It lowers the body's defense against infection, so fever, chills, sore throat or unusual bruising are reported. It can cause birth defects and miscarriage, so pregnancy prevention and pregnancy testing are part of treatment, and it is not swapped with mycophenolate mofetil without prescriber direction.|CBC is typically reviewed weekly in the first month, twice monthly in months 2 and 3, then monthly through the first year per label; ANC below 1,300 commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; pregnancy testing and contraception counseling follow the REMS program; delayed-release tablets are not crushed, chewed or cut; Myfortic and mycophenolate mofetil are not interchangeable milligram for milligram, a known medication error|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Mycophenolic Acid;;NCBI StatPearls: Mycophenolate;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for the Care of Kidney Transplant Recipients (2009)
sirolimus|This transplant pill or liquid is taken once a day, the same way each day with or without food, and 4 hours after cyclosporine. Grapefruit juice is avoided. Fever, cough, mouth sores, slow-healing wounds, or swelling of the face or lips are reported to the transplant team.|Sirolimus whole blood trough concentrations are typically reviewed after dose changes and with interacting drugs per label; lipids, CBC, creatinine and urine protein are followed; new cough or dyspnea concerning for pneumonitis, angioedema, or poor wound healing commonly prompt notifying the provider; the oral solution is diluted only in water or orange juice; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers and ACE inhibitors are known interaction concerns|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Sirolimus;;NCBI StatPearls: Sirolimus;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for the Care of Kidney Transplant Recipients (2009)
`);
