DB.addExt(`
normal-saline|This is a sterile salt water fluid given through an IV to replace lost fluid or to carry other medicines. Some people feel a cool sensation in the arm. Shortness of breath, swelling of the legs or face, or pain and swelling at the IV site are reported to staff right away, especially in people with heart or kidney problems.|Lung sounds, blood pressure, heart rate and history of heart failure or dialysis are reviewed before fluid boluses; new crackles, dyspnea or falling SpO2 typically prompt holding further fluid and provider notification; large volumes are associated with hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, so sodium, chloride and bicarbonate are trended; residual air is removed from flexible bags before pressure infusion; it is the crystalloid used with packed red blood cells|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Sodium Chloride Injection, USP;;NCBI StatPearls: Normal Saline;;Surviving Sepsis Campaign Guidelines (2021)
hypertonic-saline-3-percent|This is a concentrated salt solution given slowly through an IV in the hospital or during critical care transport to raise a very low sodium level or reduce brain swelling. Frequent blood tests track the sodium level. Pain or swelling at the IV site, new confusion, trouble speaking or swallowing, or shortness of breath are reported to staff right away.|Baseline sodium, how long the hyponatremia has been present and IV site quality are reviewed before infusion; serum sodium is typically rechecked every 2 to 4 hours, and a rise faster than the ordered correction goal prompts provider notification because of osmotic demyelination risk; label rate limits are 100 mL/hour and 400 mL/24 hours, given by pump; the bag concentration is double checked to avoid mix-ups with 0.9% or 23.4% sodium chloride; neurologic status, lung sounds and IV site are tracked for infiltration and fluid overload|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Sodium Chloride Injection, USP 3% (Hypertonic);;NCBI StatPearls: Hypertonic Saline;;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings
sodium-chloride-23-4-percent|This is a very concentrated salt solution that the pharmacy mixes into IV fluids or IV nutrition, and in brain emergencies a small amount may be given through a central line. Blood tests track the sodium level closely. New confusion, severe thirst, seizures, or pain at the IV site are reported to staff right away.|Concentrated sodium chloride is an ISMP high-alert medication, and independent double checks of concentration and route are common practice; labeled use is only after dilution in a larger volume of fluid, and off-label undiluted bolus use is central line only; vials are stored apart from 0.9% flush vials to prevent mix-ups; serum sodium, osmolality and neurologic status are tracked after doses; peripheral infiltration typically prompts stopping the infusion and provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Sodium Chloride Injection, USP 23.4%;;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings;;NCBI StatPearls: Hypertonic Saline
potassium-phosphate|This medicine replaces phosphorus, a mineral needed for muscle, breathing and energy, and it is given slowly through an IV in the hospital. Burning at the IV site, muscle cramps or twitching, tingling, an irregular heartbeat or weakness are reported to staff right away. Blood tests check phosphorus, potassium and calcium afterward.|Serum phosphorus, potassium, calcium, magnesium and creatinine are reviewed before dosing, and hyperkalemia or hypocalcemia are common hold thresholds; dosing is ordered in mmol of phosphorus while each mL also carries 4.4 mEq of potassium; it is diluted and run by pump, within the label limits of 6.8 mmol phosphorus/hour peripherally and 15 mmol phosphorus/hour centrally, and not as a bolus per label; peaked T waves, arrhythmia or tetany typically prompt holding and provider notification; calcium-containing solutions in the same line risk precipitation|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Potassium Phosphates Injection;;NCBI StatPearls: Hypophosphatemia;;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings
sodium-phosphate|This medicine replaces phosphorus, a mineral needed for muscles, breathing and energy, and it is mixed into IV fluids or IV nutrition. Muscle cramps, tingling around the mouth, swelling, or pain at the IV site are reported to staff. Blood tests check phosphorus, calcium and sodium levels.|Serum phosphorus, calcium, sodium and renal function are reviewed before dosing; dosing is in mmol of phosphorus, and each mL also contains 4 mEq of sodium; it is diluted in a larger volume of fluid and infused slowly, since rapid infusion is associated with hypocalcemia; tetany, paresthesia or QT changes typically prompt holding and provider notification; calcium and phosphate admixture limits are checked by pharmacy to avoid precipitation|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Sodium Phosphates Injection, USP;;NCBI StatPearls: Hypophosphatemia
magnesium-citrate|This liquid laxative is taken by mouth, often chilled, followed by a full glass of water, and usually causes a bowel movement within 30 minutes to 6 hours. Cramping and loose stools are common. People with kidney disease talk with a clinician before using it, and rectal bleeding, severe stomach pain, vomiting or no bowel movement afterward are reasons to get medical advice.|Renal function is reviewed before use, since absorbed magnesium can accumulate and cause hypermagnesemia; oral tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, bisphosphonates and levothyroxine are separated from the dose by a few hours; hydration status and electrolytes are tracked with repeated doses or bowel prep regimens; loss of deep tendon reflexes, hypotension or bradycardia typically prompt provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Magnesium Citrate Oral Solution (OTC Drug Facts);;NCBI StatPearls: Laxatives;;NCBI StatPearls: Hypermagnesemia
bowel-prep-peg-electrolyte|This drink cleans out the bowel before a colonoscopy, and the label directs 8 ounces every 10 minutes until the full 4 liters is finished. Watery diarrhea usually starts within about an hour. Severe stomach pain, swelling of the belly, repeated vomiting, dizziness or fainting are reported to the care team, and people with diabetes often get special instructions for their medicines during the prep.|Swallowing ability, gag reflex and symptoms of bowel obstruction are reviewed before the prep; oral medicines taken within 1 hour of starting may not be absorbed; older adults and patients on diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs or NSAIDs are watched for dehydration, orthostasis and electrolyte changes; nasogastric administration carries aspiration risk, so the head of bed is typically elevated; abdominal distension or repeated vomiting typically prompts pausing the prep and provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Polyethylene Glycol 3350 and Electrolytes for Oral Solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Polyethylene Glycol;;NCBI StatPearls: Whole Bowel Irrigation
amino-acids-parenteral|This IV solution supplies protein as part of IV nutrition when the stomach and intestines cannot be used. It runs through a special IV line, often a central line, and frequent blood tests check sugar and body chemistry. Fever, chills, redness at the line site, shakiness or confusion are reported to the care team right away.|Central line placement, line site and the correct PN bag and rate are verified at each bag change, with an in-line filter in place; blood glucose is checked frequently at initiation, and electrolytes, phosphorus, magnesium, BUN and liver tests are trended; malnourished patients are watched for refeeding syndrome; fever or line site redness typically prompts provider notification for possible line infection; abrupt interruption of dextrose-containing PN is associated with rebound hypoglycemia|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Amino Acid Injection (Travasol);;NCBI StatPearls: Total Parenteral Nutrition;;NCBI StatPearls: Refeeding Syndrome
oral-glucose-gel|This sugar gel raises a low blood sugar and is used only when the person is awake and able to swallow. A typical dose is one 15 gram tube, with blood sugar checked again in 15 minutes and the dose repeated if still low. Once sugar is back up, a snack or meal helps keep it from dropping again, and a person who cannot swallow or is not waking up needs emergency help.|Level of consciousness, gag reflex and ability to swallow are assessed before oral glucose because of aspiration risk; fingerstick glucose is checked before and about 15 minutes after; hypoglycemia from sulfonylureas or long-acting insulin can recur, so mental status and glucose are tracked over time; declining consciousness or vomiting typically prompts provider notification and IV dextrose or glucagon per protocol|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): none - oral glucose gel is marketed OTC without an FDA-approved label;;American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025);;National EMS Scope of Practice Model (2019)
`);

