DB.addExt(`
saxagliptin-dapagliflozin|This tablet is taken once a day in the morning, with or without food, to lower blood sugar. It makes you pass more urine, so drinking enough fluids matters, and genital yeast infections are common. Vomiting, belly pain, trouble breathing or unusual tiredness can signal ketoacidosis even when sugar is normal and are reported right away, as are pain or swelling in the genital area.|Kidney function is reviewed before starting because the label limits use below eGFR 45; volume status and blood pressure are assessed, especially with diuretics; ketones are checked when patients are ill or fasting because SGLT2 inhibitor DKA can occur with near-normal glucose; dose reduction of insulin or sulfonylurea is often discussed with the prescriber to limit hypoglycemia; new dyspnea or edema is reported because saxagliptin is linked to heart failure hospitalization|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dapagliflozin and saxagliptin;;American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025);;NCBI StatPearls: Sodium-Glucose Transport Protein 2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors
ertugliflozin-sitagliptin|This tablet is taken once a day in the morning, with or without food. It makes you pass more sugar and water in the urine, so drinking enough fluids matters. Nausea, vomiting, belly pain or trouble breathing, foot sores, and genital pain or swelling are reported to the care team right away.|Renal function is reviewed before starting because the label does not recommend use below eGFR 45 and contraindicates it below 30; volume depletion is corrected before starting; ketones are checked during acute illness because euglycemic DKA can occur; feet are inspected for ulcers or infection given the lower limb amputation warning; hypoglycemia risk rises when combined with insulin or sulfonylureas|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ertugliflozin and sitagliptin;;American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025);;NCBI StatPearls: Sitagliptin
linagliptin-metformin|This tablet is taken twice a day with meals to lower blood sugar; stomach upset is common at first and usually fades. Heavy drinking raises the risk of a rare but serious acid buildup. Unusual muscle pain, trouble breathing, severe tiredness, stomach pain with vomiting, or severe belly pain going to the back are reported right away.|Renal function is reviewed before starting and periodically because the label contraindicates use below eGFR 30 and does not recommend starting at eGFR 30 to 45; the drug is typically held before iodinated contrast in at-risk patients and around surgery with restricted intake; lactate is assessed when acidosis is suspected; vitamin B12 is measured periodically; hypoglycemia risk rises with insulin or sulfonylureas|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Linagliptin and metformin hydrochloride;;American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025);;NCBI StatPearls: Metformin
empagliflozin-metformin|This tablet is usually taken twice a day with meals to lower blood sugar; it also makes you pass more sugar in the urine. Stomach upset and genital yeast infections are common. Vomiting, belly pain, fast breathing, unusual muscle pain or tiredness, and genital pain or swelling are reported right away, even if blood sugar seems normal.|Renal function is reviewed before starting because the label contraindicates use below eGFR 30 and does not recommend starting below 45; the drug is typically held before iodinated contrast in at-risk patients and before surgery; ketones and lactate are assessed when patients are ill because both euglycemic DKA and lactic acidosis can occur; volume status is reviewed with diuretics; vitamin B12 is measured periodically|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Empagliflozin and metformin hydrochloride;;American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025);;NCBI StatPearls: Empagliflozin
pioglitazone-glimepiride|This tablet is taken once a day with the first main meal. Skipping meals can cause low blood sugar, so carrying a source of sugar is helpful. Swelling of the legs, fast weight gain, shortness of breath, dark urine, blood in the urine or vision changes are reported right away.|Heart failure history is reviewed because of the boxed warning, and weight gain or edema typically prompts notifying the provider; liver tests are reviewed before starting; blood glucose is followed for hypoglycemia, which can be prolonged with glimepiride; strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil raise pioglitazone levels; beta blockers can mask hypoglycemia symptoms|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pioglitazone and glimepiride;;American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025);;NCBI StatPearls: Pioglitazone
methyltestosterone|This male hormone pill is taken by mouth once a day and is a controlled substance. It can affect the liver, so blood tests are part of treatment. Yellow skin or eyes, dark urine, swelling, or erections that last too long are reported right away, and women report voice deepening or new facial hair.|Liver function tests are reviewed before and during therapy because 17-alpha-alkylated androgens are linked to cholestatic hepatitis and peliosis hepatis; hematocrit and lipids are followed; INR is followed closely with warfarin; in children, bone age is followed because of premature epiphyseal closure; it is a schedule III controlled substance with misuse potential|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Methyltestosterone;;NCBI StatPearls: Anabolic Steroids
etonogestrel-implant|This small rod is placed under the skin of the inner upper arm and prevents pregnancy for up to 5 years. Irregular or unpredictable bleeding is the most common side effect. Pain, numbness or tingling in the arm, not being able to feel the rod, leg pain, chest pain, or signs of pregnancy are reported right away, and other medicines are reviewed because some reduce its effect.|Pregnancy is excluded before insertion, and cycle timing determines whether backup contraception is used for 7 days; the implant is palpated by both the provider and patient after insertion, and a nonpalpable implant is located by imaging; insertion and removal are performed by trained providers under the REMS program; CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin, carbamazepine and efavirenz reduce effectiveness|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Etonogestrel implant;;CDC U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use (2024);;NCBI StatPearls: Etonogestrel
elagolix|This pill lowers estrogen to reduce endometriosis pain and is taken once or twice a day, depending on the dose. Hot flashes and lighter or absent periods are common, which can make pregnancy harder to notice, so a non-hormonal birth control method is used. Mood changes, thoughts of self-harm, yellow skin or eyes, or a possible pregnancy are reported right away.|Pregnancy is excluded before starting and non-hormonal contraception is used because estrogen-containing contraceptives lose efficacy; liver function and hepatic impairment are reviewed, since severe impairment is a contraindication; mood is followed for depression and suicidal ideation; duration is limited by dose because of bone loss; cyclosporine and gemfibrozil are contraindicated combinations|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Elagolix;;NCBI StatPearls: Endometriosis
relugolix-combination|This tablet is taken once a day at about the same time, starting soon after a period begins, for up to 2 years total. Periods often become very light, so a non-hormonal birth control method is used. Leg pain or swelling, chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, severe headache, weakness on one side, yellow skin, or thoughts of self-harm are reported right away.|Thrombotic risk factors such as smoking over age 35, prior clots and migraine with aura are reviewed because of the boxed warning; pregnancy is excluded before starting and non-hormonal contraception is used; blood pressure, mood and liver tests are followed; bone density is assessed at baseline and periodically per label; total duration is limited to 24 months|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Relugolix, estradiol, and norethindrone acetate;;ACOG Practice Bulletin: Management of Symptomatic Uterine Leiomyomas (2021);;NCBI StatPearls: Uterine Fibroids
ospemifene|This pill is taken once a day with food to ease painful sex or vaginal dryness after menopause. Hot flashes and vaginal discharge are common. Any vaginal bleeding, leg pain or swelling, chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, or signs of stroke are reported right away.|History of clots, stroke and hormone-sensitive cancer is reviewed because of contraindications and the boxed warning; postmenopausal bleeding typically prompts evaluation for endometrial pathology; fluconazole and estrogens or other SERMs are not combined per label; therapy is reassessed periodically for the shortest effective duration|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ospemifene;;NCBI StatPearls: Atrophic Vaginitis
`);

