DB.addExt(`
bazedoxifene-conjugated-estrogens|This is one tablet a day for menopause hot flashes and to help protect your bones. It already contains a medicine that protects the lining of the uterus, so other hormone pills are not added. Report leg pain or swelling, chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, severe headache, vision changes or any vaginal bleeding right away.|History of blood clots, stroke, breast cancer, liver disease and undiagnosed vaginal bleeding is reviewed before starting; blood pressure is typically checked at visits; additional estrogens, progestins or SERMs are not combined with it per label; enzyme inducers such as rifampin can lower bazedoxifene levels; immobilization and surgery raise clot risk and are relevant to therapy planning|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Conjugated estrogens/bazedoxifene;;The North American Menopause Society. The 2022 Hormone Therapy Position Statement
pasireotide|This injection lowers the cortisol made because of a pituitary tumor. It often raises blood sugar, so you may be asked to check your sugar often, especially in the first weeks. Report extreme thirst, frequent urination, severe tiredness, dizziness, slow heartbeat, belly pain or yellow skin.|Fasting glucose and HbA1c are reviewed before starting and glucose is followed weekly early in therapy per label; baseline ECG, potassium and magnesium are reviewed because of bradycardia and QT prolongation; symptoms of hypocortisolism (fatigue, nausea, hypotension) are typically reported to the provider; liver tests and gallbladder imaging are followed per label; SC twice-daily Signifor and monthly IM Signifor LAR are distinct products|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pasireotide;;NCBI StatPearls: Cushing Syndrome;;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of Cushing's Syndrome (2015)
osilodrostat|This pill lowers the cortisol your body makes. If your cortisol drops too low you may feel very tired, weak, dizzy or nauseated or start vomiting; report these right away. Your doctor will check blood tests and heart tracings while the dose is adjusted.|Potassium and magnesium are corrected and an ECG reviewed before starting per label; cortisol levels are typically reviewed every 1 to 2 weeks during titration; nausea, vomiting, fatigue or hypotension can signal hypocortisolism and are reported to the provider; QT-prolonging drugs and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or inducers are reviewed for interactions; blood pressure and edema reflect rising mineralocorticoid precursors|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Osilodrostat;;NCBI StatPearls: Cushing Syndrome;;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of Cushing's Syndrome (2015)
metyrapone|This medicine is part of a test of your pituitary and adrenal glands. The capsules are taken at midnight with milk or a snack, and blood is drawn early the next morning. Tell the staff about all your medicines, and report severe weakness, dizziness, vomiting or confusion.|Interfering drugs such as phenytoin, glucocorticoids and estrogens are reviewed before the test; the dose is weight based (30 mg/kg) with a 3 gram maximum per label; morning blood samples for 11-deoxycortisol, cortisol and ACTH are timed per protocol; patients with low adrenal reserve can develop adrenal crisis, so blood pressure and symptoms are observed; nausea is reduced by taking with milk or food|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Metyrapone;;NCBI StatPearls: Adrenal Insufficiency
levoketoconazole|This pill lowers the cortisol your body makes. It can harm the liver and affect heart rhythm, so you will have blood tests and heart tracings often. Report yellow skin or eyes, dark urine, belly pain, fainting, a racing heartbeat, or severe tiredness and dizziness.|Boxed warning for hepatotoxicity and QT prolongation; liver tests are reviewed before starting, weekly during titration and periodically afterward per label; ECG, potassium and magnesium are reviewed before starting and after dose changes; many CYP3A4 substrates and QT-prolonging drugs are contraindicated, so the medication list is reconciled carefully; signs of adrenal insufficiency are reported to the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Levoketoconazole;;NCBI StatPearls: Cushing Syndrome;;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of Cushing's Syndrome (2015)
etelcalcetide|This medicine is given into your dialysis line at the end of each dialysis session to lower your parathyroid hormone. It can lower your calcium. Report tingling around the mouth or in the fingers, muscle cramps or spasms, a fast or irregular heartbeat, or seizures.|Corrected calcium is reviewed before starting and is at or above the lower limit of normal per label; calcium is checked within 1 week after starting or a dose change and then about every 4 weeks; cinacalcet is discontinued at least 7 days before the first dose; the dose is given IV bolus into the venous line at the end of hemodialysis; QT prolongation and GI bleeding are associated risks|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Etelcalcetide;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder (2017);;NCBI StatPearls: Hyperparathyroidism
paricalcitol|This active form of vitamin D helps lower parathyroid hormone in kidney disease. It is usually given during dialysis. Extra vitamin D or calcium products are used only with your care team's approval. Report weakness, confusion, constipation, nausea or an irregular heartbeat.|Calcium and phosphorus are followed frequently during dose titration and PTH guides dose changes per label; hypercalcemia is the classic reason the dose is reduced or interrupted; digoxin patients are at higher risk of toxicity with hypercalcemia; dosing is in mcg/kg, so weight-based calculations receive an independent double check; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Paricalcitol;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder (2017);;NCBI StatPearls: Hyperparathyroidism
doxercalciferol|This vitamin D medicine is turned on by your liver and helps lower parathyroid hormone in kidney disease. It is given at the end of dialysis. Extra vitamin D or calcium products are used only with your care team's approval. Report weakness, confusion, nausea or an irregular heartbeat.|Calcium, phosphorus and intact PTH are followed frequently during titration per label; hypercalcemia or hyperphosphatemia is a classic reason the dose is held; the IV dose is given as a bolus at the end of dialysis three times weekly; digoxin patients are at higher risk with hypercalcemia; magnesium antacids and other vitamin D products are reviewed for interactions|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Doxercalciferol;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder (2017);;NCBI StatPearls: Hyperparathyroidism
corticotropin|This gel medicine is injected under the skin or into a muscle and makes your body produce more of its own steroid hormones. It can raise blood pressure and blood sugar and make infections more likely. Report fever, black stools, mood changes, swelling or high sugar readings, and talk to your care team before any vaccines.|Not for IV use per label; blood pressure, glucose, potassium and weight are followed during therapy; signs of infection may be masked; live vaccines are avoided per label; infants treated for infantile spasms are followed for hypertension, infection and cardiac effects; prolonged therapy is tapered gradually to reduce adrenal insufficiency risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Repository corticotropin injection;;NCBI StatPearls: Infantile Spasms
thyrotropin-alfa|You will get two injections in the buttock muscle, one day apart, before your thyroid cancer blood test or radioiodine scan or treatment. You can keep taking your thyroid hormone pill. Nausea and headache are common; report trouble breathing, neck swelling, severe headache or weakness.|Given IM into the buttock only, two 0.9 mg doses 24 hours apart per label; radioiodine and thyroglobulin draws are timed relative to the injections per protocol; patients with CNS or neck metastases are observed for symptoms of tumor swelling; dialysis patients clear the drug more slowly; hypersensitivity reactions are reported to the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Thyrotropin alfa;;American Thyroid Association Management Guidelines for Adult Patients with Thyroid Nodules and Differentiated Thyroid Cancer (2015)
`);


