DB.addExt(`
palopegteriparatide|This medicine replaces parathyroid hormone and is injected under the skin of the belly or thigh once a day at about the same time. Blood calcium is checked often, and calcium and vitamin D pills are usually lowered as the dose goes up. Tingling, muscle cramps or spasms can mean low calcium, and thirst, constipation or confusion can mean high calcium; both are reported promptly, and stopping suddenly can drop calcium quickly.|Albumin-corrected serum calcium is reviewed before and after each dose change per label, with adjustments of 3 mcg no more often than every 7 days; active vitamin D and calcium supplements are typically tapered as the dose rises; symptoms of hypocalcemia or hypercalcemia commonly prompt notifying the provider; dizziness on standing is associated with early doses; digoxin levels and toxicity are relevant when calcium rises|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Palopegteriparatide;;NCBI StatPearls: Hypoparathyroidism
crinecerfont|This capsule or liquid is taken twice a day with morning and evening meals, together with the usual steroid medicine. It lowers male-type hormones but does not replace cortisol, so steroid doses, stress doses and the emergency injection plan stay in place. Vomiting, severe weakness or fainting can be signs of adrenal crisis and are an emergency; throat tightness or face swelling are reported right away.|Glucocorticoid replacement continues per label, and dose reductions are made by the endocrinologist based on androstenedione and 17-hydroxyprogesterone; signs of adrenal insufficiency (hypotension, vomiting, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia) commonly prompt notifying the provider; doses are given with meals; strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin double the dose per label; hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema lead to discontinuation per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Crinecerfont;;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia Due to Steroid 21-Hydroxylase Deficiency (2018);;NCBI StatPearls: Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia
dexamethasone-oral|This steroid pill or liquid is usually taken in the morning with food to protect the stomach. It can raise blood sugar, change mood or sleep and increase infection risk. After more than a couple of weeks it is tapered rather than stopped suddenly, and fever, black stools or severe mood changes are reported promptly.|Blood glucose is reviewed, especially in diabetes, and sliding-scale needs often rise; signs of infection can be masked; GI protection is often reviewed when NSAIDs are also used; long courses are tapered to avoid adrenal insufficiency; the 1 mg/mL Intensol concentrate is a known mg vs mL error risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dexamethasone;;NCBI StatPearls: Dexamethasone
octreotide-lar|This is a monthly injection into the buttock muscle given in a clinic. It can cause stomach cramps, loose or fatty stools and gallstones, and it can change blood sugar. Severe belly pain, yellow skin or eyes, a very slow heartbeat, or flushing and diarrhea that come back strongly are reported promptly.|The suspension is given deep intragluteally only (not IV or subcutaneous) and is injected right after reconstitution; glucose is reviewed, particularly with insulin or sulfonylureas; heart rate is reviewed because bradycardia is a labeled risk; gallbladder symptoms and periodic ultrasound are part of monitoring; carcinoid and VIPoma patients typically continue subcutaneous octreotide for at least 2 weeks after the switch per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Octreotide (Sandostatin LAR Depot);;NCBI StatPearls: Octreotide;;NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Neuroendocrine and Adrenal Tumors
testosterone-cypionate|This testosterone shot goes deep into the muscle every 2 to 4 weeks and is a controlled substance. It can raise the red blood cell count and blood pressure and lower sperm counts. Chest pain, trouble breathing, leg swelling or pain, or a painful erection are reported right away.|Hematocrit, PSA and serum testosterone are reviewed at baseline and periodically per label; a rising hematocrit, new hypertension or signs of DVT or PE commonly prompt notifying the provider; injection is deep gluteal IM only, not IV; vial strength (100 or 200 mg/mL) is double-checked; cough or dyspnea right after injection can reflect pulmonary oil microembolism|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Testosterone Cypionate;;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Testosterone Therapy in Men with Hypogonadism (2018);;NCBI StatPearls: Testosterone
testosterone-gel|This gel is put on clean, dry skin of the shoulders and upper arms each morning, then hands are washed and the area is covered with clothing once dry. Children and women can be harmed by touching treated skin, so contact is limited and skin is washed before close contact. The gel is flammable until dry, and leg swelling, chest pain or breathing trouble are reported right away.|Pre-dose morning serum testosterone guides dose titration between 20.25 and 81 mg per label; hematocrit and PSA are reviewed periodically; secondary exposure of children is the boxed warning, and household contacts with signs of virilization are relevant; 1% and 1.62% products have different dosing and are not substituted mg for mg|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Testosterone Gel (AndroGel 1.62%);;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Testosterone Therapy in Men with Hypogonadism (2018)
somapacitan|This growth hormone shot is given under the skin once a week on the same day. Injection sites are rotated, and blood sugar can rise. Headaches with vision changes, a limp or hip or knee pain, or belly pain are reported promptly.|Dosing is weekly, not daily, and pen strength is double-checked; IGF-1, glucose, and thyroid function are reviewed periodically, and adrenal function in hypopituitarism; new limp or hip pain in children raises concern for SCFE; headache with vision change raises concern for intracranial hypertension; severe hepatic impairment is not recommended per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Somapacitan;;Pediatric Endocrine Society Guidelines for Growth Hormone and IGF-I Treatment in Children and Adolescents (2016);;NCBI StatPearls: Growth Hormone
lonapegsomatropin|This growth hormone shot is mixed and given under the skin by an auto-injector once a week on the same day. Injection sites are rotated, and blood sugar can rise. Headaches with vision changes, a limp or hip or knee pain, or belly pain are reported promptly.|Dosing is weekly, and at least 8 hours separate the last daily somatropin dose from the first weekly dose per label; IGF-1, glucose and thyroid function are reviewed periodically; new limp or hip pain in children raises concern for SCFE; headache with vision change raises concern for intracranial hypertension; adult starting doses depend on age and oral estrogen use per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lonapegsomatropin;;Pediatric Endocrine Society Guidelines for Growth Hormone and IGF-I Treatment in Children and Adolescents (2016);;NCBI StatPearls: Growth Hormone
histrelin|A small flexible rod is placed under the skin of the inner upper arm and releases medicine for 12 months to pause early puberty. The arm is kept clean and dry for a short time afterward, and some swelling or bruising is common. In the first weeks puberty signs can briefly increase; mood changes, severe headaches, vision changes or seizures are reported promptly.|LH, FSH, sex steroids and bone age are reviewed to confirm suppression; the implant site is reviewed for infection or extrusion; the implant is stored refrigerated until insertion; mood changes, headache with vision change, and seizures are labeled warnings; the implant is removed after 12 months and can be replaced at that time per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Histrelin (Supprelin LA);;NCBI StatPearls: Precocious Puberty
nafarelin|This nasal spray is used twice a day, one spray in one nostril in the morning and one in the other nostril at night for endometriosis. Nasal decongestant sprays are delayed at least 2 hours after a dose. Hot flashes and vaginal dryness are common, nonhormonal birth control is used during treatment, and low mood or thoughts of self-harm are reported right away.|Pregnancy is excluded before the start per label, and treatment typically starts on days 2 to 4 of the cycle; the endometriosis course is limited to 6 months because of bone loss; mood and depression symptoms are reviewed; nasal decongestants are separated by at least 2 hours; breakthrough bleeding can reflect missed doses|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Nafarelin (Synarel);;ACOG Practice Bulletin: Management of Endometriosis (2010)
`);

