DB.addExt(`
mecasermin|This medicine is a shot given under the skin twice a day, shortly before or after a meal or snack, because it can lower blood sugar. If your child cannot eat, that dose is usually skipped. Tell the care team about shakiness, sweating or confusion, headaches with vision changes, a new limp or hip or knee pain, or loud snoring.|Glucose and meal intake are reviewed at the start of therapy and with dose changes, since hypoglycemia is the main adverse effect; the dose is drawn up by weight (mg/kg) and given SC (IV use is contraindicated), with sites rotated; fundoscopic exam, gait and hip complaints and tonsillar size are part of routine follow-up; insulin and other glucose-lowering drugs add to hypoglycemia|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Mecasermin;;Pediatric Endocrine Society Guidelines for Growth Hormone and Insulin-Like Growth Factor-I Treatment in Children and Adolescents (2016)
thyroid-desiccated|This thyroid tablet is usually taken once a day on an empty stomach, apart from calcium, iron and antacids by several hours. It is made from pig thyroid and has not been approved by the FDA, so your provider checks blood tests after any change. Tell your provider about a racing or irregular heartbeat, chest pain, shakiness, or trouble sleeping.|Heart rate and rhythm are reviewed before dose increases, with tachycardia or chest pain as common reasons to hold and notify the provider; TSH and free T4 are typically rechecked 6 to 8 weeks after dose or brand changes; grain and mg confusion is a known error (1 grain = 60 mg on Armour labeling); calcium, iron, PPIs and bile acid sequestrants reduce absorption and warfarin effect increases|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Thyroid tablets (Armour Thyroid), unapproved drug labeling;;American Thyroid Association Guidelines for the Treatment of Hypothyroidism (2014)
hydrocortisone-granules|The capsule is opened and the granules are poured on the tongue, on a spoon, or on a spoonful of cool soft food like yogurt, followed by a drink so all granules are swallowed. The granules are not chewed, crushed, or mixed into liquids. During illness, fever or vomiting, the stress dose plan applies, and a child who cannot keep doses down needs emergency care.|Dose is based on body surface area (mg/m2/day) and checked carefully when switching from other hydrocortisone forms, a time linked to adrenal crisis; vomiting, lethargy, hypotension or hypoglycemia are signs of adrenal crisis that typically prompt urgent provider notification; growth velocity and weight are tracked for overreplacement; CYP3A4 inducers can lower cortisol exposure|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Hydrocortisone oral granules (Alkindi Sprinkle);;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia Due to Steroid 21-Hydroxylase Deficiency (2018);;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Diagnosis and Treatment of Primary Adrenal Insufficiency (2016)
cortisone|This steroid tablet is usually taken with food. Stopping suddenly after long use can be dangerous, so changes are made with your provider. During illness or surgery the dose is often increased. Fever, vomiting, black stools, high blood sugar, or mood changes are reasons to contact your provider.|Indication and duration are reviewed, since long courses suppress the HPA axis and abrupt stopping is associated with adrenal crisis; glucose, potassium, BP and weight are reviewed during therapy; potency differs from hydrocortisone (25 mg cortisone is about 20 mg hydrocortisone), a source of conversion errors; NSAIDs add GI bleeding risk and CYP3A4 inducers lower effect|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Cortisone acetate;;NCBI StatPearls: Corticosteroids
triamcinolone-injection|This is a long-acting steroid shot given into a muscle or a joint, and its effect can last several weeks. If you have diabetes, your blood sugar may run high for a few days afterward. Tell your provider about redness, warmth or swelling at a joint with fever, or skin dimpling or lightening at the injection site.|The vial is shaken to resuspend the drug; IM doses go deep into the gluteal muscle per label, as deltoid or superficial injection is linked to fat atrophy; it is not for IV, epidural, intrathecal or intraocular use; glucose is reviewed in diabetic patients for several days afterward; ritonavir and cobicistat are known to cause Cushing syndrome with triamcinolone|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Triamcinolone acetonide injectable suspension (Kenalog-40);;NCBI StatPearls: Triamcinolone
estradiol-progesterone|This capsule is taken once each evening with food. The progesterone part can make you sleepy, so it is taken at night. Chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, leg pain or swelling, sudden severe headache, weakness on one side, or vision loss are emergencies, and any vaginal bleeding is reported to your provider.|Clot history, breast cancer history, liver disease and undiagnosed bleeding are reviewed before therapy; BP and bleeding pattern are reviewed during therapy; sudden dyspnea, focal deficits, leg swelling or vision loss typically prompt holding and provider notification; drowsiness from progesterone adds to alcohol and sedatives|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Estradiol and progesterone (Bijuva);;The 2022 Hormone Therapy Position Statement of The North American Menopause Society
conjugated-estrogens-vaginal|This cream is placed in the vagina with the applicator, filled to the amount your provider prescribed. A small amount of estrogen reaches the bloodstream. It can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms. Tell your provider about any vaginal bleeding after menopause, breast changes, leg swelling, or chest pain.|The applicator dose is in grams of cream (0.5 g holds about 0.31 mg conjugated estrogens), a common source of confusion; postmenopausal bleeding is evaluated for endometrial cancer; clot, stroke and breast cancer history are reviewed before therapy; latex barrier methods can be weakened by the cream|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Conjugated estrogens vaginal cream (Premarin Vaginal Cream);;The 2020 Genitourinary Syndrome of Menopause Position Statement of The North American Menopause Society
elinzanetant|This hormone-free medicine is taken as two capsules once a day at bedtime, about the same time each night. It can make you sleepy, so driving or risky tasks are best left until you know how it affects you, and alcohol or sleep medicines can add to drowsiness. It is not used during pregnancy; tell your provider about yellow skin or eyes, or dark urine.|Pregnancy status is reviewed before therapy (contraindicated per label); daytime sedation and use of other CNS depressants are reviewed; not recommended in ESRD or moderate to severe hepatic impairment per label; CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers alter exposure|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Elinzanetant;;The 2023 Nonhormone Therapy Position Statement of The North American Menopause Society
norgestrel-otc|This birth control pill is taken once a day at the same time, with no break between packs. If a pill is more than 3 hours late, the label directs taking it as soon as remembered, with condoms as backup for the next 48 hours. It does not protect against HIV or other sexually transmitted infections, and severe pain low on one side of the belly is a reason to get care right away.|Pill timing and missed pills are reviewed, since a delay over 3 hours reduces effectiveness; irregular bleeding is a common expected effect; enzyme-inducing drugs (rifampin, carbamazepine, St John's wort) reduce effectiveness; severe one-sided pelvic pain with late menses is associated with ectopic pregnancy|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Norgestrel (Opill);;CDC U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use (2024)
`);

