DB.addExt(`
ethinyl-estradiol-levonorgestrel|This birth control pill is taken once a day at the same time, and missed pills lower protection, so the pack instructions explain what to do. Spotting, nausea and breast tenderness are common early on. Leg pain or swelling, chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, a severe headache or vision change is an emergency, and smoking raises the risk of heart attack and stroke.|Blood pressure is typically reviewed at baseline and at follow-up, and a significant rise is a reason to notify the prescriber; smoking status over age 35, migraine with aura and clot history are contraindications per label; enzyme inducers such as rifampin or carbamazepine lower efficacy and backup contraception is discussed; leg swelling, chest pain or new neurologic symptoms are handled as possible thrombosis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Levonorgestrel and ethinyl estradiol;;CDC U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use (2024);;NCBI StatPearls: Oral Contraceptive Pills
ethinyl-estradiol-norethindrone|This birth control pill is taken once a day; with Loestrin 21 there is one week with no pills, and with Loestrin Fe the last 7 brown pills contain iron, not hormones. Spotting between periods is common with low-dose pills. Leg pain or swelling, chest pain, sudden shortness of breath or a severe headache is an emergency, and iron pills are kept away from children.|Blood pressure is typically reviewed at baseline and at follow-up, and a significant rise is a reason to notify the prescriber; smoking over age 35, migraine with focal symptoms and clot history are contraindications per label; Loestrin (combined) and norethindrone 0.35 mg (progestin-only) are a known mix-up; enzyme inducers lower efficacy; Fe packs are an iron ingestion risk for young children|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Norethindrone acetate and ethinyl estradiol;;CDC U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use (2024);;NCBI StatPearls: Oral Contraceptive Pills
desogestrel-ethinyl-estradiol|This birth control pill is taken once a day, no more than 24 hours apart, with 21 active pills and 7 reminder pills. It can help acne, and spotting is common at first. Leg pain or swelling, chest pain, sudden shortness of breath or a severe headache is an emergency, and smoking raises the risk of clots, heart attack and stroke.|Blood pressure is typically reviewed at baseline and at follow-up; clot history, inherited thrombophilia and smoking over age 35 are key screening items, since desogestrel pills carry a higher VTE risk than levonorgestrel pills; enzyme inducers lower efficacy; leg swelling, chest pain or new neurologic symptoms are handled as possible thrombosis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Desogestrel and ethinyl estradiol;;CDC U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use (2024);;NCBI StatPearls: Oral Contraceptive Pills
testosterone-nasal|This testosterone gel is pumped once into each nostril three times a day, and blowing the nose or sniffing is avoided for 1 hour afterward. Runny nose, nosebleeds and nasal discomfort are common. Leg pain or swelling, sudden shortness of breath, trouble urinating or very frequent nosebleeds are reported to the prescriber.|Hematocrit, PSA and serum testosterone are typically reviewed at baseline and periodically, and a high hematocrit is a common reason to pause therapy per label; nasal history (surgery, deviated septum, chronic sinus disease) is reviewed before starting; blood pressure is reviewed during testosterone therapy; it is a Schedule III controlled substance; warfarin effect can increase|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Testosterone nasal gel;;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism (2018)
danazol|This capsule is taken twice a day for endometriosis, or two to three times a day for hereditary angioedema. It can cause weight gain, acne, oily skin and a deeper voice that may not go back to normal. Pregnancy is prevented with a non-hormonal method while taking it, and leg swelling, chest pain, severe headache, vision change or yellow skin is reported right away.|Pregnancy is excluded before starting and nonhormonal contraception is used per label; liver function tests and lipids are reviewed periodically; headache with papilledema, nausea or visual change is evaluated as possible intracranial hypertension; warfarin effect rises markedly and INR is reviewed closely; danazol and dantrolene are a sound-alike pair|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Danazol;;US HAEA Medical Advisory Board Guidelines for the Management of Hereditary Angioedema (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Danazol
oxymetholone|This steroid tablet is taken once a day to help the body make more red blood cells, and it often takes 3 to 6 months to work. It can cause acne, swelling and, in women, a deeper voice or extra hair growth. Yellow skin or eyes, belly pain or unusual bleeding is reported right away, and children taking it have regular bone x-rays.|Liver function tests, lipids and hemoglobin are typically reviewed periodically; jaundice or abdominal pain raises concern for cholestasis, peliosis hepatis or liver tumor and prompts notification; INR is reviewed closely with warfarin because effect rises markedly; bone age x-rays every 6 months in prepubertal children per label; it is a Schedule III controlled substance|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oxymetholone;;LiverTox (NIH): Androgenic Steroids
rabeprazole|This acid-reducing tablet is swallowed whole once a day, and for ulcers it is taken after breakfast. Headache or mild stomach upset can happen. Watery diarrhea that does not stop, muscle cramps, a fast or irregular heartbeat, or black stools is reported to the prescriber.|Delayed-release tablets are swallowed whole and not crushed; magnesium is typically reviewed with long-term use or with digoxin or diuretics; persistent diarrhea raises concern for C. difficile; rilpivirine is contraindicated and methotrexate, warfarin and atazanavir are interaction concerns; Aciphex and Aricept are a look-alike pair|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Rabeprazole;;ACG Clinical Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (2022);;NCBI StatPearls: Proton Pump Inhibitors
dexlansoprazole|This acid-reducing capsule is taken once a day, with or without food. The capsule can be opened and sprinkled on applesauce if swallowing is hard, without chewing the beads. Watery diarrhea that does not stop, muscle cramps, a fast or irregular heartbeat, or black stools is reported to the prescriber.|The capsule is swallowed whole or opened onto applesauce without chewing the granules; magnesium is typically reviewed with long-term use; persistent diarrhea raises concern for C. difficile; moderate hepatic impairment limits the dose to 30 mg per label; rilpivirine is contraindicated and methotrexate and tacrolimus are interaction concerns|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dexlansoprazole;;ACG Clinical Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (2022);;NCBI StatPearls: Proton Pump Inhibitors
nizatidine|This acid-reducing capsule is taken once at bedtime or twice a day. Headache or dizziness can happen. Black stools, vomiting blood, trouble swallowing or weight loss is reported to the prescriber, and kidney problems are mentioned because they change the dose.|Creatinine clearance is reviewed because doses are reduced below 50 mL/min per label; confusion in older or renally impaired patients is a reason to notify; persistent ulcer symptoms are evaluated because relief can mask gastric malignancy; nizatidine, nicardipine and tizanidine are look-alike names|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Nizatidine;;ACG Clinical Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (2022);;NCBI StatPearls: H2 Blockers
`);

