DB.addExt(`
trimethobenzamide|This medicine helps with nausea and vomiting after surgery or a stomach bug. It can cause drowsiness, so driving and alcohol are best avoided until its effect is known. Muscle stiffness, unusual movements, confusion or a rash are reported to a clinician; the shot is not used in children.|Pediatric age is relevant because the injection is contraindicated in children and Reye syndrome can be masked; the injection is given deep IM only and IV use is not recommended per label; blood pressure is reviewed after IM doses because hypotension can occur; sedation and extrapyramidal reactions commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; trimethobenzamide and trimethoprim are a known sound-alike pair|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Trimethobenzamide;;NCBI StatPearls: Trimethobenzamide
dolasetron|This tablet is taken within one hour before chemotherapy to prevent nausea and vomiting. Headache and tiredness are common. Fainting, a racing or irregular heartbeat or severe dizziness are reported right away.|Potassium and magnesium are reviewed and corrected before dosing per label; baseline ECG risk factors such as long QT, heart block and antiarrhythmic use are reviewed; PR, QRS or QT prolongation commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; other QT-prolonging and serotonergic drugs are reviewed for interactions|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dolasetron;;NCBI StatPearls: Dolasetron;;NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Antiemesis
rolapitant|This tablet is taken once before chemotherapy along with other anti-nausea medicines and keeps working for several days. It is not repeated more often than every 2 weeks. It affects how some other medicines are handled for weeks, so new prescriptions are reviewed with a pharmacist.|Current medicines are reviewed for CYP2D6 substrates, with thioridazine and pimozide contraindicated per label; doses are spaced no less than 2 weeks apart; digoxin levels, INR and methotrexate toxicity are relevant with interacting drugs; dexamethasone dosing is not reduced because rolapitant does not inhibit CYP3A4|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Rolapitant;;NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Antiemesis
plecanatide|This tablet is taken once a day for long-term constipation or IBS with constipation, with or without food. Diarrhea is the most common side effect, and severe diarrhea is reported to a clinician. The tablets are stored away from children because they can cause dangerous dehydration in young kids.|Age is relevant because use is contraindicated under 6 years and avoided under 18 per label; bowel obstruction history is reviewed before starting; severe diarrhea commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; hydration and electrolytes are reviewed when diarrhea is significant|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Plecanatide;;NCBI StatPearls: Plecanatide;;American College of Gastroenterology Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome (2021)
tenapanor|This tablet is taken twice a day right before breakfast and right before dinner. Diarrhea is common, and severe diarrhea is reported to a clinician. The tablets are stored away from children because they can cause dangerous dehydration in young kids.|Age is relevant because use is contraindicated under 6 years and avoided from 6 to under 12 per label; bowel obstruction history is reviewed before starting; severe diarrhea commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; in dialysis patients on Xphozah, phosphorus is reviewed with other binders|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tenapanor;;NCBI StatPearls: Tenapanor;;American College of Gastroenterology Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome (2021)
naldemedine|This tablet is taken once a day for constipation caused by opioid pain medicine, with or without food. Stomach pain or diarrhea can happen; severe or worsening stomach pain is reported right away. It does not treat an opioid overdose, and it is stopped if the opioid is stopped.|Bowel obstruction history and GI wall disease are reviewed before starting; severe, persistent or worsening abdominal pain is associated with perforation and prompts holding and notifying the provider; opioid withdrawal symptoms such as sweating, chills, yawning and anxiety are reported; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers are reviewed for interactions|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Naldemedine;;NCBI StatPearls: Opioid-Induced Constipation;;American Gastroenterological Association Guideline on the Medical Management of Opioid-Induced Constipation (2019)
alvimopan|This capsule is given in the hospital before and after bowel surgery to help the bowels start working sooner. Chest pain or new heart symptoms are reported to staff right away. Recent opioid pain medicine use is shared with the team before surgery.|Opioid history is reviewed because therapeutic opioid use for more than 7 consecutive days before surgery is a contraindication per label; dispensing is limited to REMS-enrolled hospitals and a maximum of 15 doses; return of bowel function is documented; chest pain or cardiac symptoms prompt notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Alvimopan;;NCBI StatPearls: Alvimopan
eluxadoline|This tablet is taken twice a day with food for IBS with diarrhea. Alcohol is limited because heavy drinking raises the risk of pancreatitis. New or worsening stomach pain, especially pain that spreads to the back, or severe constipation is reported right away.|Gallbladder status is reviewed because patients without a gallbladder are contraindicated per label; alcohol intake and pancreatitis history are reviewed before starting; severe constipation or upper abdominal pain radiating to the back commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; it is a Schedule IV controlled substance; other constipating drugs add risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Eluxadoline;;NCBI StatPearls: Eluxadoline;;American College of Gastroenterology Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome (2021)
alosetron|This tablet is for women with severe diarrhea-type IBS and is started at a low dose. Constipation, rectal bleeding, bloody diarrhea or new or worse stomach pain are reasons to stop it and contact the prescriber right away. Fluvoxamine is not taken with it.|Constipation is reviewed before each dose because it is a reason not to start or to discontinue per label; rectal bleeding, bloody diarrhea or new abdominal pain are associated with ischemic colitis and prompt holding and notifying the provider; fluvoxamine is contraindicated and other CYP1A2 inhibitors are reviewed|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Alosetron;;NCBI StatPearls: Alosetron;;American College of Gastroenterology Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome (2021)
obeticholic-acid|This medicine was used for primary biliary cholangitis and was withdrawn from the US market in 2025. Itching was common. Yellow skin or eyes, a swollen belly, confusion, vomiting blood or black stools were reasons to seek care right away.|Cirrhosis status and signs of portal hypertension are reviewed because decompensated cirrhosis is a contraindication per label; ALP, bilirubin and liver enzymes are reviewed during therapy; jaundice, ascites or encephalopathy prompt holding and notifying the provider; bile acid binding resins are separated by at least 4 hours|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Obeticholic Acid;;NCBI StatPearls: Obeticholic Acid;;AASLD Practice Guidance on Primary Biliary Cholangitis
`);

