DB.addExt(`
trimipramine|This antidepressant capsule often causes sleepiness, so it is commonly taken at bedtime, and mood benefits can take 2 to 4 weeks. Dry mouth, constipation and dizziness on standing are common, and alcohol adds to drowsiness. New thoughts of self-harm, a racing or irregular heartbeat, fainting or trouble urinating are reported right away, and an overdose is a medical emergency; 911 and Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) are resources.|Mood, suicidality and sedation are typically reviewed at each visit, with closer attention in young adults and after dose changes; baseline ECG is commonly reviewed in older adults and cardiac disease, and QRS widening or new conduction delay prompts notifying the provider; orthostatic blood pressure and fall risk are reviewed, especially in the elderly; a 14-day separation from MAOIs, linezolid and IV methylene blue applies per label; quantities dispensed are often limited in patients at risk of overdose|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Trimipramine;;NCBI StatPearls: Tricyclic Antidepressants;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
amoxapine|This antidepressant tablet is usually taken two or three times a day or once at bedtime, and benefits can take a week or more. Drowsiness, dry mouth and constipation are common. Muscle stiffness, restlessness, unusual movements of the face or tongue, fever with confusion, a seizure or thoughts of self-harm are reported right away, and an overdose is a medical emergency; 911 and Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) are resources.|Mood and suicidality are reviewed at each visit; abnormal involuntary movements, rigidity or fever are reviewed because the 7-hydroxy metabolite blocks D2 receptors (tardive dyskinesia and NMS risk); seizure history is reviewed before starting, and new seizures prompt notifying the provider; orthostatic blood pressure is reviewed in older adults; amoxapine and amoxicillin are a known look-alike pair|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Amoxapine;;NCBI StatPearls: Tricyclic Antidepressants
maprotiline|This antidepressant tablet is taken once or a few times a day, and mood benefits usually take 2 to 3 weeks. Dry mouth, drowsiness and constipation are common, and doses are increased slowly to lower the risk of seizures. A seizure, rash, fast or irregular heartbeat or thoughts of self-harm are reported right away.|Seizure history is reviewed before starting, since the label lists seizure disorders as a contraindication; doses are typically held at the starting level for 2 weeks and raised in 25 mg steps per label; mood and suicidality are reviewed at each visit; ECG and orthostatic blood pressure are reviewed in older adults or cardiac disease; the US product was discontinued in 2020, so current use is uncommon|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Maprotiline;;NCBI StatPearls: Tricyclic Antidepressants
isocarboxazid|This antidepressant tablet blocks an enzyme that also breaks down tyramine, a substance in aged cheese, cured meats, tap beer and some fermented foods, so those foods can cause a dangerous rise in blood pressure. Many cold medicines, cough syrups with dextromethorphan, stimulants and some pain medicines interact with it, so every new medicine is reviewed with a pharmacist. A sudden severe headache, chest pain, fever with stiffness or confusion is an emergency.|Blood pressure, including orthostatic readings, is typically reviewed before doses, and a sudden rise with occipital headache prompts notifying the provider; the medication list is reviewed for serotonergic drugs, sympathomimetics, meperidine and dextromethorphan, and a 14-day washout applies when switching antidepressants; liver tests are reviewed because liver disease is a contraindication; the label calls for stopping at least 10 days before elective surgery|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Isocarboxazid;;NCBI StatPearls: Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors
selegiline-transdermal|This antidepressant patch is applied once every 24 hours to clean, dry skin on the upper chest or back, upper thigh or outer upper arm, rotating sites. At the 6 mg dose special diet limits are not usually needed, but at 9 or 12 mg high-tyramine foods such as aged cheese and tap beer are avoided. Heating pads over the patch, cough and cold medicines and many pain medicines can cause serious reactions, and a sudden severe headache or fever with confusion is an emergency.|Blood pressure and mood are reviewed at each visit; the patch site and dose are documented, and patches are removed and folded sticky sides together after use; the medication list is reviewed for SSRIs, SNRIs, tramadol, meperidine, methadone, dextromethorphan and carbamazepine, all contraindicated per label; tyramine restrictions apply at 9 and 12 mg and for 2 weeks after lowering to 6 mg per label; selegiline metabolites can cause a positive amphetamine urine screen|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Selegiline transdermal system;;NCBI StatPearls: Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors
gepirone|This extended-release antidepressant tablet is taken once a day and swallowed whole, and the dose is raised over the first 2 weeks. Dizziness, nausea and trouble sleeping are common early on. Fainting, a pounding or irregular heartbeat, new antibiotics or antifungals, or thoughts of self-harm are reported right away.|A baseline ECG is reviewed, and the label lists QTc above 450 msec as a contraindication; ECGs are repeated during titration, and QTc prolongation prompts notifying the provider; potassium and magnesium are corrected before starting per label; the medication list is reviewed for strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (contraindicated), moderate inhibitors (dose halved) and MAOIs; mood and suicidality are reviewed at each visit|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Gepirone;;Fabre-Kramer product information: Exxua
iloperidone|This antipsychotic tablet is taken twice a day, and the dose is increased slowly over the first week or so to prevent dizziness and fainting. Getting up slowly helps, and missed doses for more than a few days can mean restarting at a low dose. Fainting, a racing heartbeat, an erection lasting more than 4 hours, or fever with stiff muscles are reported right away.|Orthostatic blood pressure and heart rate are typically reviewed during titration, and symptomatic hypotension or syncope prompts holding and notifying the provider; baseline ECG and potassium and magnesium are reviewed in patients with cardiac risk because of QTc prolongation; the medication list is reviewed for strong CYP2D6 or CYP3A4 inhibitors (dose halved per label); weight, glucose and lipids are reviewed periodically|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Iloperidone;;NCBI StatPearls: Antipsychotic Medications;;American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Schizophrenia (2020)
olanzapine-samidorphan|This once-a-day tablet combines an antipsychotic with an opioid blocker. Opioid pain medicines and opioid cough medicines do not work well while taking it and are not used with it, and trying to overcome the blockade with large opioid doses can be fatal. Weight, blood sugar and cholesterol are checked over time, and fever with rash, stiff muscles or confusion is reported right away.|Recent opioid use is reviewed before the first dose, since the label requires waiting at least 7 days after short-acting and 14 days after long-acting opioids; emergency or surgical teams are informed that the patient has an opioid antagonist on board; weight, fasting glucose and lipids are reviewed at baseline and periodically; sedation and orthostatic blood pressure are reviewed, especially in older adults; fever with rash or organ involvement is reviewed for DRESS|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Olanzapine and samidorphan;;NCBI StatPearls: Antipsychotic Medications;;American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Schizophrenia (2020)
thiothixene|This antipsychotic capsule is taken once or a few times a day. Restlessness, muscle stiffness, shakiness or sudden muscle spasms of the neck, jaw or eyes can happen and are reported, as are fever with stiff muscles or confusion. Alcohol adds to drowsiness, and getting up slowly helps with dizziness.|Abnormal movements are reviewed at baseline and periodically (for example with AIMS), and acute dystonia or akathisia prompts notifying the provider; temperature, rigidity and mental status are reviewed for NMS; CBC is reviewed if fever or sore throat develops because of leukopenia risk; orthostatic blood pressure is reviewed in older adults; thiothixene and thioridazine are a known sound-alike pair|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Thiothixene;;NCBI StatPearls: Antipsychotic Medications
trifluoperazine|This antipsychotic tablet is usually taken twice a day. Restlessness, muscle stiffness, tremor or sudden spasms of the neck, jaw or eyes can happen and are reported, as are yellow skin, sore throat with fever, or fever with stiff muscles. Sun protection helps because of skin sensitivity, and alcohol adds to drowsiness.|Abnormal movements are reviewed at baseline and periodically, and acute dystonia or akathisia prompts notifying the provider; CBC and liver tests are reviewed if sore throat, fever or jaundice develops (agranulocytosis and cholestatic jaundice risk); for non-psychotic anxiety the label limits use to 6 mg per day and 12 weeks; temperature, rigidity and mental status are reviewed for NMS|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Trifluoperazine;;NCBI StatPearls: Antipsychotic Medications
`);


