DB.addExt(`
fluphenazine-decanoate|This long-acting injection for schizophrenia is given in the clinic every few weeks, so keeping injection appointments matters. Stiffness, restlessness, tremor, or new tongue or face movements are reported to the care team. High fever with muscle stiffness or confusion is an emergency, and hot weather and alcohol add risk.|Patients are typically stabilized on oral fluphenazine before the depot per label; a dry syringe and needle of at least 21 gauge is used, and Z-track deep IM technique commonly reduces leakage; fluphenazine hydrochloride (2.5 mg/mL) and decanoate (25 mg/mL) are a known concentration mix-up; fever, rigidity or altered mental status typically prompt holding and notifying the provider because of NMS risk; CBC is reviewed when infection or sore throat appears because of agranulocytosis risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fluphenazine Decanoate;;NCBI StatPearls: Fluphenazine;;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings
dextromethorphan-quinidine|This capsule helps reduce sudden crying or laughing episodes and is taken once a day for the first week, then twice a day. Dizziness and falls can happen, and fainting, a racing or irregular heartbeat, unusual bruising or bleeding, or yellow skin are reported right away. Extra cough medicines containing dextromethorphan and new antidepressants are reviewed with the pharmacist first.|An ECG is reviewed at baseline and after starting in patients with QT risk factors or on CYP3A4 inhibitors per label; MAOIs within 14 days, quinine and mefloquine are contraindicated per label; bruising, petechiae or bleeding typically prompt holding and notifying the provider because of quinidine thrombocytopenia; serotonergic drugs are linked to serotonin syndrome; quinidine inhibits CYP2D6 and changes levels of many other drugs|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dextromethorphan and Quinidine;;NCBI StatPearls: Pseudobulbar Affect
ciclesonide|This inhaler is a daily preventer for asthma and does not stop an attack in progress; a rescue inhaler is used for that. It is used every day, even when breathing feels fine, and rinsing the mouth with water and spitting after each use helps prevent thrush. Worsening breathing or more rescue inhaler use is reported to the prescriber.|Rescue inhaler use and asthma symptoms are reviewed at each visit; the mouth is inspected for white patches of thrush; patients switched from oral steroids are watched for adrenal insufficiency during the taper per label; growth in adolescents and eye exams are part of long-term monitoring; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise exposure|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ciclesonide;;Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) Strategy Report (2024);;NCBI StatPearls: Inhaled Corticosteroids
flunisolide|This steroid nasal spray works best when used every day and can take several days to help. The nose is cleared first and the spray is aimed away from the middle of the nose. Frequent nosebleeds, nose sores or crusting are reported to the prescriber.|Nasal mucosa is inspected for irritation, bleeding or ulceration with long-term use; recent nasal surgery or trauma is reviewed before starting; children are dosed per the label maximum of 4 sprays in each nostril per day; combined nasal and inhaled steroids increase total steroid exposure|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Flunisolide;;NCBI StatPearls: Intranasal Corticosteroids
arformoterol|This nebulizer medicine opens the airways for about 12 hours and is used twice a day, morning and evening. It is not a rescue medicine for sudden breathing trouble. Fast heartbeat, chest pain, shakiness or breathing that gets worse right after a treatment is reported promptly.|Heart rate and rhythm are reviewed, and new tachyarrhythmia typically prompts holding and notifying the provider; paradoxical bronchospasm leads to discontinuation per label; potassium and glucose are relevant with diuretics or diabetes; duplicate LABA therapy with formoterol or salmeterol inhalers is a known error; vials stay in the foil pouch until use|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Arformoterol;;GOLD Report: Global Strategy for COPD (2025)
olodaterol|This soft mist inhaler is used as two puffs once a day at the same time each day. It does not work fast enough for sudden breathing trouble, so a rescue inhaler is kept on hand. Fast heartbeat, chest pain or tremor is reported.|Heart rate and rhythm are reviewed, and new tachyarrhythmia typically prompts holding and notifying the provider; paradoxical bronchospasm leads to discontinuation per label; duplicate LABA therapy with Stiolto or other combination inhalers is a known error; potassium is relevant with loop or thiazide diuretics|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Olodaterol;;GOLD Report: Global Strategy for COPD (2025)
aclidinium|This dry powder inhaler is used twice a day, morning and evening, to keep the airways open; it does not stop a sudden attack. Eye pain, blurred vision, seeing halos, or trouble passing urine are reported right away. People with a severe milk protein allergy are not candidates for this inhaler.|Glaucoma and urinary retention history are reviewed before starting; eye pain or difficulty urinating typically prompt holding and notifying the provider; milk protein allergy is a labeled contraindication because of the lactose carrier; duplicate LAMA therapy with tiotropium or umeclidinium is a known error|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Aclidinium;;GOLD Report: Global Strategy for COPD (2025)
revefenacin|This nebulizer medicine is used once a day to keep the airways open; it does not stop a sudden attack. Eye pain, blurred vision, or trouble passing urine are reported right away. Liver disease and new medicines are reviewed with the prescriber.|Hepatic impairment is reviewed before starting because the label does not recommend use in any degree of hepatic impairment; OATP1B inhibitors such as rifampin and cyclosporine raise exposure; eye pain or difficulty urinating typically prompt holding and notifying the provider; duplicate LAMA therapy is a known error|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Revefenacin;;GOLD Report: Global Strategy for COPD (2025)
`);

