DB.addExt(`
tapinarof|This cream is applied in a thin layer to the affected skin once a day, and hands are washed afterward unless the hands are being treated. It does not contain a steroid. Red bumps around hair follicles (folliculitis), skin rash or irritation are reported to the prescriber.|Thin layer once daily to affected areas per label; not for oral, ophthalmic or intravaginal use; folliculitis and contact dermatitis are the most common reasons for review; psoriasis use is limited to adults and atopic dermatitis use to patients 2 years and older per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tapinarof;;NCBI StatPearls: Psoriasis;;NCBI StatPearls: Atopic Dermatitis
podofilox|This liquid or gel is put only on the visible warts twice a day for 3 days in a row, followed by 4 days with no treatment, and this weekly cycle can be repeated up to 4 times. Burning, soreness and redness at the warts are common. It is kept away from the eyes and out of reach of children, and pregnancy is discussed with the prescriber.|Treatment area is limited to 10 cm2 of wart tissue and 0.5 g of gel per day per label; the 3 days on, 4 days off cycle is reviewed with the patient; pregnancy is a reason it is not recommended in CDC STI guidelines; severe pain, bleeding or ulceration typically prompts holding and notifying the provider; ingestion by a child is a poisoning concern|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Podofilox;;CDC STI Treatment Guidelines (2021);;NCBI StatPearls: Genital Warts
ivermectin-topical|A pea-size amount of this cream is spread thinly on each affected area of the face once a day, away from the eyes and lips. It treats the red bumps and pimples of rosacea and works gradually over weeks. Burning or irritation of the skin is reported to the prescriber.|Pea-size amount to each affected facial area once daily per label; targets papulopustular lesions rather than background erythema; burning and irritation are the main local reactions; Soolantra cream and Sklice lice lotion are different products and strengths|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ivermectin;;NCBI StatPearls: Rosacea
brimonidine-topical|A pea-sized amount of this gel is spread thinly on each of five areas of the face once a day, away from the eyes and lips, and hands are washed afterward. Redness can come back or worsen as the effect wears off. The tube is stored where young children cannot reach it, because swallowing even a small amount can make a child very sleepy with slow breathing and heartbeat.|Pea-sized amount to each of five facial areas once daily per label; dizziness, low blood pressure or slow heart rate are reported postmarketing; rebound erythema and flushing are common reasons for review; pediatric ingestion causes clonidine-like toxicity and is a Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) matter; caution with depression, vascular insufficiency and severe cardiovascular disease per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Brimonidine;;NCBI StatPearls: Rosacea;;NCBI StatPearls: Alpha-2 Agonist Toxicity
oxymetazoline-topical|A pea-sized amount of this cream is spread thinly over the whole face once a day, away from the eyes and lips, and hands are washed right after. Skin irritation or more pimples can occur. Eye pain, blurred vision or seeing halos are reported right away, especially with a history of glaucoma.|Pea-sized amount once daily over the entire face per label; narrow-angle glaucoma history is reviewed because of an angle-closure warning; blood pressure effects are possible in cardiovascular disease; the 1% cream differs from the nasal spray and the 0.1% Upneeq eye drop; pediatric ingestion of imidazolines can cause clonidine-like toxicity|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oxymetazoline;;NCBI StatPearls: Rosacea
hydroquinone|This cream is rubbed into the dark spots only, usually twice a day, with daily sunscreen because sunlight brings the spots back. Results take weeks to months, and if nothing improves after 2 months the prescriber reassesses. Skin irritation, blistering or a blue-black darkening of the skin is reported.|Single-ingredient hydroquinone is marketed without FDA approval, while Tri-Luma is the FDA-approved combination; a test patch before full use is described in product labeling; broad-spectrum sunscreen accompanies therapy; lack of response by 2 months, contact dermatitis or ochronosis typically prompts stopping and notifying the provider; imported lightening creams can contain mercury or steroids|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Hydroquinone (unapproved drug labeling);;NCBI StatPearls: Hydroquinone;;NCBI StatPearls: Melasma
tralokinumab|This eczema medicine is a shot under the skin, usually every other week after a larger first dose, and can be given at home after training. Eye redness, itching or discharge, cold symptoms and injection site reactions are common. Swelling of the face, trouble breathing or a widespread rash is an emergency, and live vaccines are discussed with the prescriber first.|Loading dose followed by every-other-week dosing per label; conjunctivitis and keratitis symptoms are reviewed at visits; helminth infections are treated before starting per label; live vaccines are avoided during treatment; signs of hypersensitivity typically prompt holding and notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tralokinumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Atopic Dermatitis
lebrikizumab|This eczema medicine is a shot under the skin, given every 2 weeks at first and then every 4 or 8 weeks once the skin is better. Eye redness or itching, injection site reactions and shingles can occur. Allergic reactions such as swelling or trouble breathing are an emergency, and live vaccines are discussed with the prescriber first.|Induction 500 mg at Weeks 0 and 2 then 250 mg every two weeks, then maintenance every four or eight weeks per label; approved for adolescents only at 40 kg or more; conjunctivitis, keratitis and herpes zoster are reviewed; helminth infections are treated before starting; live vaccines are avoided during treatment|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lebrikizumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Atopic Dermatitis
nemolizumab|This medicine is a shot under the skin every 4 weeks after a double first dose, aimed mainly at stopping itch. Headache, joint or muscle aches and hives can occur. New wheezing or eczema flares, or signs of an allergic reaction, are reported to the prescriber.|Prurigo nodularis dose depends on weight (cutoff 90 kg) per label; atopic dermatitis use is combined with topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors per label; new or worsening asthma and eczema flares are reviewed; hypersensitivity typically prompts holding and notifying the provider; live vaccines are avoided during treatment|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Nemolizumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Prurigo Nodularis;;NCBI StatPearls: Atopic Dermatitis
abrocitinib|This eczema pill is taken once a day with or without food. It weakens part of the immune system, so fever, cough, painful skin blisters (shingles), leg swelling, chest pain or shortness of breath are reported right away. Blood thinners that affect platelets, other than low-dose aspirin, are not combined with it in the first 3 months.|Boxed warning for serious infections, mortality, malignancy, MACE and thrombosis; CBC with platelets and lymphocytes is reviewed at baseline and about 4 weeks after starting or increasing the dose, and lipids at about 4 weeks; antiplatelet therapy other than low-dose aspirin is contraindicated in the first 3 months; TB and viral hepatitis screening precede therapy; serious infection or thrombosis typically prompts holding and notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Abrocitinib;;NCBI StatPearls: Janus Kinase Inhibitors;;NCBI StatPearls: Atopic Dermatitis
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
ritlecitinib|This capsule is taken once a day, with or without food, and is swallowed whole. It lowers immune activity, so fever, cough, painful skin blisters or other signs of infection are reported promptly. Chest pain, shortness of breath, or a swollen painful leg can signal a heart problem or blood clot and are an emergency. Blood tests are done before starting and during treatment, and live vaccines are not given while taking it.|TB screening, hepatitis screening and baseline lymphocyte and platelet counts are reviewed before starting per label; initiation is not recommended with lymphocytes below 500/mm3 or platelets below 100,000/mm3 per label; fever or signs of serious infection typically prompt holding and notifying the provider; herpes zoster, thrombosis symptoms and chest pain are reportable findings; combination with other JAK inhibitors or potent immunosuppressants is not recommended per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ritlecitinib;;NCBI StatPearls: Janus Kinase Inhibitors
deuruxolitinib|This tablet is taken twice a day, with or without food, for severe hair loss from alopecia areata. It lowers immune activity, so fever, cough, painful skin blisters or other signs of infection are reported promptly. New medicines, especially the antifungal fluconazole, are checked with the pharmacist because some raise drug levels. Chest pain, shortness of breath or a swollen painful leg are an emergency.|CYP2C9 poor metabolizer status and moderate or strong CYP2C9 inhibitors such as fluconazole are contraindications per label; TB and hepatitis screening and baseline blood counts are reviewed before starting; fever or serious infection typically prompts holding and notifying the provider; hemoglobin, neutrophil, lymphocyte and platelet counts and lipids are followed during therapy; thrombosis and MACE symptoms are reportable findings|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Deuruxolitinib;;NCBI StatPearls: Janus Kinase Inhibitors
capsaicin-topical|The high-strength Qutenza patch is put on only by a clinician in the office, sometimes after a numbing cream, and stays on for 30 or 60 minutes. Burning and redness at the site are expected for a day or more, and cool packs can help. Touching the eyes, nose or mouth after contact with capsaicin causes intense burning. Low-strength store creams also burn at first, and heat on treated skin makes it worse.|Qutenza is handled and applied only by physicians or health care professionals using nitrile gloves per label; blood pressure is reviewed before, during and after the procedure because pain can raise it; severe pain or a marked rise in blood pressure typically prompts removal and provider notification; eye and mucous membrane exposure is prevented and treated with flushing; cleansing gel removes residual capsaicin after the patch comes off|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Capsaicin;;NCBI StatPearls: Capsaicin
pramoxine|This anti-itch lotion is rubbed onto the itchy or sore skin up to 3 or 4 times a day. It is for use on the skin only and is kept out of the eyes. If the rash gets worse or lasts more than 7 days, a doctor or nurse can look at it. Children under 2 years use it only with advice from a doctor.|Skin integrity and the cause of the itch are reviewed before use; application is limited to 3 to 4 times daily per label; worsening rash or symptoms beyond 7 days typically prompt provider notification; pramoxine has low cross-sensitivity with amide and ester local anesthetics; sound-alike names include promethazine and pramipexole|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pramoxine;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Anesthetics
zinc-oxide|This thick white paste protects skin from wetness and helps heal diaper rash. It is spread on clean, dry skin with each diaper change, especially at bedtime. It is for skin only and is kept out of the eyes. A rash that gets worse, lasts more than 7 days, or has red bumps around the edges is checked by a health care provider.|Skin is cleansed and dried before application per label; worsening rash, open wounds or satellite lesions suggesting Candida typically prompt provider notification; the opaque barrier can hide skin breakdown, so the skin is inspected at changes|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Zinc oxide;;NCBI StatPearls: Diaper Dermatitis
selenium-sulfide|This medicated lotion or shampoo is lathered on the skin or scalp with a little water, left on for the time directed, then rinsed well. It is kept away from the eyes and genital area, and jewelry is removed first because it can be damaged. Broken or inflamed skin is not treated with it. Irritation, burning or more hair loss is reported.|The product is kept off broken or inflamed skin per label; for tinea versicolor it stays on 10 minutes before thorough rinsing per label; jewelry is removed before use; irritation or worsening rash typically prompts provider notification; recurrence of tinea versicolor is common|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Selenium sulfide;;NCBI StatPearls: Tinea Versicolor
mometasone-topical|This steroid cream is spread in a thin film on the rash once a day. It is not covered with tight bandages or plastic unless the prescriber says so, and it is used only as long as directed. Long use on the face, groin or armpits can thin the skin. If the rash is not better in 2 weeks, the prescriber reassesses it.|Area, duration and use under occlusion are reviewed because they raise systemic absorption; children have greater risk of HPA axis suppression; no improvement within 2 weeks typically prompts reassessment per label; therapy is stopped when control is achieved per label; skin atrophy, striae and signs of infection are reportable findings|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Mometasone furoate;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Corticosteroids
clascoterone|This acne cream is spread in a thin layer, about 1 gram, on the affected skin in the morning and evening. It is kept out of the eyes, mouth and other moist areas. Redness, dryness or peeling can happen. Unusual tiredness, weakness or dizziness is reported because the cream can rarely affect the body's stress hormones.|Application is about 1 gram twice daily per label; HPA axis suppression and hyperkalemia were observed in trials, so fatigue, weakness or symptoms of adrenal insufficiency typically prompt provider notification; large treated areas and occlusion raise systemic exposure; local skin reactions are reviewed at follow-up|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Clascoterone;;NCBI StatPearls: Acne Vulgaris
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
delgocitinib|This cream is spread in a thin layer twice a day only on the hands and wrists, on clean, dry skin. It is kept away from the eyes, mouth and other mucous membranes. Redness, swelling, pus or pain around the nails or fingers can be an infection and is reported to the prescriber.|Hands are typically inspected for paronychia, cellulitis and other infection before and during therapy; the labeled limit is 30 g per 2 weeks or 60 g per month; vaccination status, including shingles vaccine, is reviewed before starting per label; systemic JAK inhibitors and potent immunosuppressants are not recommended together per label; new or changing skin lesions are relevant because non-melanoma skin cancer is a labeled warning|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Delgocitinib;;NCBI StatPearls: Janus Kinase Inhibitors
methoxsalen|This capsule is taken 1.5 to 2 hours before the light treatment with a low-fat snack or milk, and the dose depends on body weight. The skin and eyes are very sensitive to sunlight for the rest of the day, so UV-blocking wraparound sunglasses are worn for 24 hours and sun exposure is kept to a minimum. Painful redness, blisters, or eye problems are reported to the care team.|Dose is based on the label weight table and timing relative to UVA is confirmed; UVA-blocking eye protection for 24 hours after each dose is part of standard PUVA teaching; other photosensitizing drugs (thiazides, tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, phenothiazines) are reviewed; severe erythema or blistering typically prompts holding treatment and notifying the provider; soft gelatin and hard capsule forms are not interchangeable at the same dose|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Methoxsalen;;NCBI StatPearls: Psoralen Plus Ultraviolet A (PUVA) Therapy
afamelanotide|This small implant is placed under the skin above the hip bone by a trained clinician every 2 months. The skin and freckles may darken, and nausea or soreness at the implant site can happen. Usual light protection continues, and yearly skin checks with a dermatologist are part of care.|Insertion is done by a trained professional with aseptic technique, and the patient is observed for 30 minutes afterward per label; the dressing stays on 24 hours; hypersensitivity signs after insertion prompt notifying the provider; full-body skin examination twice yearly is recommended per label because nevi and freckles can darken|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Afamelanotide;;NCBI StatPearls: Erythropoietic Protoporphyria
lidocaine-topical|This numbing ointment is spread thinly on the sore area and used only in the amount prescribed. After it is used in the mouth or throat, eating and drinking wait until feeling returns to prevent choking or biting the tongue. Numbness around the mouth, ringing in the ears, confusion or a seizure are emergencies, and the tube is stored away from children.|The total lidocaine dose from all products (patch, jelly, injection) is counted together per label; adults are limited to 5 g of ointment per application and about 17 to 20 g per day; swallowing and gag reflex are reviewed after oropharyngeal use; perioral numbness, tinnitus or confusion typically prompt removing the product and notifying the provider; cyanosis with low SpO2 can reflect methemoglobinemia|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lidocaine;;NCBI StatPearls: Lidocaine;;NCBI StatPearls: Local Anesthetic Toxicity
diphenhydramine-topical|This anti-itch cream is rubbed on the itchy area up to 3 or 4 times a day, but not on large areas of the body or on chickenpox or measles. It is not used together with Benadryl taken by mouth. A rash that gets worse, or confusion, flushing or a fast heartbeat in a child, are reasons to get medical help.|Extent of skin and any chickenpox lesions are reviewed because absorption over large or broken areas can cause anticholinergic toxicity; oral diphenhydramine and other anticholinergics add to the risk; confusion, mydriasis, flushing, tachycardia or urinary retention in a child typically prompt notifying the provider; worsening rash with use can reflect allergic contact dermatitis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Diphenhydramine and Zinc Acetate topical (OTC Drug Facts);;NCBI StatPearls: Diphenhydramine
abametapir|This lice lotion is used one time: it is shaken, worked into dry hair and scalp until fully coated, left on for 10 minutes, then rinsed with warm water. An adult applies it to children, it is kept out of the eyes, and the bottle is kept away from small children because swallowing it is dangerous. Leftover lotion is thrown away.|Age is confirmed as 6 months or older because of benzyl alcohol toxicity risk in young infants; application is a single 10-minute treatment with an adult supervising; eye contact is rinsed with water; current medicines are reviewed because abametapir inhibits CYP3A4, CYP2B6 and CYP1A2 for about 2 weeks; ingestion by a child prompts Poison Control contact|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Abametapir;;NCBI StatPearls: Pediculosis Capitis
tirbanibulin|This ointment is used once a day for 5 days in a row on the treated area of the face or scalp, one packet each time. Hands are washed right after, and the area is not washed or touched for about 8 hours. Redness, flaking and crusting are expected for about a week, and it is kept away from the eyes and lips.|The treatment field is limited to 100 cm2 per label; local skin reactions (erythema, flaking, crusting, swelling, erosion) peak around day 8 and are typically graded at follow-up; periocular transfer can cause eye irritation; severe ulceration or signs of infection prompt notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tirbanibulin;;NCBI StatPearls: Actinic Keratosis
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
berdazimer|This gel comes as two tubes that are mixed in equal amounts on the dosing guide right before each use and spread thinly on each bump once a day for up to 12 weeks. The gel is left to dry for 10 minutes, and swimming, bathing or washing waits for 1 hour. Burning, redness or itching at the bumps is common; severe swelling or blistering is reported to the prescriber.|Lesion locations are reviewed before treatment, with the eyes, mouth and mucous membranes excluded; equal amounts from Tube A and Tube B are mixed only at the time of use and not stored; hands are washed afterward unless they are treated; application-site pain, erythema and swelling are expected, while blistering or allergic contact dermatitis typically prompts provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Berdazimer;;NCBI StatPearls: Molluscum Contagiosum
trifarotene|This cream is applied as a thin layer once a day in the evening on clean, dry skin, usually one pump for the face and two for the chest and back. Dryness, redness and stinging are common in the first weeks, and a moisturizer helps. Sunscreen and limited sun exposure lower the risk of sunburn, and waxing treated skin is best skipped.|Skin is reviewed for eczema, sunburn or broken areas before application; pump-based dosing per label limits overuse; irritation is most common in the first 4 weeks and moisturizer is recommended from the start per label; pregnancy plans are discussed because retinoids as a class carry fetal risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Trifarotene;;NCBI StatPearls: Retinoids
clindamycin-benzoyl-peroxide|This acne gel is applied to clean, dry skin as directed, usually once or twice a day depending on the brand. It can bleach hair, towels and clothing. Dryness and peeling are common; severe diarrhea, stomach cramps or bloody stools are reported to the prescriber right away.|History of inflammatory bowel disease or antibiotic-associated colitis is reviewed because the label lists these as contraindications; once-daily brands (Duac, Acanya, Onexton) differ from the twice-daily BenzaClin type; topical erythromycin is antagonistic with clindamycin; diarrhea during or after use typically prompts provider notification for possible C. difficile infection|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Clindamycin and Benzoyl Peroxide;;NCBI StatPearls: Clindamycin;;NCBI StatPearls: Benzoyl Peroxide
adapalene-benzoyl-peroxide|This acne gel is applied once a day after washing, using a pea-sized amount for each area of the face. It can bleach hair and fabric, so hands are washed afterward. Dryness, redness and stinging are common at first; swelling of the face or trouble breathing is an emergency.|Skin is reviewed for sunburn, eczema or broken areas before application; the 0.3% Forte strength is labeled from 12 years and the 0.1% strength from 9 years; irritation commonly peaks in the first weeks; signs of hypersensitivity such as facial swelling or throat tightness typically prompt stopping and urgent evaluation|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Adapalene and Benzoyl Peroxide;;NCBI StatPearls: Adapalene;;NCBI StatPearls: Benzoyl Peroxide
clindamycin-tretinoin|This acne gel is used once a day at bedtime: a pea-sized amount is dotted on the chin, cheeks, nose and forehead and rubbed over the face. Sunscreen and limited sun exposure help prevent sunburn. Severe diarrhea or bloody stools are reported to the prescriber right away.|History of regional enteritis, ulcerative colitis or antibiotic-associated colitis is reviewed because these are label contraindications; the gel is kept away from the eyes, mouth and angles of the nose; sun sensitivity is expected from tretinoin; diarrhea typically prompts provider notification for possible C. difficile infection|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Clindamycin Phosphate and Tretinoin;;NCBI StatPearls: Clindamycin;;NCBI StatPearls: Tretinoin
minocycline-topical-foam|After shaking the can, a cherry-sized amount of foam is rubbed into the acne areas once a day, at least 1 hour before bedtime. The foam is flammable, so fire, flames and smoking are kept away during and right after use. Bathing, showering or swimming waits at least 1 hour, and headache with blurred vision or a rash is reported to the prescriber.|Tetracycline allergy is reviewed before use; the flammable propellant is a burn risk near flame or smoking; pregnancy and age under 9 are relevant because of class tooth and bone effects; headache with visual change can reflect intracranial hypertension and typically prompts provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Minocycline;;NCBI StatPearls: Minocycline
spesolimab|This medicine is given as a 90-minute IV infusion for a flare of generalized pustular psoriasis, or as shots under the skin to help prevent flares. Fever, cough, burning with urination or other signs of infection are reported to the care team. Live vaccines are not given during treatment, so vaccines are reviewed before starting.|TB screening and infection review are completed before starting per label; the IV dose is diluted in 0.9% sodium chloride and infused over 90 minutes through a 0.2 micron in-line filter; infusion reactions, rash or signs of DRESS typically prompt stopping the infusion and notifying the provider; live vaccines are avoided during therapy|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Spesolimab;;NCBI StatPearls: Generalized Pustular Psoriasis
halobetasol|This very strong steroid is applied as a thin layer twice a day for no more than 2 weeks and not more than 50 grams a week. It is not used on the face, groin or armpits, and the area is not covered with bandages unless directed. Skin thinning, stretch marks or a rash that worsens are reported to the prescriber.|Treated body surface area and duration are reviewed because the label limits use to 2 weeks and 50 g per week; occlusion and use on the face, groin or axillae are avoided per label; children and large-area use carry higher HPA axis suppression risk; worsening or infected skin typically prompts provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Halobetasol Propionate;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Corticosteroids
desoximetasone|This strong steroid is applied as a thin film to the rash twice a day and rubbed in gently. It is not used on the face, groin or under diapers unless the prescriber directs it. Skin thinning, stretch marks or a rash that gets worse are reported to the prescriber.|Product strength is reviewed because 0.25% ointment is super-high potency; children are more susceptible to HPA axis suppression and the smallest effective amount is used per label; diapers and occlusion increase absorption; signs of skin infection or atrophy typically prompt provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Desoximetasone;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Corticosteroids
fluocinolone-topical|This steroid is applied as a thin film to the rash 2 to 4 times a day, depending on the product and how severe the rash is. In hairy areas, the hair is parted so the medicine reaches the skin. A worsening rash, signs of infection or skin thinning are reported to the prescriber; the body oil contains peanut oil.|The specific product and strength are reviewed because fluocinolone ranges from medium potency cream to low potency oil and is easily confused with the stronger fluocinonide; peanut allergy is relevant for Derma-Smoothe/FS; occlusion is discontinued if infection develops per label; children have higher HPA axis suppression risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fluocinolone Acetonide;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Corticosteroids
`);

