DB.addExt(`
betamethasone-valerate-topical|This steroid cream, ointment or lotion is spread in a thin layer on the rash, usually once or twice a day, and only on the areas the prescriber named. Bandaging or wrapping the area increases how much is absorbed. Thinning skin, stretch marks, signs of infection, or use near the eyes are reported to the prescriber, and using it for a long time on the face or groin is linked to skin damage.|Skin at the site is typically reviewed for atrophy, striae, telangiectasia and infection; the amount, area and duration of use are reviewed because large areas, occlusion and children raise HPA axis suppression risk; glucose is relevant in patients with diabetes using extensive amounts; ointment is more potent than cream or lotion of the same strength, and betamethasone valerate is commonly confused with the higher-potency dipropionate|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Betamethasone Valerate;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Corticosteroids
calcipotriene-betamethasone|This psoriasis medicine is applied once a day to the plaques for up to 4 weeks, rubbed in gently, and hands are washed afterward. It is kept off the face, groin and armpits and is not covered with a bandage unless the prescriber says so. Adults stay under 100 grams a week; confusion, weakness, constipation or heavy urination can be signs of high calcium and are reported to the prescriber.|The weekly amount used and body surface area treated are typically reviewed (label maximum 100 g per week in adults and 60 g per week ages 12 to 17, not over 30% of body surface area); serum calcium is reviewed when hypercalcemia is suspected and the product is stopped until calcium normalizes per label; skin atrophy and use on the face, groin or axillae are noted; other corticosteroids and calcium or vitamin D supplements add risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Calcipotriene and Betamethasone Dipropionate;;NCBI StatPearls: Psoriasis;;American Academy of Dermatology-National Psoriasis Foundation Guidelines of Care for the Management of Psoriasis with Topical Therapy (2021)
doxycycline-low-dose-rosacea|This capsule is taken once each morning on an empty stomach, at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals, with a full glass of water and while sitting or standing. It treats the bumps of rosacea, not an infection, and taking more does not work better. Sunburn, a bad headache with blurry vision, painful swallowing, severe diarrhea, or pregnancy are reported to the prescriber.|Allergy to tetracyclines, pregnancy and age are typically reviewed before starting because tetracyclines can discolor developing teeth; separation from antacids, iron, calcium and magnesium products is part of administration; headache with visual change is reported as possible intracranial hypertension, especially with isotretinoin; INR is followed in patients on warfarin; the 40 mg rosacea capsule is not interchangeable with antibacterial doxycycline doses|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Doxycycline (Oracea);;NCBI StatPearls: Doxycycline;;NCBI StatPearls: Rosacea
eflornithine-cream|This cream is rubbed in a thin layer on the face and under the chin twice a day, at least 8 hours apart, and the area is not washed for 4 hours. It slows hair growth but does not remove hair, so shaving, plucking or other methods continue, with the cream applied at least 5 minutes after hair removal. Results take 4 to 8 weeks, and stinging, burning, rash or acne are reported if bothersome.|Skin at the site is typically reviewed for irritation, rash and acne; the cream is applied at least 5 minutes after hair removal and the area is left unwashed for 4 hours per label; response is commonly reassessed at about 6 months, when lack of improvement leads to stopping per label; underlying causes of hirsutism such as PCOS are part of the broader evaluation|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Eflornithine Hydrochloride (Vaniqa);;NCBI StatPearls: Hirsutism
bimatoprost-eyelash|This liquid is brushed along the base of the upper eyelashes once each night with a fresh sterile applicator for each eye, after removing makeup and contact lenses. Extra applications do not make lashes grow faster, and lashes return to their old look after stopping. It can darken the eyelid skin or the colored part of the eye, and eye redness, pain, vision change or use of glaucoma eye drops are reported to the eye doctor.|Any glaucoma prostaglandin drop use is typically reviewed because the combination can blunt IOP lowering per label; eye redness, itching, eyelid darkening and iris color change are noted; one sterile applicator per eye and application only to the upper lid margin are part of label technique; contact lenses are removed and reinserted 15 minutes later|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Bimatoprost (Latisse);;NCBI StatPearls: Bimatoprost
travoprost|One drop goes in the affected eye once each evening; using it more often can make it work less well. Contact lenses come out before the drop and go back in after 15 minutes, and other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart. The colored part of the eye can slowly turn browner and eyelashes can grow; eye pain, redness or vision change are reported to the eye doctor.|Drop technique and once-evening timing are typically reviewed because more frequent dosing can lessen the IOP effect per label; iris and eyelid pigmentation and eyelash changes are discussed before starting; eye pain, swelling or vision change can reflect inflammation or macular edema and are reported; other eye drops are separated by at least 5 minutes|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Travoprost (Travatan Z);;American Academy of Ophthalmology Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Preferred Practice Pattern (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Open-Angle Glaucoma
tafluprost|One drop from a fresh single-use container goes in the affected eye once each evening, and the opened container is thrown away after use. This drop has no preservative, which can help people with dry or sensitive eyes. The colored part of the eye can slowly darken and eyelashes can grow; eye pain, redness or vision change are reported to the eye doctor.|Single-dose containers are used once and discarded per label; once-evening timing is reviewed because more frequent dosing can lessen the IOP effect; iris pigmentation and eyelash changes are discussed before starting; other eye drops are separated by at least 5 minutes; eye pain or vision change are reported as possible inflammation or macular edema|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tafluprost (Zioptan);;American Academy of Ophthalmology Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Preferred Practice Pattern (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Open-Angle Glaucoma
latanoprostene-bunod|One drop goes in the affected eye once each evening; using it more often can make it work less well. Contact lenses come out before the drop and go back in after 15 minutes, and other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart. The colored part of the eye can slowly darken and eyelashes can grow; eye pain, redness or vision change are reported to the eye doctor.|Once-evening timing is typically reviewed because more frequent dosing can lessen the IOP effect per label; contact lens removal is part of administration because the product contains benzalkonium chloride; iris pigmentation and eyelash changes are discussed before starting; eye pain or vision change are reported as possible inflammation or macular edema|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Latanoprostene Bunod (Vyzulta);;American Academy of Ophthalmology Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Preferred Practice Pattern (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Open-Angle Glaucoma
netarsudil|One drop goes in the affected eye once each evening; if a dose is missed, the next evening dose is taken as usual. Red eyes are very common with this drop and small marks can form on the clear front of the eye, which the eye doctor checks. Contact lenses come out before the drop and go back in after 15 minutes, and eye pain or vision change are reported.|Conjunctival hyperemia is typically discussed before starting because it affects about half of patients; corneal verticillata is followed on slit-lamp exams; once-evening dosing is reviewed because twice-daily dosing is poorly tolerated per label; contact lens removal is part of administration because of benzalkonium chloride; other eye drops are separated by at least 5 minutes|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Netarsudil (Rhopressa);;American Academy of Ophthalmology Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Preferred Practice Pattern (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Open-Angle Glaucoma
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
brinzolamide|This milky eye drop is shaken well and used in the affected eye three times a day to lower eye pressure. Vision is often blurry for a short time after the drop, and a bitter taste can happen. Soft contact lenses go back in about 15 minutes after the dose, and a rash or eye swelling is worth reporting, especially with a sulfa allergy.|Sulfonamide allergy history is reviewed, since brinzolamide is a sulfonamide and systemic sulfonamide reactions can occur per label; severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min) is not studied and not recommended per label; oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide are not combined with it per label; the suspension is shaken well before each dose, and other eye drops are spaced at least 10 minutes apart per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Brinzolamide;;NCBI StatPearls: Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors;;American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma (2020)
apraclonidine|This eye drop is usually given by the eye team, one drop before and one drop after a laser eye procedure, to keep eye pressure from spiking. The eye can look whiter and the eyelid a little higher for a while. Telling the team about any MAO inhibitor medicine or a clonidine allergy matters, and feeling faint or very sleepy is worth reporting.|MAO inhibitor therapy and clonidine hypersensitivity are labeled contraindications and are reviewed before dosing; pulse and blood pressure are relevant in cardiovascular disease because the drug can lower both; each single-use 1% container is used for one dose and discarded per label; ocular redness, itching and lid swelling with repeated use suggest an allergic-like reaction|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Apraclonidine;;NCBI StatPearls: Apraclonidine;;American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma (2020)
pilocarpine-ophthalmic|This eye drop makes the pupil small and helps fluid drain from the eye. Brow ache and dim or blurry vision, especially at night, are common at first, so night driving can be harder. Sudden flashes of light, new floaters or a curtain over vision are an emergency, and pressing on the inner corner of the eye for 2 minutes after the drop limits absorption.|Pilocarpine-naive patients typically start at the 1% strength per label because higher strengths are poorly tolerated at first; retinal detachment risk factors and night driving needs are reviewed; punctal occlusion for 2 minutes after dosing limits systemic exposure per label; systemic cholinergic signs such as sweating, salivation, bradycardia or wheezing suggest absorption; other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pilocarpine Hydrochloride Ophthalmic Solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Pilocarpine;;American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Primary Angle Closure Disease (2020)
cyclopentolate|These drops widen the pupil and relax focusing for an eye exam, so vision is blurry and bright light is uncomfortable for up to a day. Sunglasses help, and children are watched after the drops. A child who becomes confused, flushed, unsteady or has a fast heartbeat after the drops is a reason to seek care right away.|Infants receive a single drop of 0.5% with nasolacrimal pressure for 2 to 3 minutes and are observed for at least 30 minutes per label; CNS disturbances such as ataxia, hallucinations or incoherent speech are more common in children and with stronger solutions; narrow angles carry a risk of angle closure after dilation; tachycardia, flushing and fever suggest systemic anticholinergic effects; cyclopentolate and cyclosporine eye drops are a look-alike pair|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Cyclopentolate Hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Cyclopentolate
fluorescein|This yellow dye is injected into a vein for a special photo of the back of the eye, or put on the eye surface to look for scratches. Skin can look yellow for up to 12 hours and urine bright yellow for up to 36 hours, which is expected. Nausea can happen in the first few minutes, and itching, trouble breathing or pain at the IV site is reported right away.|Allergy and asthma history are reviewed because respiratory and anaphylactic reactions can occur and these patients are monitored closely per label; IV patency is confirmed because the high-pH solution can cause severe tissue damage with extravasation per label; the adult dose is 500 mg IV and children receive 7.7 mg/kg up to 500 mg per label; nausea and vomiting in the first minutes are common; fluorescence can interfere with some lab tests for up to 36 hours|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fluorescein;;NCBI StatPearls: Fluorescein Angiography
dexamethasone-ophthalmic|This steroid eye drop is shaken well and used as directed, often more often at first and then tapered down. Stopping suddenly can bring the inflammation back. Eye pain, worsening redness, discharge or vision changes are worth reporting, and longer use needs eye pressure checks by the eye doctor.|Acute untreated bacterial, viral (including epithelial herpes simplex), mycobacterial and fungal eye infections are labeled contraindications; intraocular pressure is typically reviewed with use beyond 10 days because of steroid-induced glaucoma; prescriptions beyond one bottle are renewed only after further evaluation per label; the suspension is shaken well before use; the dose is tapered rather than stopped abruptly in significant inflammation|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dexamethasone Ophthalmic Suspension;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Ophthalmic Corticosteroids
loteprednol|This steroid eye drop is shaken hard before each use, usually four times a day. It is designed to cause fewer eye pressure problems than older steroid drops, but checkups are still part of longer use. Eye pain, worsening redness, discharge or vision changes are worth reporting, and soft contact lenses are not worn during treatment.|Viral, mycobacterial and fungal eye infections and hypersensitivity to corticosteroids are labeled contraindications; re-evaluation is typical if signs fail to improve after two days per label; intraocular pressure is reviewed with use beyond 10 days; the suspension is shaken vigorously before use per label; therapy is not stopped prematurely per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Loteprednol Etabonate;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Ophthalmic Corticosteroids
difluprednate|This strong steroid eye drop is usually used four times a day after eye surgery and then tapered down on a schedule. Eye pressure checks are part of treatment because this steroid commonly raises pressure. Eye pain, worsening redness, discharge or vision changes are worth reporting, and contact lenses are not worn during treatment.|Active viral, mycobacterial and fungal eye infections are labeled contraindications; intraocular pressure rises are common with this potent steroid and IOP is reviewed with use beyond 10 days; the initial prescription and renewals beyond one bottle follow slit-lamp examination per label; the taper follows the labeled schedule rather than abrupt discontinuation|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Difluprednate;;NCBI StatPearls: Topical Ophthalmic Corticosteroids
ketorolac-ophthalmic|This anti-inflammatory eye drop is usually used four times a day, for itchy allergy eyes or for 2 weeks after cataract surgery. A brief sting is common. People with an aspirin or NSAID allergy tell their eye doctor, and eye pain, worsening redness or blurred vision during use is worth reporting.|Aspirin and NSAID sensitivity is reviewed because of cross-sensitivity; corneal epithelial breakdown, keratitis or melting is associated with prolonged use, diabetes, dry eye and complicated surgery; drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart per label; bleeding tendency and anticoagulants are relevant around surgery; Acular strengths (0.5%, LS 0.4%, Acuvail 0.45%) are not interchangeable|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ketorolac Tromethamine Ophthalmic Solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Ketorolac
bromfenac|This anti-inflammatory eye drop is used once a day, starting the day before cataract surgery and continuing for 2 weeks after. It contains a sulfite, so people with a sulfite allergy tell their eye doctor. Eye pain, worsening redness or blurred vision during use is worth reporting, and contact lenses are not worn during treatment.|Sulfite allergy history is reviewed because the product contains sodium sulfite per label; aspirin and NSAID sensitivity is reviewed because of cross-sensitivity; keratitis and corneal melting are associated with diabetes, dry eye, complicated surgery and prolonged use; drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart per label; bleeding tendency is relevant around surgery|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Bromfenac;;NCBI StatPearls: Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
nepafenac|This anti-inflammatory eye drop is used once a day starting the day before cataract surgery and for 2 weeks after, with an extra drop before surgery. The bottle is shaken first, other eye drops are spaced 5 minutes apart, and contact lenses stay out. Increasing eye pain, worsening redness or blurred vision is reported to the eye surgeon.|The drug and strength are confirmed, since Ilevro 0.3% is once daily and Nevanac 0.1% is three times daily; NSAID or aspirin allergy is reviewed before the first dose; other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart per label; increasing pain, corneal haze or vision loss typically prompts notifying the eye surgeon; anticoagulant use is noted because ocular bleeding can increase|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Nepafenac;;NCBI StatPearls: Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs
gatifloxacin-ophthalmic|This antibiotic eye drop is used often on the first day, every 2 hours while awake up to 8 times, then 2 to 4 times a day for the rest of the week. Contact lenses stay out during the infection, and the bottle tip is not touched to the eye. Eye pain, light sensitivity or worse vision is reported to the prescriber.|Quinolone allergy is reviewed before the first dose; the Day 1 schedule is more frequent than Days 2 through 7 per label; hand hygiene and bottle tip care lower spread of infection; worsening pain, light sensitivity or vision loss typically prompts notifying the provider because keratitis can mimic conjunctivitis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Gatifloxacin;;NCBI StatPearls: Bacterial Conjunctivitis
ofloxacin-ophthalmic|This antibiotic eye drop is used on a set schedule; for a corneal ulcer it is used very often, including overnight doses in the first 2 days. Contact lenses stay out, and the dropper tip is not touched to the eye. Rash, swelling, worse eye pain or vision changes are reported right away.|Quinolone allergy is reviewed before the first dose; the corneal ulcer schedule includes doses every 30 minutes while awake and overnight doses on Days 1 and 2 per label; the product is for topical eye use only and is not injected; rash, hives or facial swelling typically prompts holding and notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ofloxacin;;NCBI StatPearls: Bacterial Keratitis;;NCBI StatPearls: Bacterial Conjunctivitis
polymyxin-b-trimethoprim|This antibiotic eye drop is placed in the affected eye every 3 hours, up to 6 times a day, for 7 to 10 days. The bottle tip is not touched to the eye, and hands are washed before and after. Eyelid swelling, itching, rash or worse redness is reported to the prescriber.|Allergy history is reviewed before the first dose; the label limits use to children 2 months and older and adults, and it is not indicated for ophthalmia neonatorum; eyelid swelling, itching or rash suggests hypersensitivity and typically prompts holding and notifying the provider; marked lid swelling, fever or painful eye movement raises concern for periorbital or orbital cellulitis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Polymyxin B and Trimethoprim;;NCBI StatPearls: Bacterial Conjunctivitis
lifitegrast|This dry eye drop is used twice a day, about 12 hours apart, one drop in each eye from a single-use container that is thrown away after use. Contact lenses are removed first and can go back in after 15 minutes. A strange taste and brief eye irritation are common; swelling of the face, tongue or eyes is reported right away.|The single-use container is discarded after dosing both eyes; contact lenses are removed before dosing and reinserted after 15 minutes per label; dysgeusia and instillation-site irritation are expected effects; tongue or facial swelling suggests hypersensitivity and typically prompts notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lifitegrast;;NCBI StatPearls: Dry Eye Syndrome
perfluorohexyloctane|This dry eye drop is used 4 times a day. It contains no water, so the bottle is held upside down and squeezed in a special way to release a small drop. Contact lenses are removed before use and stay out for at least 30 minutes; blurred vision or eye redness can happen.|The bottle technique differs from standard drops, so the label instructions are reviewed with the patient; contact lenses are kept out for at least 30 minutes after dosing per label; blurred vision and conjunctival redness are the most common effects; hypersensitivity reactions typically prompt notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Perfluorohexyloctane;;NCBI StatPearls: Dry Eye Syndrome
varenicline-nasal|This nose spray treats dry eye by triggering natural tears: one spray in each nostril twice a day, about 12 hours apart. Sneezing right after the dose is very common. A missed dose is skipped, and the next one is used at the usual time.|The spray is primed with 7 sprays before first use and re-primed with 1 spray after more than 5 days of nonuse per label; sneezing, cough and throat or nasal irritation are common; the 0.03 mg nasal dose is far below oral varenicline doses for smoking cessation; nosebleeds or persistent nasal irritation are reported to the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Varenicline (Tyrvaya);;NCBI StatPearls: Dry Eye Syndrome
faricimab|This medicine is given as an injection into the eye by an eye doctor, monthly at first and then less often. The eye is numbed and cleaned before the shot. Eye pain, increasing redness, light sensitivity, new floaters or vision loss in the days after an injection are reported to the eye doctor right away.|Ocular infection or active intraocular inflammation are contraindications per label; intraocular pressure and optic nerve perfusion are reviewed after injection; pain, redness, photophobia or decreased vision after injection raises concern for endophthalmitis or retinal vasculitis and typically prompts urgent provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Faricimab;;NCBI StatPearls: Age-Related Macular Degeneration;;American Academy of Ophthalmology. Age-Related Macular Degeneration Preferred Practice Pattern
brolucizumab|This medicine is given as an injection into the eye by an eye doctor, monthly for the first 3 doses and then every 8 to 12 weeks. Eye pain, redness, new floaters, light sensitivity or any drop in vision after an injection is reported to the eye doctor right away, because a rare inflammation of retinal blood vessels can threaten vision.|Ocular infection or active intraocular inflammation are contraindications per label; intraocular pressure is reviewed after injection; new floaters, pain or vision loss raises concern for retinal vasculitis or occlusion and typically prompts urgent provider notification; prior intraocular inflammation is reviewed before each dose|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Brolucizumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Age-Related Macular Degeneration
pegcetacoplan|This medicine is given as an injection into the eye by an eye doctor every 1 to 2 months to slow geographic atrophy; it does not bring back lost vision. Eye pain, redness, new floaters, light sensitivity or sudden vision change after an injection is reported right away. Regular eye exams also look for the wet form of macular degeneration.|Ocular infection or active intraocular inflammation are contraindications per label; only 0.1 mL (15 mg) is delivered per dose per label; intraocular pressure is reviewed after injection; pain, redness or vision loss raises concern for endophthalmitis or retinal vasculitis and typically prompts urgent provider notification; the subcutaneous product Empaveli is a different dose and indication|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pegcetacoplan;;NCBI StatPearls: Age-Related Macular Degeneration
`);


;
DB.addExt(`
avacincaptad-pegol|This medicine is given as a shot into the eye at the retina clinic, usually once a month, to slow the dry form of macular degeneration. The eye may feel scratchy or look red at the injection spot for a few days. Increasing eye pain, redness, sensitivity to light or a sudden drop in vision after a shot is reported to the eye doctor right away.|Eye or eyelid infection and active intraocular inflammation are labeled contraindications and typically lead to postponing the injection; intraocular pressure and optic nerve perfusion are reviewed after injection; new pain, redness, photophobia or vision loss after injection is associated with endophthalmitis or retinal detachment; new wet AMD changes in the treated eye are a labeled risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Avacincaptad pegol;;Astellas product information: Izervay
verteporfin|This medicine is given by IV over 10 minutes, and then a special laser is aimed at the eye. For 5 days afterward the skin and eyes are very sensitive to sunlight and bright indoor lights, so covering skin and wearing dark glasses outdoors help, and sunscreen does not protect. Normal indoor light is fine and helps clear the medicine from the skin.|Porphyria is a labeled contraindication, and liver disease is reviewed before treatment; the dose is diluted only in 5% dextrose because saline causes precipitation; extravasation causes severe pain and photosensitivity at the site, so the IV is typically a free-flowing large arm vein and the infusion is stopped per label if leakage occurs; light protection for 5 days and a wristband reminder are standard per label; back pain during the infusion is a known reaction|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Verteporfin;;Bausch + Lomb product information: Visudyne
lotilaner-ophthalmic|This eye drop is used as 1 drop in each eye twice a day, about 12 hours apart, for 6 weeks to clear eyelash mites. Contact lenses come out before the drop and go back in at least 15 minutes later. Mild stinging or burning is common and usually brief.|Collarettes at the lash base and eyelid redness are the findings followed during treatment; contact lenses are removed before instillation and reinserted at least 15 minutes after per label; other eye drops are separated by at least 5 minutes; the dropper tip is kept from touching the eye to prevent contamination|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lotilaner;;Tarsus Pharmaceuticals product information: Xdemvy
ciprofloxacin-ophthalmic|This antibiotic eye drop is used exactly on the schedule given, which can be very frequent at first for a corneal ulcer. A white crust or spot on the eye can come from the medicine itself. Worsening pain, more redness or changes in vision are reported to the eye doctor right away, and contact lenses are usually left out during treatment.|Quinolone allergy is reviewed before the first dose; corneal ulcer dosing starts at 2 drops every 15 minutes for 6 hours per label, so dosing times are tracked closely; a white crystalline corneal precipitate is a known effect and does not preclude continued use per label; worsening pain, a larger infiltrate or vision loss typically prompt provider notification; the ophthalmic and otic ciprofloxacin products are distinct|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ciprofloxacin;;NCBI StatPearls: Ciprofloxacin;;NCBI StatPearls: Bacterial Conjunctivitis
tobramycin-dexamethasone-ophthalmic|This eye drop has an antibiotic and a steroid, and the bottle is shaken well before each use. It is used only as long as the eye doctor directs, because long use can raise eye pressure and cause cataracts. Eye pain, blurred vision, halos around lights or a worsening red eye are reported right away.|History of herpes simplex eye disease, fungal infection and glaucoma is reviewed before use; intraocular pressure is typically checked with use of 10 days or longer per label; the suspension is shaken before each dose; not more than 20 mL is prescribed initially without further evaluation per label; worsening pain or a dendritic corneal lesion typically prompts provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tobramycin and dexamethasone;;NCBI StatPearls: Tobramycin;;NCBI StatPearls: Dexamethasone
neomycin-polymyxin-dexamethasone|This eye drop has two antibiotics and a steroid, and the bottle is shaken well before each use. Itching, redness or swelling of the eyelids can be an allergy to neomycin and is reported to the eye doctor. Long use can raise eye pressure, so it is used only as long as directed.|Neomycin or aminoglycoside allergy, herpes simplex eye disease and glaucoma are reviewed before use; intraocular pressure is typically checked with use of 10 days or longer per label; new eyelid itching, redness or rash is associated with neomycin sensitization; not more than 20 mL is prescribed initially without further evaluation per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Neomycin, polymyxin B and dexamethasone;;NCBI StatPearls: Neomycin;;NCBI StatPearls: Polymyxin B
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
methazolamide|This tablet lowers eye pressure and is usually taken two or three times a day. Tingling in the fingers or toes, tiredness and a changed taste are common. A rash, sore throat, unusual bruising, kidney stone pain or confusion are reported to the prescriber promptly, and people with a sulfa allergy tell their care team before starting.|Sulfonamide allergy history is reviewed before the first dose; sodium, potassium and bicarbonate are reviewed at baseline and periodically, and low sodium or potassium is a labeled contraindication; rash, sore throat, fever or bruising commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider because of rare fatal sulfonamide reactions; high-dose aspirin is a known dangerous combination; methazolamide and methimazole are a sound-alike pair|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Methazolamide;;NCBI StatPearls: Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors
ganciclovir-ophthalmic|This antiviral eye gel treats a herpes infection on the front of the eye. It is used 5 times a day until the sore heals and then 3 times a day for 7 more days. Contact lenses are not worn while the eye is infected, and blurred vision for a short time after each dose is common.|Fluorescein staining and visual acuity are typically reviewed by the eye clinician to judge ulcer healing and the switch to the 3 times daily phase; topical steroid drops are a recognized hazard in active epithelial herpes keratitis; the tube tip is kept from touching the eye; contact lenses are removed during treatment|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel (Zirgan);;NCBI StatPearls: Herpes Simplex Keratitis
trifluridine-ophthalmic|These antiviral eye drops treat a herpes infection of the cornea. They are used often, up to 9 times a day at first, and then less often for a week after healing. Mild burning is common, and the bottle is kept in the refrigerator; treatment is not continued past 21 days without the eye doctor's review.|Dosing frequency is reviewed carefully, with a maximum of 9 drops per day and continuous use beyond 21 days avoided per label; corneal healing is typically reassessed at 7 and 14 days; topical steroid use in active epithelial disease is a recognized hazard; the bottle is stored refrigerated; trifluridine eye drops are distinct from oral trifluridine-tipiracil chemotherapy|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Trifluridine ophthalmic solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Herpes Simplex Keratitis
natamycin|This antifungal eye drop treats fungal infections of the eye surface. The bottle is shaken well, and drops are used very often at first, about every 1 to 2 hours, then less often after a few days for 2 to 3 weeks. Worsening pain, more redness or less vision is reported to the eye doctor right away.|Corneal scrapings for smear and culture are typically obtained before therapy; the suspension is shaken well before each dose; frequent initial dosing is tapered per label after 3 to 4 days; lack of improvement after 7 to 10 days is associated with resistant or deep infection and is reported to the provider; topical steroids are a recognized hazard in fungal keratitis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Natamycin ophthalmic suspension (Natacyn);;NCBI StatPearls: Fungal Keratitis
dexamethasone-intravitreal-implant|This is a tiny steroid implant placed in the eye with a needle in the eye doctor's office, and it slowly dissolves over months. Small floating spots and mild redness are common afterward. Eye pain, worsening redness, light sensitivity or vision loss in the days after the injection is an emergency because it can mean infection.|Ocular or periocular infection, advanced glaucoma and a torn posterior lens capsule are labeled contraindications reviewed before injection; the injection is done under controlled aseptic conditions; intraocular pressure and signs of endophthalmitis are monitored after injection per label; cataract and steroid-induced pressure rise are expected class risks discussed with patients|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dexamethasone intravitreal implant (Ozurdex);;American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Diabetic Retinopathy;;NCBI StatPearls: Intravitreal Injections
fluocinolone-intravitreal-implant|This is a tiny steroid implant injected into the eye that releases medicine for about 3 years and does not dissolve. Eye pressure checks continue for years because pressure can rise, and cataracts are common. Eye pain, worsening redness or sudden vision loss after the injection is an emergency.|Ocular infection and glaucoma with cup to disc ratio above 0.8 are labeled contraindications; for DME, a prior steroid course without a significant pressure rise is part of the indication; intraocular pressure and signs of endophthalmitis are monitored after injection and long term; Iluvien, Yutiq and Retisert differ in strength and indication and are a known confusion risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fluocinolone acetonide intravitreal implant (Iluvien);;NCBI StatPearls: Intravitreal Injections
cenegermin|These eye drops contain nerve growth factor to help a numb, damaged cornea heal. One drop goes in the affected eye 6 times a day, every 2 hours, for 8 weeks, using a new vial each day. Some eye pain is common while nerves regrow, and contact lenses are taken out before each dose.|The dosing schedule of 6 times daily at 2-hour intervals for 8 weeks is reviewed with the patient; one vial is used per day and discarded at day end; other eye drops are separated by at least 15 minutes; corneal staining and defect size are typically followed by the ophthalmologist; storage logistics (frozen at the pharmacy, refrigerated at home) are part of therapy|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Cenegermin-bkbj (Oxervate);;NCBI StatPearls: Neurotrophic Keratitis
oxymetazoline-ophthalmic|This eye drop lifts a droopy upper eyelid for part of the day and is used once daily. Each single-use container is thrown away after the dose, and contact lenses are taken out and put back 15 minutes later. Sudden eyelid droop, double vision, eye pain or halos around lights are reported to a clinician promptly.|New or sudden ptosis is evaluated for neurologic causes such as myasthenia gravis, third nerve palsy or Horner syndrome per label; blood pressure is relevant in cardiovascular disease; narrow angles carry a risk of angle closure; MAO inhibitors and antihypertensives are noted interactions; the eye product is distinct from oxymetazoline nasal spray|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oxymetazoline hydrochloride ophthalmic solution (Upneeq);;NCBI StatPearls: Ptosis
acoltremon|This eye drop for dry eye is used in each eye twice a day, about 12 hours apart. A stinging or burning feeling right after the drop is very common and usually passes. Contact lenses are taken out before the dose and put back after 15 minutes, and other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart.|Instillation site pain is the most common reaction (about half of patients) and is discussed before starting; one single-dose vial treats both eyes and is discarded after use; other eye drops are separated by at least 5 minutes per label; a missed dose is skipped and dosing resumes at the next scheduled time per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Acoltremon ophthalmic solution (Tryptyr);;NCBI StatPearls: Dry Eye Disease
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
lidocaine-ophthalmic-gel|This numbing gel is placed on the eye by the eye care team just before a procedure, so the eye does not feel pain. The eye stays numb for a short time afterward, so rubbing the eye is avoided until feeling returns. Eye pain, worsening redness or vision changes in the following days are reported to the eye doctor.|Allergy to amide local anesthetics is reviewed before application; 2 drops are applied to the area of the planned procedure and can be reapplied to maintain effect per label; the numb eye cannot sense dust or trauma, so protection of the eye is part of post-procedure teaching; the product is for topical ophthalmic use by a healthcare provider and is not for injection per label; repeated topical anesthetic use is associated with corneal epithelial injury|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lidocaine hydrochloride ophthalmic gel;;NCBI StatPearls: Lidocaine
phenylephrine-ketorolac-intraocular|This medicine is added to the eye rinse fluid used during cataract or lens surgery to keep the pupil open and ease pain afterward. It is given only in the operating room. Allergy to aspirin or other pain relievers like ibuprofen is shared with the surgical team, and eye pain, redness or vision loss after surgery is reported right away.|NSAID or aspirin allergy and MAO inhibitor use are reviewed before surgery; per label 4 mL is diluted in 500 mL of ocular irrigating solution and is not used undiluted; diluted solution is stored no more than 4 hours at room temperature or 24 hours refrigerated per label; blood pressure is observed in patients with hypertension because phenylephrine can raise it|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Phenylephrine and ketorolac intraocular solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Phenylephrine
timolol-dorzolamide|This eye drop is used twice a day to lower eye pressure and protect vision, even when the eyes feel fine. Pressing gently on the inner corner of the eye for a minute after the drop can lower how much reaches the body, and a bitter taste is common. Wheezing, a very slow pulse, fainting or a rash are reported to the prescriber right away.|Heart rate and asthma or COPD history are reviewed before use, and bradycardia or wheezing commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; sulfa allergy is relevant because dorzolamide is a sulfonamide; other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart per label; contact lenses are removed before dosing; Cosopt, Combigan and Simbrinza are look-alike glaucoma combinations|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dorzolamide and timolol ophthalmic solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Timolol;;NCBI StatPearls: Dorzolamide
brimonidine-timolol|This eye drop is used twice a day, about 12 hours apart, to lower eye pressure. It can cause tiredness, dry mouth or eye redness and itching. Wheezing, a very slow pulse or fainting are reported right away, and the bottle is stored out of reach of young children because swallowing it can be dangerous.|Heart rate, blood pressure and asthma or COPD history are reviewed before use, and bradycardia, hypotension or wheezing commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; the product is contraindicated in children younger than 2 years per label; allergic conjunctivitis is a common reason for discontinuation; other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart per label; MAO inhibitor use is reviewed because of brimonidine|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Brimonidine and timolol ophthalmic solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Brimonidine;;NCBI StatPearls: Timolol
brinzolamide-brimonidine|This milky eye drop is shaken well and used three times a day to lower eye pressure. Blurred vision for a short time, a bad taste or tiredness can happen. A rash, eye swelling or severe drowsiness are reported to the prescriber, and the bottle is kept away from young children.|Sulfa allergy is reviewed because brinzolamide is a sulfonamide; the suspension is shaken before each dose and other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart per label; severe renal impairment and age under 2 years are reasons the product is not used per label; drowsiness and low blood pressure are monitored; oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are not combined per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Brinzolamide and brimonidine ophthalmic suspension;;NCBI StatPearls: Brimonidine
netarsudil-latanoprost|This eye drop is used once each evening; using it more often can make it work less well. Red eyes are common, and the colored part of the eye can slowly darken and eyelashes can grow longer. Eye pain, swelling or a sudden change in vision are reported to the eye doctor.|The label dose is one drop in the evening and not more than once daily; contact lenses are removed before dosing and reinserted 15 minutes later; conjunctival hyperemia and corneal verticillata are expected effects per label; history of herpetic keratitis or intraocular inflammation is reviewed; other eye drops are spaced at least 5 minutes apart|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Netarsudil and latanoprost ophthalmic solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Latanoprost
bimatoprost-implant|This tiny implant is placed inside the front of the eye by the eye doctor and slowly releases medicine to lower eye pressure, then dissolves. Some redness or a gritty feeling can happen after the procedure. Increasing eye pain, light sensitivity, redness or vision loss in the days after the procedure are reported to the eye doctor right away.|Prior Durysta in the same eye is reviewed because the label states it is not readministered to that eye; corneal endothelial dystrophy, prior corneal transplant and an absent or ruptured posterior capsule are contraindications per label; the procedure is done under aseptic conditions; eye pain, redness and vision loss after the procedure are evaluated for endophthalmitis or corneal edema|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Bimatoprost intracameral implant;;NCBI StatPearls: Bimatoprost
travoprost-implant|This tiny titanium implant is placed inside the eye by the eye doctor and slowly releases medicine to lower eye pressure. It stays in the eye, so it is mentioned before any MRI scan. Eye pain, redness, light sensitivity or vision loss after the procedure are reported to the eye doctor right away.|Ocular or periocular infection, corneal endothelial dystrophy and prior corneal transplant are contraindications per label; the label states readministration more than once per year is not recommended; corneal endothelial cell density is reviewed before readministration per label; the device is MRI conditional per label; endophthalmitis and device dislocation are evaluated after the procedure|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Travoprost intracameral implant;;NCBI StatPearls: Travoprost
darifenacin|This extended-release pill for overactive bladder is swallowed whole once a day, with or without food. Dry mouth and constipation are common, and overheating can happen more easily because sweating is reduced. Trouble passing urine, severe constipation or swelling of the face, lips or tongue are reported right away.|Urinary retention, gastric retention and uncontrolled narrow-angle glaucoma are contraindications per label; the dose is 7.5 mg once daily, up to 15 mg, with a 7.5 mg cap in moderate hepatic impairment or with potent CYP3A4 inhibitors per label; tablets are not crushed or chewed; anticholinergic burden and confusion are reviewed in older adults; post-void residual and bowel function are typical monitoring|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Darifenacin;;NCBI StatPearls: Darifenacin;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
trospium|This bladder pill is usually taken twice a day on an empty stomach, at least 1 hour before meals. Dry mouth and constipation are common, and overheating can happen because the body sweats less. Trouble passing urine or swelling of the face, lips or tongue is reported right away.|Urinary retention, gastric retention and uncontrolled narrow-angle glaucoma are label contraindications and are reviewed before the first dose; creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min reduces the dose to 20 mg once daily at bedtime per label; doses are timed at least 1 hour before meals because food lowers absorption; bowel function, voiding and confusion in older adults are reviewed; other anticholinergics add to dry mouth, constipation and retention|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Trospium chloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Trospium;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
pentosan-polysulfate|This capsule is taken three times a day with water, at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals, and relief of bladder pain can take about 3 months. It acts a little like a mild blood thinner, so unusual bleeding or bruising is reported. Regular eye exams are part of long-term use, and changes in vision or trouble reading are reported.|Anticoagulant, antiplatelet and NSAID use is reviewed because pentosan has weak heparin-like activity; baseline and periodic retinal examinations are recommended per label for pigmentary maculopathy; liver enzymes are reviewed if symptoms suggest hepatotoxicity; response is reassessed after 3 months per label; bleeding or new vision changes typically prompt provider notification|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pentosan polysulfate sodium;;NCBI StatPearls: Interstitial Cystitis;;AUA Guideline: Diagnosis and Treatment of Interstitial Cystitis/Bladder Pain Syndrome (2022)
vardenafil|This pill is taken about an hour before sex, no more than once a day, and works only with sexual stimulation. It is dangerous with nitrate heart medicines such as nitroglycerin, so emergency staff are told about recent doses. An erection lasting more than 4 hours, sudden vision or hearing loss, or chest pain is an emergency.|Nitrate and riociguat use are contraindications and are reviewed before dosing; QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics and congenital long QT are label cautions; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir limit the dose per label; blood pressure is reviewed with alpha-blockers; the orally disintegrating and film-coated tablets are not interchangeable|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Vardenafil;;NCBI StatPearls: Vardenafil;;AUA Guideline: Erectile Dysfunction (2018)
avanafil|This pill can work as soon as about 15 minutes before sex and is taken no more than once a day. It is dangerous with nitrate heart medicines such as nitroglycerin, so emergency staff are told about recent doses. An erection lasting more than 4 hours, sudden vision or hearing loss, or chest pain is an emergency.|Nitrate and riociguat use are contraindications and are reviewed before dosing; with alpha-blockers the starting dose is 50 mg per label; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are not recommended and moderate inhibitors limit the dose to 50 mg once every 24 hours per label; severe renal or hepatic impairment are not recommended per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Avanafil;;NCBI StatPearls: Avanafil;;AUA Guideline: Erectile Dysfunction (2018)
tiopronin|This pill is taken in 3 doses at about the same times each day, together with plenty of fluids to keep the urine dilute. Regular urine tests check cystine and protein levels. Rash, fever, joint pain, swelling of the legs or foamy urine is reported to the care team.|Urinary cystine is measured 1 month after starting and every 3 months per label, with a target below 250 mg/L; urinalysis for protein is reviewed because proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome are label warnings; prior penicillamine reactions are reviewed; high fluid intake and urinary alkalinization are part of the regimen; Thiola and Thiola EC tablets differ in release|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tiopronin;;NCBI StatPearls: Cystinuria;;AUA Guideline: Medical Management of Kidney Stones (2014, amended 2019)
cysteamine|This medicine is taken every 12 hours, ideally on an empty stomach, and is part of lifelong care for cystinosis. It can cause stomach upset and a strong breath or body odor. Bloody or black stools, severe headache, vision changes, skin rash or unusual skin marks are reported to the care team.|Leukocyte cystine is measured to guide dosing; the delayed-release Procysbi schedule is every 12 hours, while immediate-release Cystagon is every 6 hours, a common source of errors; bicarbonate or carbonate products are separated by at least 1 hour per label; GI bleeding, rash, headache with vision change and lethargy typically prompt provider notification; CBC and alkaline phosphatase are reviewed|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Cysteamine bitartrate;;NCBI StatPearls: Cystinosis
collagenase-clostridium-histolyticum|This injection is given in the clinic into the hand cord or penile plaque, followed by a stretching or modeling procedure. Bruising, swelling and pain at the site are common. For Peyronie treatment, sex is avoided for a few weeks after each cycle, and sudden penile pain, swelling, a popping sound or loss of erection is reported right away.|Anticoagulants other than low-dose aspirin within 7 days are reviewed because of bleeding and hematoma risk; Peyronie treatment is given only through the XIAFLEX REMS Program because of the boxed warning for corporal rupture; injection-site swelling, hand sensation and tendon function are reviewed after Dupuytren treatment; hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis, is a label warning|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Collagenase clostridium histolyticum;;NCBI StatPearls: Dupuytren Contracture;;AUA Guideline: Peyronie's Disease (2015)
testosterone-undecanoate|These capsules are taken twice a day with food, because the medicine needs fat to be absorbed. Blood pressure and blood tests are checked regularly, and blood pressure can rise. Chest pain, shortness of breath, leg swelling or trouble urinating is reported; the medicine is kept away from others because it is a controlled substance.|Blood pressure is reviewed before starting and periodically because increases are a label warning; hematocrit, PSA and serum testosterone are reviewed, with testosterone checked 7 days after a dose change per label; pregnancy and prostate or breast cancer are contraindications; the injectable Aveed form carries a boxed warning for POME and anaphylaxis with post-injection observation; Schedule III controlled substance|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Testosterone undecanoate;;Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Testosterone Therapy in Men with Hypogonadism (2018);;NCBI StatPearls: Testosterone
flavoxate|This pill eases bladder spasms and burning but does not cure an infection; it is used alongside treatment of the cause. It can cause drowsiness, dry mouth or blurred vision, so driving can be affected. Trouble passing urine, confusion or a fast heartbeat is reported.|GI or lower urinary tract obstruction is a label contraindication and is reviewed before the first dose; glaucoma and age-related confusion are cautions; drowsiness and blurred vision affect driving; other anticholinergic drugs add to the burden in older adults; it is used together with treatment of the underlying cause per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Flavoxate hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Flavoxate;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
dutasteride-tamsulosin|This prostate capsule is taken once a day about 30 minutes after the same meal and is swallowed whole. Dizziness when standing up is common at first, and an erection lasting more than 4 hours is an emergency. Women who are or may become pregnant are kept from handling leaking capsules, blood donation waits at least 6 months after the last dose, and an eye surgeon is told about this medicine before cataract surgery.|Orthostatic blood pressure is typically reviewed at baseline and after starting, and symptomatic hypotension or syncope commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; PSA falls about 50 percent within 6 months, so a new baseline is set and values are doubled for comparison per label; capsules are not opened or crushed and are not handled by pregnant staff; tamsulosin is linked to intraoperative floppy iris syndrome, which is relevant before cataract surgery; PDE5 inhibitors and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors add to hypotension risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dutasteride and Tamsulosin;;NCBI StatPearls: Dutasteride;;NCBI StatPearls: Tamsulosin
finasteride-tadalafil|This prostate capsule is taken once a day on an empty stomach for up to 26 weeks. It contains tadalafil, so taking it with nitroglycerin or other nitrate medicines can drop blood pressure dangerously. Sudden vision or hearing loss or an erection lasting more than 4 hours is an emergency, and women who are or may become pregnant are kept from handling broken capsules.|Nitrate use, including as-needed nitroglycerin, is reviewed before dosing because the combination is contraindicated and can cause profound hypotension; blood pressure is reviewed when alpha blockers or antihypertensives are also taken; creatinine clearance below 50 mL/min or hemodialysis is a not-recommended setting per label; PSA is roughly halved by finasteride, so values are interpreted against a new baseline; sudden vision or hearing changes commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Finasteride and Tadalafil;;NCBI StatPearls: Finasteride;;NCBI StatPearls: Tadalafil
oxybutynin-transdermal|This bladder patch is changed twice a week, every 3 or 4 days, on clean dry skin of the belly, hip or buttock, using a new spot each time. Dry mouth, constipation and skin redness under the patch are common. Confusion, trouble passing urine, eye pain or overheating in hot weather are reasons to call the prescriber.|Urinary retention, gastric retention and uncontrolled narrow-angle glaucoma are contraindications per label, so voiding history and eye history are reviewed; mental status is reviewed in older adults because of anticholinergic burden; application sites are rotated and the same site is not reused within 7 days; old patches are removed before a new one is applied to avoid double dosing; other anticholinergics and cholinesterase inhibitors are relevant interactions|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oxybutynin Transdermal System;;NCBI StatPearls: Oxybutynin;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
collagenase-peyronie|This medicine is injected into the scar in the penis in the urologist's office, usually in cycles of two injections followed by gentle stretching. Bruising, swelling and pain are common for a few days. A popping sound with sudden loss of erection, rapidly growing bruising or swelling, or trouble urinating is an emergency, and sexual activity is paused for the time the urologist advises after each cycle.|Available for Peyronie disease only through the XIAFLEX REMS program and injected by trained providers; anticoagulant use other than low-dose aspirin is reviewed because of severe penile hematoma risk; signs of corporal rupture such as sudden detumescence, popping sensation and rapid swelling commonly prompt urgent provider notification; patients are observed for hypersensitivity reactions after injection; Xiaflex is distinct from topical collagenase ointment (Santyl)|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Collagenase Clostridium Histolyticum;;NCBI StatPearls: Peyronie Disease;;Endo product information: Xiaflex
acetohydroxamic-acid|This pill is taken 3 to 4 times a day on an empty stomach along with antibiotics for a long-lasting urine infection with stones. It can cause headache, upset stomach and a skin rash after drinking alcohol. It can harm an unborn baby, so reliable birth control is part of treatment, and leg swelling, chest pain or shortness of breath is an emergency.|Serum creatinine is reviewed before dosing because doses are reduced above 1.8 mg/dL and the drug is not used above 2.5 mg/dL per label; CBC with reticulocyte count is reviewed for Coombs-negative hemolytic anemia; pregnancy status and contraception are confirmed because the drug is contraindicated in pregnancy; iron supplements reduce absorption of both drugs; leg swelling or dyspnea commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider for possible DVT or PE|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Acetohydroxamic Acid;;NCBI StatPearls: Struvite Stones
sodium-ferric-gluconate|This iron is given through the dialysis machine or IV during dialysis sessions, usually over several sessions. Some people feel flushing, cramps, nausea or lightheadedness during the dose. Itching, hives, wheezing, swelling or feeling faint during or after the dose is reported to staff right away.|Ferritin, transferrin saturation and hemoglobin are reviewed before repletion courses; blood pressure is reviewed during administration because hypotension is linked to rate; the adult dose is 125 mg elemental iron in 100 mL 0.9% sodium chloride over 1 hour or undiluted at up to 12.5 mg/min per label; patients are observed for hypersensitivity for at least 30 minutes afterward per label; mL and mg of elemental iron are not interchangeable, a known source of dose errors|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Sodium Ferric Gluconate Complex in Sucrose;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease (2012);;NCBI StatPearls: Iron Supplementation
methoxy-polyethylene-glycol-epoetin-beta|This is a long-acting shot or IV dose, usually every 2 weeks or once a month, to raise red blood cells in kidney disease. Blood pressure is checked often, and iron pills or iron infusions are commonly part of treatment. Chest pain, trouble speaking, weakness on one side, a seizure, leg swelling or a dialysis access that stops working is an emergency.|Blood pressure is typically reviewed before each dose, and uncontrolled hypertension commonly prompts holding and notifying the provider; hemoglobin is reviewed regularly, and a rapid rise or values above target lead to dose reduction or interruption per label; iron studies (ferritin, transferrin saturation) are reviewed before and during therapy; the lowest dose that reduces transfusion need is used because of boxed warning risks above 11 g/dL; conversion doses from epoetin or darbepoetin follow the label table|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Methoxy Polyethylene Glycol-Epoetin Beta;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease (2012);;NCBI StatPearls: Erythropoietin
daprodustat|This is a once-daily pill for anemia in people on dialysis, taken with or without food. Blood pressure and blood counts are checked often, and the dose is adjusted by the care team. Chest pain, trouble speaking, weakness on one side, leg swelling, black stools or vomiting blood is an emergency.|Hemoglobin is reviewed regularly, and values above 12 g/dL lead to interruption per label; blood pressure is reviewed because uncontrolled hypertension is a contraindication; gemfibrozil is contraindicated and clopidogrel or moderate CYP2C8 inhibitors halve the starting dose per label; iron status and liver tests are reviewed during therapy; GI bleeding signs and heart failure symptoms commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Daprodustat;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease (2012)
vadadustat|This is a once-daily pill for anemia in people on dialysis. Iron pills and some phosphate binders are spaced apart from it as the pharmacist directs, and blood counts and liver tests are checked often. Chest pain, trouble speaking, weakness on one side, leg swelling, yellow skin or eyes, or dark urine is an emergency.|Liver tests (ALT, AST, bilirubin) are reviewed before starting and during the first months per label, and significant elevations commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; hemoglobin and blood pressure are reviewed regularly; oral iron and iron, calcium or aluminum containing phosphate binders reduce absorption, so dose timing is separated per label; statin doses such as rosuvastatin and simvastatin are limited per label because vadadustat raises their levels|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Vadadustat;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease (2012)
`);

