DB.addExt(`
lusutrombopag|This pill is taken once a day for 7 days, starting 8 to 14 days before your planned procedure, with or without food. A blood count is checked before the procedure. Belly pain, leg swelling, chest pain or shortness of breath can signal a blood clot and are worth reporting right away.|The 7-day course is timed so the procedure falls 2 to 8 days after the last dose per label; a platelet count is obtained before therapy and not more than 2 days before the procedure; abdominal pain or leg swelling is recognized as a possible sign of portal vein or deep vein thrombosis; it is not intended to normalize platelet counts in chronic liver disease per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lusutrombopag;;NCBI StatPearls: Thrombopoietin Receptor Agonists
fostamatinib|This pill is taken twice a day with or without food. It can raise blood pressure and cause diarrhea, so home blood pressure readings and fluids help. Yellow skin, dark urine, fever or bleeding are worth reporting to your care team.|Blood pressure is typically reviewed every 2 weeks until stable and then monthly per label; liver tests and CBC with ANC are reviewed monthly, with dose reduction or interruption for hepatotoxicity, neutropenia or severe diarrhea per label; diarrhea is managed with hydration and antidiarrheals; strong CYP3A4 inducers lower R406 exposure, and rosuvastatin and digoxin levels can rise|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fostamatinib;;American Society of Hematology 2019 Guidelines for Immune Thrombocytopenia;;NCBI StatPearls: Fostamatinib
crizanlizumab|This medicine is given as an IV infusion over 30 minutes, two weeks apart at first and then every 4 weeks. Fever, chills, itching, hives, nausea or new pain during or after the infusion are worth telling the infusion staff about. Pain crises can still happen, so your usual pain plan stays in place.|Infusion reactions such as fever, chills, nausea, pruritus or urticaria are typically reviewed during and after each infusion, with slowing or interruption per label; it is diluted in 0.9% sodium chloride or 5% dextrose to 100 mL and given over 30 minutes; platelet count samples are run promptly or drawn in citrate tubes to limit clumping artifact per label; it can be given with or without hydroxyurea|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Crizanlizumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Sickle Cell Disease
voxelotor|Voxelotor (Oxbryta) was taken off the market in September 2024, so leftover tablets are worth discussing with your hematology team. It was taken once a day with or without food. New pain crises, rash or hives are worth reporting.|The manufacturer withdrew voxelotor worldwide in September 2024 after an imbalance in vaso-occlusive crises and deaths in post-marketing studies; hemoglobin subtype results by HPLC are unreliable during treatment per label; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or inducers change dosing per label; hypersensitivity reactions including rash and urticaria are described|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Voxelotor;;NCBI StatPearls: Sickle Cell Disease
mitapivat|This pill is taken twice a day, with the dose raised slowly over several months. Stopping it suddenly can cause a fast breakdown of red blood cells, so doses are lowered gradually when it is stopped. Dark urine, yellow eyes, belly pain or tiredness are worth reporting.|Hemoglobin and hemolysis markers are reviewed during titration every 4 weeks per label; abrupt interruption is linked to acute hemolysis, so discontinuation is done by taper per label; liver tests at baseline and every 4 weeks for 24 weeks apply to Aqvesme in thalassemia, which carries a boxed warning and REMS for hepatocellular injury; strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided, and hormonal contraceptive efficacy can fall|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Mitapivat;;NCBI StatPearls: Pyruvate Kinase Deficiency
exagamglogene-autotemcel|This is a one-time treatment made from your own stem cells, given after strong chemotherapy that clears the bone marrow. For weeks afterward, blood counts are very low, so fever, bleeding or bruising are reported right away. The chemotherapy can affect fertility, which is discussed before treatment.|Hydroxyurea is stopped at least 8 weeks before mobilization and conditioning and iron chelators at least 7 days before conditioning per label; patient identity is matched to the product before infusion through a central line; CBCs are followed until neutrophil and platelet engraftment, with bleeding and infection precautions during cytopenias; unmodified rescue cells are stored in case of engraftment failure|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Exagamglogene autotemcel;;NCBI StatPearls: Sickle Cell Disease
lovotibeglogene-autotemcel|This is a one-time treatment made from your own stem cells with an added gene, given after strong chemotherapy. Blood counts are low for weeks, so fever or bleeding are reported right away. Blood tests continue for many years because of a risk of blood cancer.|Boxed warning for hematologic malignancy, with CBCs at least every 6 months and integration site analysis per label; hydroxyurea and antiretrovirals are stopped before mobilization per label; patient identity is matched to the product before central line infusion; HIV nucleic acid tests can read falsely positive after treatment|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Lovotibeglogene autotemcel;;NCBI StatPearls: Sickle Cell Disease
etranacogene-dezaparvovec|This is a one-time IV gene therapy. Liver blood tests are done weekly for the first months and then monthly for a year, and steroids may be used if liver tests rise. Limiting alcohol and reporting yellow skin, dark urine or bleeding helps protect the liver and catch problems early.|FIX inhibitor testing and liver health assessment precede dosing per label; the diluted product is infused at 500 mL/hour with observation for infusion reactions; ALT is reviewed weekly for 3 months and monthly up to 1 year, with a corticosteroid course considered for ALT rises per label; FIX activity guides whether factor IX concentrate is still used for surgery or bleeding|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Etranacogene dezaparvovec;;NCBI StatPearls: Hemophilia B
valoctocogene-roxaparvovec|This is a one-time IV gene therapy. Liver blood tests are done weekly for at least 6 months, and steroids are often used if liver tests rise. Limiting alcohol and reporting yellow skin, dark urine, bleeding or leg swelling helps catch problems early.|AAV5 antibody status, infections and liver fibrosis are assessed before dosing per label; the infusion starts at 1 mL/min and increases every 30 minutes up to 4 mL/min, with slowing or stopping for infusion reactions; ALT is reviewed weekly for at least 26 weeks, with corticosteroids considered for ALT rises per label; FVIII activity tends to decline over years|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Valoctocogene roxaparvovec;;NCBI StatPearls: Hemophilia A
concizumab|This medicine is injected under the skin once a day with a prefilled pen after a first larger loading dose. A blood level at about 4 weeks sets your long-term dose. If a bleed happens, your hemophilia plan for factor or bypassing agents applies, and chest pain, leg swelling or sudden weakness are reported right away.|Loading dose on Day 1 then once-daily dosing, with the Week 4 plasma concentration guiding the maintenance dose per label; breakthrough bleeds are treated with factor or bypassing agents, and high or frequent doses raise thromboembolism risk per label; D-dimer and prothrombin fragment 1.2 rise as an expected lab effect; signs of thrombosis and hypersensitivity are reviewed|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Concizumab;;NCBI StatPearls: Hemophilia A
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
marstacimab|This is a once-a-week shot under the skin that helps prevent bleeding in hemophilia A or B. It starts with a double loading dose, then one dose on the same day each week. Sudden leg swelling, chest pain, trouble breathing or stroke signs can mean a blood clot and are an emergency, and the hemophilia center is told about any breakthrough bleed treated with factor or bypassing products.|Hemophilia type, inhibitor status and recent factor or bypassing agent use are reviewed before dosing; signs of thrombosis typically prompt holding and notifying the provider; D-dimer and prothrombin fragment 1.2 rise from the drug, so they are less useful for diagnosing clots; the 75 mg and 150 mg presentations are a dose-error risk in children; pregnancy status is relevant because of embryofetal toxicity per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Marstacimab;;WFH Guidelines for the Management of Hemophilia, 3rd edition (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Hemophilia B
fitusiran|This shot under the skin is given every 1 or 2 months and lowers a natural blood thinner so the blood clots better in hemophilia. Blood tests for antithrombin level decide the dose. Signs of a blood clot, severe pain in the upper right belly, or yellow skin are reported right away, and the hemophilia center gives special lower doses of factor for breakthrough bleeds.|Antithrombin activity is reviewed at set times to guide dose changes per label, with persistently low levels linked to thrombosis; LFTs are reviewed for hepatotoxicity; right upper quadrant pain or fever raises concern for gallbladder disease; breakthrough bleeds use reduced factor or bypassing agent doses per label; heparin effect is blunted when antithrombin is low|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fitusiran;;WFH Guidelines for the Management of Hemophilia, 3rd edition (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Hemophilia A
protein-c-concentrate|This medicine replaces protein C, a natural blood thinner that is missing in severe protein C deficiency. It is given into a vein, sometimes at home after training. New dark purple skin patches, swelling of a limb, bleeding, or an allergic reaction are reported right away.|Protein C activity is reviewed to guide dosing, with a target near 100% for acute episodes per label; injection rate is limited to 2 mL per minute, and 0.2 mL/kg/minute in children under 10 kg; the product contains trace heparin, so HIT history is relevant; bleeding risk rises with concurrent anticoagulants or thrombolytics; skin lesions of purpura fulminans are documented|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Protein C Concentrate (Human);;NCBI StatPearls: Protein C Deficiency
aminolevulinic-acid|This liquid is painted on sun-damaged skin spots, and after a waiting time the spots are treated with a blue light in the office. Burning or stinging during the light is common. The treated skin stays very sensitive to light for about 40 hours, so it is kept covered from sun and bright light.|Porphyria history and photosensitizing medicines are reviewed before treatment; incubation is 14 to 18 hours for face or scalp and 3 hours for arms per label; treated areas are protected from light for 40 hours; protective eyewear is worn during illumination; burning during illumination can be managed by pausing the light|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Aminolevulinic acid hydrochloride (Levulan Kerastick);;NCBI StatPearls: Actinic Keratosis;;NCBI StatPearls: Photodynamic Therapy
givosiran|This monthly shot under the skin is given by a health care worker to prevent porphyria attacks. Blood tests for liver and kidney function are done regularly. Severe belly pain, dark urine, weakness, or allergic symptoms like trouble breathing are reported right away, and alcohol, fasting and some medicines can still trigger attacks.|Dose is calculated from actual body weight at 2.5 mg/kg per label; LFTs are reviewed before starting and monthly early in therapy, and severe transaminase elevations prompt holding per label; creatinine and homocysteine are reviewed; anaphylaxis treatment is available during administration; CYP1A2 and CYP2D6 substrate levels can rise|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Givosiran;;NCBI StatPearls: Acute Intermittent Porphyria
danicopan|This pill is taken three times a day with food, together with an infusion medicine for PNH. It lowers the immune system's defense against some bacteria, so meningococcal vaccines are part of the plan and a safety card is carried. Fever, headache with stiff neck, rash, or confusion is an emergency, and doses are not stopped without the care team.|Meningococcal and pneumococcal vaccination status is reviewed per the REMS; fever or signs of infection typically prompt urgent provider notification; LFTs and lipids are reviewed; hemoglobin, LDH and reticulocytes track response; rosuvastatin and other BCRP substrates can rise in level|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Danicopan;;NCBI StatPearls: Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria
tbo-filgrastim|This daily shot under the skin helps the body make infection-fighting white cells after chemotherapy. Bone or muscle aches are common. Fever, pain in the upper left belly or shoulder tip, trouble breathing, or allergic symptoms are reported right away.|The first dose is given no earlier than 24 hours after chemotherapy per label; CBC with differential is reviewed regularly during therapy; left upper quadrant or shoulder pain raises concern for splenic rupture; dyspnea or hypoxia raises concern for ARDS; dosing in mcg/kg is a known mcg versus mg error point|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Tbo-filgrastim;;NCBI StatPearls: Filgrastim
factor-ix|This clotting factor is given into a vein to treat or prevent bleeding in hemophilia B, often at home. The dose depends on weight and how severe the bleed is. Head injury, severe joint or muscle bleeding, or signs of an allergic reaction are emergencies, and the hemophilia center is contacted for serious bleeds.|Dose is calculated from weight and desired factor IX rise, about 1% per IU/kg for this product per label; infusion rate stays at or below 10 mL/minute; factor IX activity and inhibitor titers guide therapy; hypersensitivity and thrombosis or DIC signs typically prompt stopping the infusion and notifying the provider|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Coagulation Factor IX (Human) (AlphaNine SD);;WFH Guidelines for the Management of Hemophilia, 3rd edition (2020);;NCBI StatPearls: Hemophilia B
von-willebrand-factor|This clotting protein is given into a vein to treat or prevent bleeding in von Willebrand disease. Nosebleeds, heavy periods or bleeding after surgery that does not stop, signs of a blood clot, or allergic symptoms are reported right away.|Dosing is in VWF:RCo IU/kg per label; recombinant factor VIII is paired with the first dose when baseline FVIII is below 40% or unknown; infusion rate is up to 4 mL per minute; VWF:RCo and factor VIII activity are reviewed to limit excess FVIII and thrombosis; hypersensitivity typically prompts stopping the infusion|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Von Willebrand Factor (Recombinant);;ASH ISTH NHF WFH 2021 Guidelines on the Management of Von Willebrand Disease;;NCBI StatPearls: Von Willebrand Disease
`);

;
DB.addExt(`
anagrelide|This capsule lowers a high platelet count and is usually taken two to four times a day, with blood counts checked often at first. A racing or pounding heartbeat, headache, swelling, shortness of breath or chest pain are reported to the care team. Aspirin and blood thinners raise bleeding risk with this medicine, and stopping it suddenly lets platelets climb back up within days.|Baseline cardiovascular examination including ECG is recommended per label, and QTc, potassium and magnesium are relevant before dosing; platelet count is reviewed frequently during titration, with dose increases of no more than 0.5 mg/day in any week per label; palpitations, edema, dyspnea or chest pain commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; concurrent aspirin or anticoagulants increase bleeding risk; moderate hepatic impairment starts at 0.5 mg/day per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Anagrelide;;NCBI StatPearls: Essential Thrombocythemia
factor-xiii-concentrate|This medicine replaces a clotting protein the body does not make enough of and is usually given by IV about once a month. Hives, chest tightness, wheezing or faintness during the infusion are reported right away. A bad headache, head injury, or unusual bleeding is treated as an emergency because people with this condition can bleed inside the head.|Prior reactions to plasma products and the date of the last dose are reviewed before infusion; the infusion rate does not exceed 4 mL/min per label; signs of hypersensitivity commonly prompt stopping the infusion and notifying the provider; FXIII trough activity (target 5% to 20%) and inhibitor testing are used when response seems inadequate; signs of thrombosis are reported|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Factor XIII Concentrate (Human);;NCBI StatPearls: Factor XIII Deficiency
efanesoctocog-alfa|This is a long-acting factor VIII medicine for hemophilia A that is usually injected into a vein once a week, often at home after training. Hives, chest tightness, trouble breathing or bleeding that does not stop as expected are reported to the hemophilia team. Carrying a record of the product name and last dose helps emergency staff.|Time and dose of the last infusion and inhibitor status are reviewed; the injection is given slowly over 1 to 10 minutes per label; hypersensitivity signs commonly prompt stopping the infusion and notifying the provider; poor bleeding control or low factor VIII activity raises concern for inhibitors (Bethesda assay); head trauma in a hemophilia patient is typically handled as an emergency with early factor replacement per the treatment center plan|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Efanesoctocog Alfa;;NCBI StatPearls: Hemophilia A
pozelimab|This medicine blocks part of the immune system that damages the gut in CHAPLE disease; the first dose is an IV infusion and later doses are weekly shots under the skin. Because it raises the risk of serious meningococcal infection, fever, headache with stiff neck, rash, confusion or vomiting are treated as an emergency even after vaccines. The patient safety card is carried at all times.|Meningococcal vaccination (A, C, W, Y and B) at least 2 weeks before the first dose is reviewed per label, with antibacterial prophylaxis when vaccination is incomplete; fever or signs of meningitis commonly prompt urgent provider notification; infusion and hypersensitivity reactions are observed during the IV loading dose; serum albumin and total protein are followed as response markers|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pozelimab;;Regeneron product information: Veopoz
crovalimab|This medicine protects red blood cells in PNH; the first dose is given by IV and later doses are shots under the skin, eventually once a month. Fever, headache with stiff neck, rash or confusion can be meningococcal infection and are treated as an emergency even after vaccines. Dark urine, unusual tiredness or signs of a blood clot are reported, especially after a missed or stopped dose.|Meningococcal vaccination status and PIASKY REMS enrollment are reviewed per label; dose is based on weight band (40 to less than 100 kg or 100 kg or more); fever or meningitis signs commonly prompt urgent provider notification; patients switched from eculizumab or ravulizumab are observed for immune complex reactions such as arthralgia, rash and fever; LDH and hemoglobin are followed for breakthrough hemolysis|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Crovalimab;;NCBI StatPearls: Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria
ferric-maltol|This iron capsule is taken twice a day on an empty stomach, 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals. Gas, stomach pain, diarrhea, constipation and dark stools can happen. Iron pills are kept away from children because an overdose can be deadly for a child.|Hemoglobin, ferritin and transferrin saturation are reviewed before and during therapy to avoid iron overload; use during an IBD flare is avoided per label; oral drugs that bind iron such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones and levothyroxine are separated from the dose; dark stools from iron are distinguished from melena by history and testing|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ferric Maltol;;NCBI StatPearls: Iron Deficiency Anemia
ferric-pyrophosphate-citrate|This iron is given through the dialysis machine during each hemodialysis treatment to replace iron lost during dialysis. Itching, swelling, wheezing or feeling faint during treatment are reported to the dialysis staff right away. Regular blood tests track iron and hemoglobin.|Triferic AVNU is infused undiluted over 3 to 4 hours through the dialysis circuit per label; Triferic and Triferic AVNU are distinct formulations (dialysate additive versus IV); hypersensitivity signs commonly prompt stopping the infusion and notifying the provider; hemoglobin, ferritin and transferrin saturation are followed with ESA therapy|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ferric Pyrophosphate Citrate;;KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease (2012)
alfentanil|This is a strong, fast-acting pain medicine given only by anesthesia staff during surgery or procedures. Patients are watched closely for breathing problems, and drowsiness can last a short time afterward. Telling staff about other sedatives, opioids or medicines like erythromycin or antifungals helps keep it safe.|Opioid, high-alert medication per ISMP with independent double checks for infusions; airway equipment, oxygen and naloxone are kept available per label; SpO2, EtCO2, respiratory rate and chest wall rigidity are monitored; CYP3A4 inhibitors such as erythromycin and fluconazole prolong effects; mcg/kg dosing based on lean body weight reduces dosing errors|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Alfentanil;;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings;;NCBI StatPearls: Alfentanil
pentazocine|This pain pill contains pentazocine plus naloxone and is taken by mouth only; injecting it can cause severe reactions. It can cause dizziness, confusion or seeing things, and mixing it with alcohol, sleeping pills or other opioids is dangerous. People on methadone, buprenorphine or other opioids can go into withdrawal with this medicine.|Respiratory rate and sedation level are reviewed before dosing, with marked sedation or slow breathing commonly prompting holding and notifying the provider; total daily dose stays at or below 12 tablets per label; hallucinations or confusion are reported; precipitated withdrawal can occur in patients on full mu agonists; blood pressure can rise, which is relevant in coronary disease|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Pentazocine and Naloxone;;CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain (2022)
levorphanol|This strong opioid pain pill lasts a long time and can build up over several days, so doses are taken exactly as prescribed. Drowsiness, constipation and nausea are common; alcohol, sleeping pills and benzodiazepines can cause dangerous breathing problems. Naloxone kept at home is a resource for overdose, and pills are stored locked away from children.|Respiratory rate and sedation are reviewed before dosing, with slow breathing or heavy sedation commonly prompting holding and notifying the provider; the long half-life (11 to 16 hours) means toxicity can build over days after dose changes; bowel regimen and constipation are reviewed; benzodiazepine and MAOI interactions are significant; Schedule II with Opioid Analgesic REMS|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Levorphanol;;CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain (2022);;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings
`);


;
DB.addExt(`
codeine-acetaminophen|This pain pill contains an opioid (codeine) and acetaminophen and can cause drowsiness, dizziness and constipation. Other products that contain acetaminophen are counted toward the daily limit, and alcohol and sleep or anxiety medicines add dangerous sedation. Slow or shallow breathing or extreme sleepiness is an emergency, and the medicine is kept locked away from children.|Respiratory rate and sedation level are typically reviewed before each dose, and slow breathing or heavy sedation commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; total acetaminophen from all sources is tracked against the 4,000 mg daily limit; codeine is contraindicated under 12 years and after pediatric tonsillectomy per label; benzodiazepines, alcohol and CYP3A4 inhibitors add risk; naloxone is the opioid reversal agent|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Acetaminophen and Codeine Phosphate;;CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain (2022);;NCBI StatPearls: Codeine
hydrocodone-acetaminophen|This pain pill combines an opioid with acetaminophen and is taken only as prescribed, usually every 4 to 6 hours when needed. Drowsiness, dizziness, nausea and constipation are common, and alcohol, sleeping pills and other acetaminophen products add danger. Slow breathing or being very hard to wake is an emergency, and pills are stored locked away from others.|Respiratory rate and sedation level are typically reviewed before each dose, and slow breathing or heavy sedation commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; total acetaminophen from all sources is tracked against the 4,000 mg daily limit; Schedule II controlled substance with count and waste procedures; benzodiazepines, alcohol and CYP3A4 inhibitors add risk; bowel regimen and naloxone availability are part of opioid safety|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Hydrocodone Bitartrate and Acetaminophen;;CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain (2022);;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings
oxycodone-acetaminophen|This strong pain pill combines oxycodone with acetaminophen and is taken only as prescribed, usually every 6 hours when needed. Drowsiness, dizziness, nausea and constipation are common, and alcohol, sleeping pills and other acetaminophen products add danger. Slow breathing or being very hard to wake is an emergency; pills that do not come from a pharmacy can contain fentanyl.|Respiratory rate and sedation level are typically reviewed before each dose, and slow breathing or heavy sedation commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; total acetaminophen is tracked against the 4 gram daily limit per label; Schedule II controlled substance with count and waste procedures; benzodiazepines, alcohol and CYP3A4 inhibitors add risk; naloxone is the opioid reversal agent|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oxycodone and Acetaminophen;;CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain (2022);;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings
prilocaine|This dental numbing shot makes the lip, cheek and tongue numb for a few hours, so chewing and hot drinks are easier once feeling returns. Bluish lips or fingernails, headache, shortness of breath or unusual tiredness after dental work can be signs of a blood reaction called methemoglobinemia and are an emergency.|History of methemoglobinemia, G6PD deficiency and oxidizing drugs such as dapsone or nitrates is reviewed before use; weight-based maximum of 8 mg/kg (600 mg in adults 70 kg or more) within 2 hours per label; cyanosis with SpO2 near 85% that does not improve with oxygen suggests methemoglobinemia, for which methylene blue is the antidote; aspiration before injection reduces intravascular delivery|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Prilocaine Hydrochloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Methemoglobinemia;;NCBI StatPearls: Local Anesthetic Toxicity
bupivacaine-liposomal|This long-acting numbing medicine is given during surgery and can keep the area numb for about 3 days. A numb arm or leg after a nerve block can cause falls, so support is used when walking. Numbness around the mouth, ringing in the ears, a metallic taste, confusion or seizures are reported right away.|Other local anesthetics given in the last 96 hours are reviewed because doses are cumulative; lidocaine and other non-bupivacaine local anesthetics are separated by 20 minutes or more per label because they trigger immediate bupivacaine release; maximum 266 mg per procedure per label; signs of local anesthetic systemic toxicity prompt notifying the provider, with lipid emulsion as the rescue therapy; numb limbs after nerve block raise fall risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Bupivacaine Liposome;;NCBI StatPearls: Local Anesthetic Toxicity;;NCBI StatPearls: Bupivacaine
edrophonium|This medicine is given in the hospital as a short test, and its effects last only a few minutes. Watering eyes, drooling, sweating, stomach cramps or a slow heartbeat can happen briefly, and staff keep an antidote ready.|Heart rate, rhythm and asthma history are reviewed before testing; atropine 0.4 to 0.5 mg is kept immediately available per label; bradycardia or bronchospasm during testing typically prompts stopping and notifying the provider; continuous cardiac monitoring during IV testing is standard; digoxin and beta-blockers add bradycardia risk|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Edrophonium Chloride;;NCBI StatPearls: Edrophonium;;NCBI StatPearls: Myasthenia Gravis
ziconotide|This pain medicine is delivered into the spinal fluid by a pump and is increased slowly over weeks. Confusion, memory problems, hallucinations, mood changes or thoughts of self-harm are reported right away, as are fever, headache or a stiff neck, which can signal infection. Driving and other risky tasks are often limited until the effect on alertness is known.|Psychiatric history is reviewed before starting because pre-existing psychosis is a contraindication per label; mental status, mood and cognition are assessed at each dose change; titration is no faster than 2.4 mcg/day 2 to 3 times per week up to 19.2 mcg/day per label; fever, headache or stiff neck suggest meningitis; creatine kinase is reviewed periodically|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ziconotide;;NCBI StatPearls: Ziconotide
amisulpride|This anti-nausea medicine is given through the IV around surgery. Dizziness, chills or soreness at the IV site can happen, and palpitations or fainting are reported to the nurse.|QTc, potassium and magnesium are reviewed in patients with arrhythmia history or QT-prolonging drugs; 5 mg is the prevention dose and 10 mg the treatment dose per label, given over 1 to 2 minutes; other QT-prolonging antiemetics such as droperidol add risk; levodopa effects are reduced by dopamine antagonism|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Amisulpride;;NCBI StatPearls: Postoperative Nausea and Vomiting
oliceridine|This IV opioid pain medicine is given only in the hospital, sometimes with a button pump. It can cause sleepiness, nausea and dizziness, and staff check breathing closely. Only the patient presses the pump button.|Respiratory rate, SpO2 and sedation are reviewed before each dose, and slow breathing or heavy sedation commonly prompt holding and notifying the provider; cumulative daily dose is tracked against the 27 mg label maximum; benzodiazepines and CYP3A4 or CYP2D6 inhibitors add risk; QT-prolonging drugs are relevant; naloxone is the reversal agent|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oliceridine;;ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings;;NCBI StatPearls: Opioid Analgesics
suzetrigine|This non-opioid pain pill starts with a larger first dose on an empty stomach, then a smaller dose every 12 hours. Grapefruit and some antibiotics and antifungals can raise its level, so all medicines are shared with the pharmacist. It can make some birth control pills less effective, so a backup method is discussed with the prescriber.|Strong CYP3A inhibitors are a contraindication per label, and moderate inhibitors reduce the dose; hepatic function is reviewed because severe impairment is avoided and moderate impairment changes the schedule; the initial 100 mg dose is given on an empty stomach; hormonal contraceptive interactions are reviewed; itching, rash and muscle spasms are the common adverse effects|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Suzetrigine;;NCBI StatPearls: Suzetrigine
`);

