DB.addExt(`
fluticasone-nasal|This steroid nose spray works best when used once a day on a regular schedule, and full relief can take several days. Spraying toward the outer side of the nostril, away from the middle wall, helps prevent nosebleeds. Nosebleeds that keep coming back, vision changes, or use with HIV medicines such as ritonavir are worth reporting to a clinician.|Nasal history is typically reviewed for recent surgery, ulcers or trauma before starting; HIV regimens with ritonavir or cobicistat are flagged because of reported Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression; epistaxis, septal irritation or perforation commonly prompt provider notification; children are usually limited to the shortest effective course per OTC label with growth noted; fluticasone propionate (Flonase) and fluticasone furoate (Sensimist) are distinct products with different spray strengths|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fluticasone Propionate Nasal Spray;;NCBI StatPearls: Fluticasone;;AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Allergic Rhinitis (2015)
budesonide-nasal|This steroid nose spray is used once a day and works gradually over several days. Once symptoms improve, the dose usually drops to one spray in each nostril. Frequent nosebleeds, eye pain or vision changes are worth reporting, and a clinician is usually consulted before use with other steroid medicines.|Nasal history (recent surgery, ulcers, unhealed injury), glaucoma, cataracts and other steroid use are typically reviewed before use per OTC label; epistaxis or septal irritation commonly prompts provider notification; budesonide is the intranasal steroid most often favored in pregnancy; the name overlaps with nebulized (Pulmicort) and oral (Entocort, Uceris) budesonide, a known source of product mix-ups|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Budesonide Nasal Spray;;NCBI StatPearls: Budesonide
beclomethasone-nasal|This prescription steroid nose spray is used once a day (Qnasl) or twice a day (Beconase AQ), and relief builds over several days. Qnasl is a dry spray, so there is little drip. Nosebleeds, sores in the nose, white patches in the nose or throat, or vision changes are worth reporting.|Recent nasal surgery or trauma, glaucoma, cataracts and untreated infections such as tuberculosis are typically reviewed before starting; epistaxis, nasal ulceration or Candida infection commonly prompt provider notification; Qnasl (nasal) and QVAR (inhaled for asthma) are a look-alike pair; growth is reviewed in children on long-term therapy|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Beclomethasone Dipropionate Nasal Aerosol;;NCBI StatPearls: Beclomethasone
ciclesonide-nasal|This prescription steroid nose spray is used once a day and becomes active inside the nose. Relief builds over one to two weeks of regular use. Nosebleeds, sores in the nose, or vision changes are worth reporting.|Recent nasal surgery, ulcers or trauma, glaucoma and cataracts are typically reviewed before starting; epistaxis or nasal ulceration commonly prompts provider notification; Omnaris (2 sprays per nostril) and Zetonna (1 actuation per nostril) differ in dosing and device; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise systemic exposure|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ciclesonide Nasal Spray;;NCBI StatPearls: Allergic Rhinitis;;AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Allergic Rhinitis (2015)
ipratropium-nasal|This nose spray dries up a watery runny nose but does not help much with stuffiness or sneezing. Keeping the spray away from the eyes matters, because contact can blur vision or trigger eye pain. Nose dryness and small nosebleeds are common; eye pain, vision changes or trouble urinating are worth reporting right away.|Narrow-angle glaucoma and prostatic hyperplasia history are typically reviewed before starting; eye pain, halos or blurred vision after use suggest eye contamination and commonly prompt provider notification; 0.03% and 0.06% strengths have different labeled uses; systemic anticholinergic effects are uncommon because of low absorption|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Ipratropium Bromide Nasal Solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Ipratropium
sublingual-grass-pollen-extract|This grass pollen tablet is placed under the tongue to dissolve once a day, starting months before grass season. The first dose is given in the clinic with at least 30 minutes of watching. Mouth itching is common; trouble breathing, throat tightness, widespread hives or fainting are an emergency, and the prescribed epinephrine auto-injector is kept on hand, with 911 as a resource.|Asthma control, recent systemic allergic reactions and eosinophilic esophagitis history are typically reviewed before dosing; the first dose is given in a healthcare setting with at least 30 minutes of observation per label; beta-blocker use is flagged because it can blunt epinephrine response; throat swelling, wheeze, persistent swallowing problems or chest pain commonly prompt holding the tablet and notifying the provider; epinephrine auto-injector teaching is part of the label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Oralair (Grass Mixed Pollens Allergen Extract);;AAAAI/ACAAI Joint Task Force on Practice Parameters: Allergen Immunotherapy Practice Parameter
dust-mite-allergen-extract|This dust mite tablet is placed under the tongue once a day, all year. The first dose is given in the clinic with at least 30 minutes of watching, and later doses are taken at home. Mouth or throat itching is common; trouble breathing, throat tightness, hives or fainting are an emergency, and the prescribed epinephrine auto-injector is kept on hand, with 911 as a resource.|Asthma control, prior severe allergic reactions and eosinophilic esophagitis history are typically reviewed before dosing; the first dose is given under supervision with at least 30 minutes of observation per label; beta-blocker use is flagged because it can blunt epinephrine response; throat swelling, wheeze, dysphagia or chest pain commonly prompt holding the tablet and notifying the provider; the sublingual tablet is distinct from injectable standardized mite extracts used for allergy shots|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Odactra (House Dust Mite Allergen Extract);;AAAAI/ACAAI Joint Task Force on Practice Parameters: Allergen Immunotherapy Practice Parameter
olopatadine-nasal|This antihistamine nose spray is used twice a day and often helps within an hour. A bitter taste right after spraying is common. Drowsiness can happen, especially with alcohol or sleep medicines, and frequent nosebleeds are worth reporting.|Recent nasal surgery or ulcers are typically reviewed before starting; epistaxis or nasal ulceration commonly prompts provider notification; additive drowsiness with alcohol and CNS depressants is noted; the 0.6% nasal spray is a different product from olopatadine eye drops (Pataday, Patanol)|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Olopatadine Hydrochloride Nasal Spray;;NCBI StatPearls: Allergic Rhinitis;;AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Allergic Rhinitis (2015)
`);

