DB.addExt(`
phenylephrine-oral|This store-shelf decongestant pill is usually taken every 4 hours, with no more than 6 tablets in a day. It can cause nervousness, dizziness or trouble sleeping, and it is not taken with MAO inhibitor antidepressants. Many cold medicines also contain acetaminophen, so reading every label helps prevent a double dose.|Blood pressure history is typically reviewed, since alpha-1 agonism can raise pressure; MAO inhibitor or linezolid use is a contraindication per label; combination cold products are reviewed for duplicate acetaminophen; oral phenylephrine (Sudafed PE) and pseudoephedrine (Sudafed) are a known look-alike pair; an FDA advisory panel in 2023 found the oral 10 mg dose ineffective as a decongestant|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Phenylephrine Hydrochloride (oral, OTC Drug Facts);;NCBI StatPearls: Phenylephrine
epinephrine-auto-injector|This pen is for a severe allergic reaction and is pressed firmly into the outer thigh, through clothing if needed, and held in place. Symptoms can come back as the medicine wears off, so 911 is called after any use, and a second pen may be used if symptoms continue. Keeping two unexpired pens nearby at all times is part of the allergy plan.|Device strength is matched to weight per label (0.3 mg for 30 kg or more, 0.15 mg for 15 to 30 kg); the anterolateral thigh is the labeled site, and buttock, hand or foot injection is associated with complications; airway, blood pressure and heart rate are reassessed after dosing because biphasic reactions occur; beta-blocker use can blunt the response; accidental digital injection is a known device error|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Epinephrine Injection (EpiPen);;NCBI StatPearls: Anaphylaxis;;National EMS Scope of Practice Model (2019)
grass-pollen-allergen-extract|This tablet is placed under the tongue once a day and allowed to dissolve, starting at least 12 weeks before grass season. The first dose is taken in the clinic with a 30-minute watch period. Mouth itching is common, but throat swelling, trouble breathing or swallowing, or hives are emergencies that the prescribed epinephrine pen is meant for.|Asthma control is reviewed before starting, since severe or uncontrolled asthma is a contraindication per label; the first dose is observed for at least 30 minutes per label; an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed per boxed warning; beta-blocker use lowers the response to epinephrine; mouth wounds or oral inflammation commonly pause therapy per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Timothy Grass Pollen Allergen Extract (Grastek);;NCBI StatPearls: Allergen Immunotherapy
ragweed-allergen-extract|This tablet is placed under the tongue once a day and allowed to dissolve, starting at least 12 weeks before ragweed season. The first dose is taken in the clinic with a 30-minute watch period. Mouth itching is common, but throat swelling, trouble breathing or swallowing, or hives are emergencies that the prescribed epinephrine pen is meant for.|Asthma control is reviewed before starting, since severe or uncontrolled asthma is a contraindication per label; the first dose is observed for at least 30 minutes per label; an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed per boxed warning; beta-blocker use lowers the response to epinephrine; a history of eosinophilic esophagitis is a contraindication per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Short Ragweed Pollen Allergen Extract (Ragwitek);;NCBI StatPearls: Allergen Immunotherapy
peanut-allergen-powder|This peanut powder is mixed into a few spoonfuls of soft food like applesauce or yogurt and eaten once a day with a meal. Exercise and hot showers are skipped for a few hours after the dose, peanut foods are still avoided, and the epinephrine pen stays nearby. Vomiting, stomach pain that does not go away, trouble swallowing, or signs of a severe reaction are reported right away.|Initial dose escalation and the first dose of each up-dosing level are given in a certified healthcare setting with observation per label; uncontrolled asthma and eosinophilic GI disease are contraindications per label; an injectable epinephrine is prescribed and its use reviewed per boxed warning; missed doses, illness or exercise around dosing are reviewed because they raise reaction risk; available only through the Palforzia REMS program|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Peanut (Arachis hypogaea) Allergen Powder-dnfp (Palforzia);;NCBI StatPearls: Peanut Allergy
dexchlorpheniramine|This allergy liquid is measured with the dosing device that comes with it, not a kitchen spoon. It often causes sleepiness and dry mouth, so driving and alcohol are best avoided until its effect is known. It is not used in newborns or during breastfeeding, and very young children can become excited or have seizures from too much.|Sedation and fall risk are reviewed, especially in older adults (Beers Criteria anticholinergic); asthma or lower respiratory disease, nursing mothers and newborns are label contraindications; MAO inhibitor use is a contraindication per label; dosing is measured in mL against the 2 mg/5 mL concentration; additive CNS depression with opioids, alcohol and benzodiazepines is noted|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Dexchlorpheniramine Maleate Oral Solution;;NCBI StatPearls: Antihistamines;;2019 AGS Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society, updated 2023)
cetirizine-pseudoephedrine|This 12-hour pill is swallowed whole, one tablet every 12 hours and no more than 2 in a day. It can cause trouble sleeping, nervousness or a fast heartbeat, and it can raise blood pressure. It is not taken with MAO inhibitor antidepressants, and it is bought from behind the pharmacy counter with ID.|Blood pressure and heart rate are typically reviewed, since pseudoephedrine can raise both; MAO inhibitor use within 2 weeks is a contraindication per label; urinary retention in men with prostate enlargement is a known risk; extended-release tablets are not crushed or chewed; other decongestant-containing products are reviewed for duplication|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Cetirizine Hydrochloride and Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride (Zyrtec-D);;NCBI StatPearls: Pseudoephedrine
loratadine-pseudoephedrine|The 12-hour tablet is swallowed whole every 12 hours, while the 24-hour version is taken once a day, so checking which one is in the box matters. It can cause trouble sleeping, nervousness or a fast heartbeat and can raise blood pressure. It is not taken with MAO inhibitor antidepressants and is sold from behind the pharmacy counter.|Blood pressure and heart rate are typically reviewed, since pseudoephedrine can raise both; MAO inhibitor use within 2 weeks is a contraindication per label; the 12 Hour and 24 Hour products differ in strength and schedule; extended-release tablets are not crushed or chewed; urinary retention and insomnia are common reasons for intolerance|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Loratadine and Pseudoephedrine Sulfate (Claritin-D);;NCBI StatPearls: Pseudoephedrine
fexofenadine-pseudoephedrine|This tablet is swallowed whole with a glass of water on an empty stomach, every 12 hours for the 12-hour version. Fruit juices make the allergy part work less well, so water is the drink of choice. It can cause trouble sleeping, nervousness or a fast heartbeat and is not taken with MAO inhibitor antidepressants.|Blood pressure and heart rate are typically reviewed, since pseudoephedrine can raise both; MAO inhibitor use within 2 weeks is a contraindication per label; fruit juice and aluminum or magnesium antacids lower fexofenadine absorption; extended-release tablets are not crushed or chewed; not for children under 12 per label|FDA prescribing information (DailyMed): Fexofenadine Hydrochloride and Pseudoephedrine Hydrochloride (Allegra-D);;NCBI StatPearls: Fexofenadine
`);

