"cemiplimab":{rl:"IV 350 mg over 30 minutes every 3 weeks per label for advanced CSCC, up to 24 months; adjuvant CSCC can step up to 700 mg every 6 weeks; immune-mediated reactions and transplant rejection are key risks.",rh:"New cough or dyspnea, diarrhea, rising liver tests, hyperglycemia, hypotension, infusion reactions or signs of graft rejection typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Human IgG4 PD-1 inhibitor labeled for metastatic, locally advanced or high-risk adjuvant CSCC, basal cell carcinoma after hedgehog inhibitors, and PD-L1-selected or chemotherapy-combined NSCLC.",mp:"First systemic therapy approved for advanced CSCC, a UV-driven, high tumor mutational burden cancer; solid organ transplant recipients face rejection risk with PD-1 blockade."},
"dostarlimab":{rl:"IV 500 mg over 30 minutes every 3 weeks for 6 cycles with carboplatin and paclitaxel, then 1,000 mg every 6 weeks per label for endometrial cancer; immune-mediated reactions and chemotherapy cytopenias are key risks.",rh:"Fever during chemotherapy, new cough or dyspnea, diarrhea, rising liver tests, hyperglycemia, hypotension or infusion reactions typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Humanized IgG4 PD-1 inhibitor labeled for primary advanced or recurrent endometrial cancer with chemotherapy and for dMMR endometrial cancer and dMMR solid tumors.",mp:"dMMR/MSI-high tumors (Lynch syndrome) respond strongly to PD-1 blockade; a dMMR rectal cancer trial reported complete clinical responses in all enrolled patients (off-label)."},
"retifanlimab":{rl:"IV 500 mg over 30 minutes every 4 weeks per label for Merkel cell carcinoma; anal canal regimen with carboplatin and paclitaxel uses 500 mg every 4 weeks or 375 mg every 3 weeks; immune-mediated reactions are key risks.",rh:"New cough or dyspnea, diarrhea, rising liver tests, hyperglycemia, hypotension, fever during chemotherapy or infusion reactions typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Humanized IgG4 PD-1 inhibitor labeled for metastatic or recurrent locally advanced Merkel cell carcinoma and for inoperable recurrent or metastatic squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal.",mp:"Both labeled cancers are virus-associated (Merkel cell polyomavirus and HPV), making them immunogenic targets for PD-1 blockade."},
"toripalimab":{rl:"IV 240 mg every 3 weeks with cisplatin and gemcitabine per label, or 3 mg/kg every 2 weeks as a single agent; first infusion over 60 minutes; immune-mediated reactions and chemotherapy cytopenias are key risks.",rh:"Fever during chemotherapy, infusion reactions, new cough or dyspnea, diarrhea, rising liver tests, hyperglycemia or hypotension typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Humanized IgG4 PD-1 inhibitor labeled for metastatic or recurrent locally advanced nasopharyngeal carcinoma with cisplatin and gemcitabine, and as a single agent after platinum chemotherapy.",mp:"First FDA-approved drug for nasopharyngeal carcinoma, an EBV-associated cancer endemic to Southern China and Southeast Asia; hypothyroidism is common, especially after neck radiation."},
"tislelizumab":{rl:"IV 150 mg every 2 weeks, 200 mg every 3 weeks, 300 mg every 4 weeks or 400 mg every 6 weeks with chemotherapy per label; immune-mediated reactions and chemotherapy cytopenias are key risks.",rh:"Fever during chemotherapy, new cough or dyspnea, diarrhea, rising liver tests, hyperglycemia, hypotension or infusion reactions typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Fc-engineered humanized IgG4 PD-1 inhibitor labeled for ESCC (initial with chemotherapy when PD-L1 is 1 or more, or later as a single agent) and HER2-negative gastric or GEJ adenocarcinoma with PD-L1 1 or more.",mp:"Fc engineering minimizes Fc-gamma receptor binding so macrophages do not clear the PD-1-positive T cells the drug aims to reactivate."}
