"pimozide":{rl:"Oral D2 antagonist for refractory Tourette's tics, started at 1 to 2 mg daily in divided doses, with most patients below 0.2 mg/kg/day or 10 mg/day per label; QT prolongation is the key safety issue.",rh:"QTc above label limits, palpitations, syncope, low potassium or magnesium, a new macrolide or azole prescription, or fever with rigidity typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Diphenylbutylpiperidine dopamine D2 antagonist labeled for suppression of motor and phonic tics in Tourette's disorder after failure of standard treatment.",mp:"Pimozide blocks hERG and is CYP3A4 metabolized, so clarithromycin, azoles and protease inhibitors are contraindicated because of torsades and sudden death."},
"molindone":{rl:"Oral typical antipsychotic started at 50 to 75 mg/day and raised to 100 mg/day in 3 or 4 days per label, up to 225 mg/day in severe symptoms; boxed warning for dementia-related psychosis mortality.",rh:"Fever with rigidity and confusion, new involuntary movements, marked orthostatic hypotension or oversedation typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Dihydroindolone dopamine D2 antagonist labeled for schizophrenia; mid-potency typical with relatively low weight gain.",mp:"Molindone is one of the most weight-neutral antipsychotics, and its calcium sulfate excipient can lower phenytoin and tetracycline absorption."},
"clorazepate":{rl:"Oral benzodiazepine prodrug; anxiety dose is usually 30 mg daily in divided doses within 15 to 60 mg per label; boxed warning for opioid co-use, misuse and withdrawal.",rh:"Respiratory rate below baseline, marked sedation, co-administered opioids, confusion or falls in older adults typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Long-acting benzodiazepine labeled for anxiety, adjunctive therapy of partial seizures and acute alcohol withdrawal.",mp:"Clorazepate is decarboxylated in gastric acid to desmethyldiazepam (nordiazepam), the same long-acting metabolite as diazepam."},
"meprobamate":{rl:"Oral carbamate anxiolytic at 1200 mg to 1600 mg daily in three or four divided doses per label, with more than 2400 mg daily not recommended; dependence and withdrawal seizures are key risks.",rh:"Oversedation, slow breathing, ataxia, signs of misuse or withdrawal symptoms typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"C-IV propanediol carbamate anxiolytic labeled for anxiety disorders and short-term relief of anxiety symptoms.",mp:"Meprobamate is the active metabolite of carisoprodol, and overdose can form gastric bezoars with delayed or relapsing coma."},
"serdexmethylphenidate":{rl:"Oral C-II ADHD capsule (serdexmethylphenidate/dexmethylphenidate) started at 39.2 mg/7.8 mg once daily in the morning, maximum 52.3 mg/10.4 mg per label; boxed warning for abuse and misuse.",rh:"Chest pain, syncope, sustained tachycardia or hypertension, psychosis or mania, and poor growth in children typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Prodrug of dexmethylphenidate combined with immediate-release dexmethylphenidate, labeled for ADHD in patients 6 years and older.",mp:"Methylphenidate blocks DAT and NET reuptake, while amphetamines also reverse transporters to release catecholamines; serdexmethylphenidate is activated in the lower GI tract."},
"lofexidine":{rl:"Oral alpha-2A agonist given as three 0.18 mg tablets 4 times daily per label for up to 14 days, maximum 2.88 mg per day; hypotension, bradycardia and QT prolongation are key risks.",rh:"Symptomatic hypotension, orthostatic dizziness, bradycardia, syncope or excessive sedation typically prompt holding a dose and notifying the provider.",ml:"Central alpha-2 adrenergic agonist labeled to mitigate opioid withdrawal symptoms and facilitate abrupt opioid discontinuation in adults.",mp:"Lofexidine was the first FDA-approved non-opioid treatment for opioid withdrawal symptoms; like clonidine it reduces locus coeruleus noradrenergic hyperactivity."},
"naltrexone-extended-release-injection":{rl:"Monthly deep gluteal IM naltrexone 380 mg every 4 weeks per label for alcohol dependence and opioid relapse prevention; an opioid-free period of 7-10 days is recommended first.",rh:"Recent opioid use, a positive opioid screen, severe injection site pain or induration, jaundice or depressive symptoms typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Extended-release microsphere formulation of the mu-opioid antagonist naltrexone, labeled for alcohol dependence and prevention of relapse to opioid dependence after detoxification.",mp:"Naltrexone precipitates withdrawal in opioid-dependent patients, and overdose risk is high when the blockade wanes because tolerance is lost."},
"buprenorphine-extended-release-injection":{rl:"Monthly SC buprenorphine depot: 300 mg for the first two injections, then 100 mg monthly with at least 26 days between maintenance doses per label; REMS restricted because IV use can be fatal.",rh:"Respiratory depression, marked sedation, benzodiazepine or alcohol co-use, signs of hepatitis or a disturbed injection site typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Extended-release mu partial agonist depot labeled for moderate to severe opioid use disorder after initiation with transmucosal buprenorphine.",mp:"The Sublocade boxed warning describes serious harm or death with IV administration because the polymer forms a solid mass on contact with body fluids."},
"aripiprazole-long-acting":{rl:"Monthly IM aripiprazole 400 mg per label, given no sooner than 26 days after the previous injection, started with a two-injection or 14-day oral overlap regimen; boxed warning for dementia-related psychosis mortality.",rh:"Fever with rigidity, new involuntary movements, severe akathisia, hyperglycemia or compulsive gambling or spending typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Long-acting injectable D2 and 5-HT1A partial agonist labeled for schizophrenia and maintenance monotherapy of bipolar I disorder in adults.",mp:"Aripiprazole is a D2 partial agonist; the dose is reduced to 300 mg monthly in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers and akathisia is its signature adverse effect."}
